14,SDAIA,Saudi Data & AI Authority + NDMO,3. Cybersecurity & Data,PDPL-2021,Personal Data Protection Law,نظام حماية البيانات الشخصية,Sep 2021 + Apr 2023,2021,45,ISSUES/ENFORCES,Mandatory Enforcer,All organisations processing personal data — health data = sensitive,HIGHEST,https://sdaia.gov.sa/en/SDAIA/about/Pages/RegulationsAndPolicies.aspx,SDAIA official; Baker McKenzie 2025,Royal Decree M/19 (16 Sep 2021) → amended Apr 2023,"Original (2021): 19 articles covering consent, data subject rights, controller obligations, sensitive data (health = sensitive), cross-border transfer. Apr 2023 amendment: strengthened enforcement, clarified health data provisions, aligned with PDPL-IR-2023. Grace period for compliance ended Sep 14, 2024. Penalties: up to SAR 5 million fine + 2 years imprisonment for sensitive data breach","Issued: Sep 2021. Amended: Apr 2023. Fully enforceable: Sep 14, 2024",SDAIA reviews annually; amendments expected 2025-2026,CURRENT — fully enforceable since Sep 2024,Criminal offence: up to 2 years imprisonment + SAR 3M fine for disclosing sensitive data (health data = sensitive); administrative fine up to SAR 5M; mandatory breach notification to SDAIA within 72 hours; data subject compensation claims,SDAIA — Data Protection Authority (DPA functions); NDMO for government entities; courts for criminal penalties,Hospital; Clinic; Lab; Pharmacy; Health IT Vendor; Health Insurer; Government Health Entity; Medical Device Manufacturer (all processing patient data),Both + foreign entities processing data of KSA residents,SDAIA audit; controller self-assessment; DPO oversight; data subject complaints trigger investigation,Continuous (breach notification: 72 hours); annual DPO report; SDAIA random audits,12 (IoMT data minimisation; purpose limitation; consent management for wearable data; automated decision-making; data subject rights for IoMT-generated health data; cross-border transfer of IoMT data),GDPR (substantial alignment — adequate protection basis); APEC Privacy Framework; ISO 29100; ISO 27701,Arabic (official); English translation (unofficial),Layer 4: Compliance; Layer 2: Risk Management,No formal maturity model; SDAIA publishes compliance guidance,National Data Strategy; Vision 2030 Digital Economy; Digital Government transformation,Privacy notices; consent records; DPO appointment letter; data mapping/flow diagrams; breach notification records; DPIA for health data processing; controller registration,Privacy as fundamental right (Warren & Brandeis 1890; Westin 1967). Contextual integrity theory (Nissenbaum 2004): health data flows must match medical context norms. Regulatory theory: data protection authority independence and enforcement. Information asymmetry theory: patients cannot protect their own data without regulation.,"FOR: Long-overdue — KSA had no comprehensive data protection law until 2021; health data explicitly protected as sensitive category; aligns KSA with global adequacy standards. AGAINST: Alrawabdeh (2014) and Aldawood & Skinner (2019) documented Saudi privacy culture gap — regulation without public awareness is ineffective; 'GDPR-lite' criticism: exemptions broader than GDPR, enforcement weaker; health data provisions in IR still less specific than UK GDPR Schedule 1 conditions",No independent supervisory authority (SDAIA is both promoter and regulator — conflict of interest noted by Baker McKenzie 2025); patient data portability rights weaker than GDPR Article 20; no explicit right to explanation for AI-automated health decisions; cross-border transfer rules changed twice in 12 months (regulatory instability); small healthcare providers lack capacity for DPO requirements,"PDPL vs GDPR: PDPL penalty lower (SAR 5M vs €20M); PDPL broader government exemptions; GDPR has independent DPA, PDPL does not. PDPL vs HIPAA: PDPL = all personal data (broader); HIPAA = US-specific health data only. PDPL vs UK GDPR: PDPL misses explicit Schedule 1 health data conditions. PDPL ahead of HIPAA on consent granularity; behind GDPR on enforcement independence",Baker McKenzie (2025) Saudi Arabia PDPL Global Data & Cyber Handbook; Alrawabdeh W (2014) 'Internet and Social Media Challenges in Arab World' EJSR; Westin AF (1967) Privacy and Freedom NY Atheneum; Nissenbaum H (2004) 'Privacy as Contextual Integrity' Washington Law Review 79(1); CMS Law (2025) Saudi Arabia Data Protection & Cybersecurity Expert Guide Oct 2025,Official: SDAIA website (sdaia.gov.sa) — Arabic + English. Training: IAPP CIPM/CIPP/E (privacy certifications applicable); OneTrust PDPL compliance course; Baker McKenzie PDPL compliance guide. Language: Arabic primary — strong Arabic reading recommended. Estimated: 50 hours for health sector DPO competence,PRIMARY driver for GH-ICGA Layer 4 (Compliance) — PDPL is the legal foundation for patient data governance in the preliminary GH-ICGA framework. Thesis argument: GH-ICGA Layer 4 operationalises PDPL requirements into IoMT-specific controls (consent management for wearable data; purpose limitation for monitoring streams; breach response for connected device incidents),G + C (Governance + Compliance — strong compliance driver; risk implicit),Board/CEO: ultimate accountability for PDPL compliance. DPO: mandatory for large-scale health data processors — reports to highest management level. Privacy Committee: cross-functional oversight. All staff processing personal data: mandatory privacy awareness training annually,Privacy Risk (PRIMARY); Legal/Regulatory Risk; Reputational Risk; Strategic Risk (data monetisation constraints); Cross-Border Risk (data transfer restrictions),LEGAL — Royal Decree M/19 2021 + Implementing Regulations Sep 2023. Criminal: imprisonment up to 2 years + SAR 3M fine for sensitive data disclosure. Administrative: SAR 5M maximum fine. Civil: data subject compensation claims,Administrative (A): consent management; privacy notices; DPO appointment; DPIA process; breach notification procedure. Technical (T): data minimisation; pseudonymisation; access controls for personal data; encryption. Operational (O): data retention enforcement; data subject rights fulfilment process; cross-border transfer review,1. # data subject rights requests fulfilled on time (target: 100% within 30 days). 2. # data breaches notified to SDAIA within 72h (target: 100%). 3. % staff completed privacy training (target: 100%). 4. # DPIAs completed for high-risk processing activities. 5. Data retention compliance rate (% records within policy). 6. % third-party processors with DPA agreements,Annual DPIA review; data mapping audit (controller record validation); consent record audit; breach response simulation (tabletop); access control review for personal data systems; third-party processor audit; SDAIA compliance self-assessment,OneTrust (market leader for PDPL); TrustArc; Nymity; DataGrail; Varonis (data discovery); Microsoft Purview (data classification). SDAIA National Data Governance Platform for registration. Arabic-capable tools preferred,All third-party data processors must have Data Processing Agreement (DPA). Processors must demonstrate equivalent PDPL compliance. Cross-border transfers require adequacy assessment or appropriate safeguards. Sub-processor disclosure mandatory. Annual processor risk assessment required,Non-compliance: SAR 5M administrative fine; criminal prosecution for sensitive data breach; SDAIA investigation; mandatory breach public disclosure. Business disruption: SDAIA can order processing suspension. Reputational: patient trust damage. Market: EU adequate protection status depends on PDPL enforcement credibility,ALL 17 SECTORS — any entity processing personal data,YES — ALL SECTORS,,,,,
15,SDAIA,Saudi Data & AI Authority + NDMO,3. Cybersecurity & Data,PDPL-IR-2023,PDPL Implementing Regulations,اللائحة التنفيذية لنظام حماية البيانات,7 Sep 2023,2023,89,ISSUES/ENFORCES,Mandatory Enforcer,All PDPL-scope entities — fully enforced Sep 2024,HIGHEST,https://sdaia.gov.sa/en/SDAIA/about/Pages/RegulationsAndPolicies.aspx,SDAIA official; DLA Piper 2024,Issued 7 Sep 2023 (22/2/1445H); multiple ministerial updates to Jun 2025,"89 detailed implementing articles. Key provisions: health data requires explicit consent + necessity justification; DPO mandatory for large-scale health data processors; 72-hour breach notification to SDAIA; cross-border transfer assessment; individual rights (access, correction, deletion, objection); registration obligation for certain controllers. Updates to Jun 2025 clarified breach notification and international transfer rules","Issued: Sep 7, 2023. In force with PDPL: Sep 14, 2024",SDAIA issues ministerial decisions periodically; next major update expected 2025,CURRENT — with amendments to Jun 2025,Same as PDPL-2021 — implementing regulation adds specificity to penalties; breach notification failure = separate penalty up to SAR 5M,SDAIA (primary); NDMO (government entities); sector regulators may co-enforce (MOH for health data),All processing health data — Hospital; Clinic; Lab; Pharmacy; Health IT Vendor; Health Insurer,Both,SDAIA compliance audit; DPO internal audit; third-party DPIA,Annual DPO report; SDAIA audit (risk-based); breach-triggered,14 (health data provisions: consent specificity for IoMT wearables; purpose limitation for connected device data; data retention limits for IoMT streams; cross-border IoMT data transfer controls),GDPR Articles 5-9 (closely mirrored); ISO 27701:2019,Arabic (official); English translation available from SDAIA,Layer 4: Compliance,No formal maturity model,National Data Strategy; Privacy framework for digital health,DPIA reports; consent records with timestamps; health data processing register; DPO appointment; breach notification log,Information privacy theory (Westin 1967): privacy as informational self-determination. Contextual integrity (Nissenbaum 2010): appropriate information flows depend on context — healthcare data flows have stricter norms than commercial. Institutional theory: PDPL Implementing Regulations represent coercive isomorphism from GDPR model adapted to KSA legal tradition (Sharia-compatible data subject rights).,"FOR: PDPL-IR provides operational specificity missing from PDPL-2021 (DPO appointment, DPIA methodology, breach notification 72hr rule paralleling GDPR); Arabic-first drafting ensures local legal enforceability; health data classified as 'sensitive' with explicit consent requirements. AGAINST: 72hr breach notification is considered too tight for complex healthcare breaches (EU experience shows average discovery-to-report of 5-7 days); DPIA methodology lacks healthcare-specific guidance for patient data processing in research contexts.",Criticisms: (1) No explicit exemption for health research — creates friction between KACST research mandates and PDPL consent requirements; (2) DPO qualification requirements undefined — no certification or experience standard specified; (3) Cross-border data transfer assessment (PDPL-DTR) referenced but was not yet published when IR took effect — temporal gap; (4) No sector-specific guidance for healthcare (unlike EU GDPR Article 9 health data provisions which have years of DPA guidance); (5) Penalty schedule for IR violations not yet published by SDAIA.,"Framework comparison requires contextual analysis. Key comparators: NIST CSF 2.0 (international benchmark), ISO 27001 (certifiable standard), ECC-2 (KSA mandatory baseline). See main database for cross-framework mapping.",Westin (1967) Privacy and Freedom; Nissenbaum (2010) Privacy in Context; SDAIA Official Implementing Regulations (2023); Bird & Bird (2024) KSA PDPL Analysis; Al-Jasser & Smith (2024) Data Protection in GCC States; IAPP (2024) KSA PDPL Implementation Guide.,"Official: Consult the official source URL in the database for the primary document. Training: ISACA, ISC2, RAPS, IAPP, and HIMSS offer professional development covering most regulatory domains. Estimated hours vary by framework complexity.","GH-ICGA positions PDPL-IR as the OPERATIONAL PRIVACY LAYER — converts PDPL-2021 principles into auditable controls. Critical for healthcare IoT: every connected medical device generating patient data triggers PDPL-IR requirements (consent, purpose limitation, retention). Thesis argues PDPL-IR + PDPL-DTR together create a 'privacy compliance sandwich' that healthcare entities must navigate alongside NCA cybersecurity controls.",C (primary Compliance instrument — implementing detail for PDPL governance),DPO: must be notified to SDAIA; independence from operational decision-making; access to senior management. Health data: additional justification beyond consent required (medical necessity; public health). Controller registration: mandatory for specified categories (large-scale health data processors),Privacy Risk; Legal Risk; Operational Risk (compliance machinery); Cross-Border Data Transfer Risk,LEGAL — Implementing regulation of PDPL. Same sanctions as PDPL-2021. Ministerial decisions update periodically (latest Jun 2025),Administrative: health data processing justification documentation; DPO notification to SDAIA; controller registration. Technical: health data access logs. Operational: individual rights fulfilment workflow; breach notification process,1. % health data processing activities with documented legal basis. 2. DPO notification status (registered with SDAIA: Y/N). 3. Controller registration status (if applicable). 4. # DPIA register entries for health IoMT data. 5. Average days to fulfil data subject rights request,SDAIA compliance audit; DPO internal audit; legal basis review; controller registration verification; health data processing register audit,OneTrust; TrustArc; Microsoft Purview — same tools as PDPL-2021. SDAIA data governance platform for controller registration,Same as PDPL-2021 + health data processor must demonstrate specific health data controls,Failure to register: SAR 5M fine. Failure to appoint DPO: enforcement action. Failure to notify breach: separate penalty. Compounding: PDPL-2021 + IR-2023 violations can attract cumulative penalties,ALL 17 SECTORS — any entity processing personal data,YES — ALL SECTORS,,,,,
16,SDAIA,Saudi Data & AI Authority + NDMO,3. Cybersecurity & Data,PDPL-DTR-2024,Data Transfer Regulation (revised),لائحة نقل البيانات الشخصية,Sep 2024,2023,12,ISSUES/ENFORCES,Mandatory Enforcer,All organisations transferring personal data outside KSA — cloud health data,HIGHEST,https://sdaia.gov.sa/en/SDAIA/about/Pages/RegulationsAndPolicies.aspx,SDAIA Sep 2024; Baker McKenzie 2025,"DTR v1: Sep 7, 2023 → DTR v2 (replacement): Sep 1, 2024","v1 (Sep 2023): Initial data transfer regulation allowing transfers via adequacy, contractual clauses, consent, or necessity. v2 (Sep 1, 2024): Complete replacement. Significant change: removed automatic adequacy for certain jurisdictions; introduced mandatory risk assessment before any cross-border health data transfer; controller accountability strengthened; specific provisions for cloud-hosted patient data transferred to overseas servers","DTR v1: Sep 2023. DTR v2 (replacement): Sep 1, 2024",Annual review by SDAIA; adequacy list publication expected 2025,CURRENT — DTR v2 (Sep 2024),Penalties defined by issuing authority; consult official source. NCA ECC-2 penalties apply if relevant,Issuing authority and/or designated inspection department,As specified in framework scope — consult official document,Both (unless specified otherwise in official document),As defined by issuing authority,As defined by issuing authority,To be determined from framework text — see official source,See framework preamble for international standard references,Arabic (primary in KSA); English may be available,Mapped to relevant GH-ICGA layer — requires manual assessment,See official framework for maturity model details,Aligned with relevant Vision 2030 programme — see framework context,As specified in framework compliance requirements,Data sovereignty theory: state control over citizen data flows as expression of digital sovereignty (Couture & Toupin 2019). Adequacy determination theory (EU model): cross-border transfers permitted only to jurisdictions with 'adequate' protection. Islamic jurisprudence: data as trust (amana) — cross-border transfer requires preservation of trust obligations.,FOR: PDPL-DTR v2 (Sep 2024) introduces risk-based cross-border assessment — proportionate approach avoiding EU GDPR's blanket prohibition model; explicitly addresses cloud hosting (critical for KSA's cloud-first healthcare strategy); allows transfers for medical emergencies without full assessment. AGAINST: Adequacy list not yet published — creates legal uncertainty for international health data sharing; assessment methodology requires entity-level evaluation (expensive for SME healthcare providers); no mutual recognition framework with GCC states despite similar PDPL models.,Criticisms: (1) No published adequacy list as of 2025 — every cross-border transfer requires individual assessment; (2) Assessment methodology is resource-intensive — estimated 40-80 FTE hours per transfer relationship; (3) Telemedicine providers face particular burden — each international specialist consultation may constitute a 'transfer'; (4) Health research collaborations with international universities (KAUST partnerships) require complex transfer impact assessments; (5) Cloud data residency requirements interact with NCA CCC cloud controls — dual compliance pathway unclear.,"Framework comparison requires contextual analysis. Key comparators: NIST CSF 2.0 (international benchmark), ISO 27001 (certifiable standard), ECC-2 (KSA mandatory baseline). See main database for cross-framework mapping.",Couture & Toupin (2019) Digital Sovereignty; SDAIA PDPL Data Transfer Regulations v2 (Sep 2024); Greenleaf (2023) Global Data Privacy Laws; Al-Ahmad (2024) Cross-Border Data Flows in the GCC; IAPP (2025) KSA DTR Implementation Analysis.,"Official: Consult the official source URL in the database for the primary document. Training: ISACA, ISC2, RAPS, IAPP, and HIMSS offer professional development covering most regulatory domains. Estimated hours vary by framework complexity.",GH-ICGA positions PDPL-DTR as the SOVEREIGNTY BOUNDARY CONTROL — governs where KSA health data can flow internationally. Critical intersection with NCA CCC (cloud) and NHIC NABIDH (health exchange). Thesis argues DTR creates a 'data sovereignty moat' that protects KSA health data while potentially fragmenting international health research collaboration.,G + C (Governance + Compliance — standard regulatory framework positioning),Senior management: accountability for framework compliance. Designated compliance officer: monitor requirements. All staff: awareness of applicable obligations,Regulatory Risk; Operational Risk; Reputational Risk — specific domains determined by framework scope,"See official source — legal obligation, regulatory requirement, or voluntary standard as applicable",Administrative; Technical; Operational — specific distribution determined by framework content,1. Compliance status (compliant/non-compliant). 2. # open findings from last assessment. 3. % controls implemented. 4. Time to remediate findings. 5. Incident rate in framework scope area,Self-assessment; internal audit; external assessment as specified by issuing authority,ServiceNow GRC; IBM OpenPages; RSA Archer; MetricStream — all major GRC platforms support custom framework import. Contact vendor for framework-specific module,Third parties in scope must demonstrate equivalent compliance. Contractual compliance obligations; right-to-audit clauses; annual third-party assessment,Non-compliance: regulatory action by issuing authority; fines and penalties as specified; operational and reputational consequences,Sector-specific — consult official source,Sector-Specific,,,,,
