24,SDAIA,Saudi Data & AI Authority + NDMO,3. Cybersecurity & Data,NDMO-RULEBOOK,PDPL Controllers Registration & Rules,قواعد تسجيل المتحكمين,2023 updated,2023,N/A,ISSUES/ENFORCES,Mandatory Enforcer,All controllers processing personal data — health data controllers,HIGHEST,https://sdaia.gov.sa/,CMS Law Oct 2025,Issued 2023; updates to Jun 2025,"Mandatory registration for data controllers meeting thresholds (e.g., large-scale health data processors). Defines controller obligations post-registration: annual compliance report, DPO appointment notification, breach reporting procedures. NDMO National Data Governance Platform for registration (Saudi entities only as of 2025)",Issued: 2023. Updates: Jun 2025,Ongoing,CURRENT,Penalties defined by issuing authority; consult official source. NCA ECC-2 penalties apply if relevant,Issuing authority and/or designated inspection department,As specified in framework scope — consult official document,Both (unless specified otherwise in official document),As defined by issuing authority,As defined by issuing authority,To be determined from framework text — see official source,See framework preamble for international standard references,Arabic (primary in KSA); English may be available,Mapped to relevant GH-ICGA layer — requires manual assessment,See official framework for maturity model details,Aligned with relevant Vision 2030 programme — see framework context,As specified in framework compliance requirements,"Open data theory (Janssen et al. 2012): government data as public good for innovation. Data governance theory: NDMO Rulebook establishes data stewardship model — each entity appoints data owner, custodian, steward. Digital transformation theory: data as strategic asset for Vision 2030 economic diversification.","FOR: NDMO Rulebook is the first comprehensive national data governance framework in the Middle East; mandates data classification, quality management, and sharing agreements across all government entities; explicitly includes health data governance standards. AGAINST: Rulebook scope limited to government entities — private healthcare providers operate under PDPL but not NDMO data governance; implementation maturity varies significantly across ministries.",Criticisms: (1) Overlap with PDPL data classification requirements creates dual compliance burden; (2) Data sharing agreements between MOH and private providers not standardised; (3) No enforcement mechanism within NDMO itself — relies on entity-level compliance; (4) Arabic-only official text limits international alignment assessment.,"Framework comparison requires contextual analysis. Key comparators: NIST CSF 2.0 (international benchmark), ISO 27001 (certifiable standard), ECC-2 (KSA mandatory baseline). See main database for cross-framework mapping.",Janssen et al. (2012) Open Government Data; NDMO Official Rulebook (2022); Al-Badi (2023) National Data Management in KSA; OECD (2024) Digital Government Review of KSA.,"Official: Consult the official source URL in the database for the primary document. Training: ISACA, ISC2, RAPS, IAPP, and HIMSS offer professional development covering most regulatory domains. Estimated hours vary by framework complexity.","GH-ICGA positions NDMO-RULEBOOK as the DATA STEWARDSHIP layer — governs how health data is classified, managed, and shared across government entities. Thesis argues NDMO Rulebook + PDPL together create a 'dual data governance' architecture unique to KSA.",G + C (Governance + Compliance — standard regulatory framework positioning),Senior management: accountability for framework compliance. Designated compliance officer: monitor requirements. All staff: awareness of applicable obligations,Regulatory Risk; Operational Risk; Reputational Risk — specific domains determined by framework scope,"See official source — legal obligation, regulatory requirement, or voluntary standard as applicable",Administrative; Technical; Operational — specific distribution determined by framework content,1. Compliance status (compliant/non-compliant). 2. # open findings from last assessment. 3. % controls implemented. 4. Time to remediate findings. 5. Incident rate in framework scope area,Self-assessment; internal audit; external assessment as specified by issuing authority,ServiceNow GRC; IBM OpenPages; RSA Archer; MetricStream — all major GRC platforms support custom framework import. Contact vendor for framework-specific module,Third parties in scope must demonstrate equivalent compliance. Contractual compliance obligations; right-to-audit clauses; annual third-party assessment,Non-compliance: regulatory action by issuing authority; fines and penalties as specified; operational and reputational consequences,Sector-specific — consult official source,Sector-Specific,,,,,
