26,MOH,Ministry of Health,2. Healthcare & Life Sciences,MOH-HL,Health Law,نظام الصحة,RD M/11 2002 updated,1949,N/A,ISSUES/ENFORCES,Mandatory Enforcer,All healthcare facilities and providers,HIGHEST,https://www.moh.gov.sa/en/Ministry/Rules/Pages/default.aspx,MOH official; Hammad & Al-Mehdar 2025,Royal Decree M/11 1949 (foundational) → multiple Royal Decrees updating through 2024,Foundational health law establishing MOH mandate. Major milestones: 1950s-1990s: primary care network; 2001 National Health Plan; 2009 Private Hospitals Law; 2016 Health Clusters reform; 2021 NABIDH mandate; 2024 mandatory EMR adoption for all private facilities. Constitutes the overarching legal basis for all MOH regulations,Original: 1949. Continuously updated by Royal Decrees,Ongoing — amendments via Royal Decree,CURRENT — foundational law with continuous amendments,Penalties defined by issuing authority; consult official source. NCA ECC-2 penalties apply if relevant,Issuing authority and/or designated inspection department,As specified in framework scope — consult official document,Both (unless specified otherwise in official document),As defined by issuing authority,As defined by issuing authority,To be determined from framework text — see official source,See framework preamble for international standard references,Arabic (primary in KSA); English may be available,Mapped to relevant GH-ICGA layer — requires manual assessment,See official framework for maturity model details,Aligned with relevant Vision 2030 programme — see framework context,As specified in framework compliance requirements,Health law theory: healthcare licensing as state power to protect public safety. Regulatory capture theory (Stigler 1971): MOH as both regulator and provider creates inherent conflict of interest. Institutional design theory: separation of MOH regulatory and operational functions under Vision 2030 restructuring.,"FOR: MOH Health Law (2022 update) consolidates scattered healthcare regulations into unified framework; explicitly addresses telemedicine, digital health, AI diagnostics for the first time; mandatory licensing for all digital health platforms. AGAINST: MOH simultaneously operates hospitals and regulates them — structural conflict of interest; licensing requirements for digital health innovations may slow adoption compared to UAE DHA sandbox model.",Criticisms: (1) MOH as regulator AND provider creates conflicts acknowledged by Vision 2030 restructuring plan but not yet resolved; (2) Digital health licensing framework lags behind innovation — SFDA AI-MDS published before MOH digital health licensing criteria; (3) Telemedicine regulations drafted pre-COVID lack post-pandemic cross-border provisions; (4) No explicit patient data rights within Health Law (relies on PDPL for data protection).,"Framework comparison requires contextual analysis. Key comparators: NIST CSF 2.0 (international benchmark), ISO 27001 (certifiable standard), ECC-2 (KSA mandatory baseline). See main database for cross-framework mapping.",Stigler (1971) Economic Regulation; MOH Health Law (Royal Decree M/22); Al-Yousuf et al. (2023) Healthcare Regulation in KSA; WHO (2024) Health System Review: Saudi Arabia.,"Official: Consult the official source URL in the database for the primary document. Training: ISACA, ISC2, RAPS, IAPP, and HIMSS offer professional development covering most regulatory domains. Estimated hours vary by framework complexity.",GH-ICGA positions MOH-HL as the HEALTHCARE GOVERNANCE BASELINE — the legal foundation upon which all health-sector-specific regulations build. Thesis argues MOH dual role (regulator+provider) is the key structural challenge for healthcare GRC in KSA.,G + C (Governance + Compliance — standard regulatory framework positioning),Senior management: accountability for framework compliance. Designated compliance officer: monitor requirements. All staff: awareness of applicable obligations,Regulatory Risk; Operational Risk; Reputational Risk — specific 