27,MOH,Ministry of Health,2. Healthcare & Life Sciences,MOH-HIS,Health Information System Standards,معايير نظم المعلومات الصحية,2024 update,2010,112,ISSUES/ENFORCES,Mandatory Enforcer,All MOH-affiliated hospitals and clinics,HIGHEST,https://www.moh.gov.sa/en/,MOH; NHIC NABIDH; WHO/WEF PHSSR 2025,First issued ~2010 → comprehensive update 2022 → mandatory EMR update 2024,"Progressive standards covering: 2010 (basic EHR requirements); 2015 (interoperability standards added); 2018 (NABIDH integration); 2020 (mobile health and telemedicine modules); 2022 (comprehensive 112-control update covering clinical systems, imaging, medication management, emergency, genomics, training); 2024 (mandatory EMR adoption for all MOH and private facilities aligned with NABIDH)",First: 2010. Major update: 2022. EMR mandate: 2024,Updated with NABIDH platform releases; typically annual minor updates,CURRENT — 2024 version mandatory,MOH facility licensing at risk; mandatory corrective action plan; financial penalties under Private Health Institutions Law; non-compliant systems barred from NABIDH connectivity,MOH — Directorate of Health Information (DHI); regional health cluster inspection teams; NHIC for interoperability compliance,Hospital; Clinic; Lab; Pharmacy (hospital); Government Health Entity,Both (mandatory for MOH facilities; mandatory for private facilities since 2024 PHILAW update),MOH annual IT audit; NHIC interoperability assessment; CBAHI HIT standards review during accreditation,Annual MOH audit; NABIDH connectivity continuous monitoring,28 (medical device integration into HIS; alarm management; device data capture; IoMT workflow integration; remote monitoring data ingestion; device incident reporting to HIS),HL7 FHIR R4 (interoperability); IHE profiles (integration); ISO 27799:2016 (health ISMS); DICOM (imaging),Arabic (primary); English technical annexes,Layer 1: Governance; Layer 3: Technical Controls; Layer 4: Compliance,Yes — 4 implementation levels (Basic / Intermediate / Advanced / Optimised); MOH tracks facility scores,HSTP digital health; NABIDH national EHR; Vision 2030 healthcare quality targets,HIS system documentation; NABIDH connectivity certificate; interoperability test results; staff training records; system access audit logs; downtime contingency procedure,Health informatics systems theory (Shortliffe & Cimino 2006): HIS as sociotechnical infrastructure. Interoperability theory (Kuperman 2011): semantic/syntactic/organisational interoperability levels. Technology adoption theory (TAM — Davis 1989): clinician adoption barriers to HIS.,FOR: National standardisation essential for patient safety continuity (WHO 2019); NABIDH integration creates longitudinal patient record unavailable previously; IoMT data integration requirements push hospitals toward modern system architectures. AGAINST: Ajami & Bagheri-Tadi (2013) and Aloudah et al. (2021) document implementation-reality gap in Saudi HIS — standards exist but compliance varies widely by facility tier; urban-rural HIS capacity gap poorly addressed; cybersecurity requirements within HIS standards historically weak (pre-NABIDH),112 controls create compliance burden without proportionality guidance; small private clinics face disproportionate compliance cost; no formal HIS certification programme — self-declaration accepted; IoMT data standards not harmonised with international HL7 FHIR profiles; historical emphasis on data capture over data quality,MOH-HIS vs NHS Digital standards (UK): NHS has legally binding DCB standards with enforcement; MOH-HIS is ministerial directive with softer enforcement. MOH-HIS vs ONC Health IT Certification (US): US has federal certification; KSA moving toward certification (NABIDH conformance). MOH-HIS + NHIC-NABIDH = closest to ONC approach in KSA,Ajami S & Bagheri-Tadi T (2013) 'Barriers for Adopting Electronic Health Records' MateriaSociomed 25(3); Aloudah N et al. (2021) 'Digital Health in Saudi Arabia' Sustainability 13(12); Shortliffe EH & Cimino JJ (2006) Biomedical Informatics Springer; WHO (2019) Classification of Digital Interventions; Alotaibi YK & Federico F (2017) 'Impact of Health IT on Patient Safety' Saudi Medical J 38(12),Official: MOH website (moh.gov.sa). Training: CPHIMS (Certified Professional Health Informatics & Information Management); HL7 FHIR Foundational (HL7.org); NHIC developer portal training (nhic.gov.sa). Language: Arabic primary. Estimated: 45 hours,GH-ICGA Layer 1 (Governance) + Layer 3 (Technical Controls) — MOH-HIS is the clinical data governance backbone that the preliminary GH-ICGA IoMT framework must integrate with. Thesis: GH-ICGA IoMT data model must align with HIS data standards to ensure IoMT device data flows into the national health record coherently,G + R + C (all three — clinical information governance foundation),Hospital CEO: accountable for HIS compliance. CIO/CMIO: responsible for HIS implementation and NABIDH connectivity. Clinical Informatics Committee: govern HIS standards adoption. Department Heads: responsible for clinical data quality in their systems,Operational Risk (HIS downtime = clinical risk); Patient Safety Risk; Data Quality Risk; Regulatory Risk (MOH non-compliance); Interoperability Risk (NABIDH disconnection),REGULATORY — MOH ministerial directive. Mandatory for all licensed facilities. Non-compliance risks licensing. Enforced through MOH annual audit and NABIDH connectivity monitoring,Technical (T): 68 controls — system architecture; integration; security; availability; backup. Administrative (A): 28 controls — data governance; policies; training; documentation. Operational (O): 16 controls — downtime procedures; change management; vendor management,1. NABIDH connectivity status (connected/disconnected). 2. HIS downtime incidents per month (target <4h/month). 3. Data quality score (completeness/accuracy) from NABIDH reports. 4. % clinical staff trained on HIS (target 100%). 5. Time-to-restore HIS after failure (target <4h). 6. # HIS security incidents per quarter,Annual MOH HIS audit (documentation + system walk-through); NABIDH API conformance test (NHIC automated); data quality audit; HIS security assessment; downtime procedure simulation; user access review,Epic; Cerner (Oracle Health); Philips Tasy EMR; Meditech; InterSystems HealthShare — all NABIDH-integrated. For compliance: ServiceNow Healthcare; Qualio (quality management); NHIC developer portal for FHIR conformance testing,HIS vendors: must be MOH-approved/NABIDH-certified; contractual data protection obligations; source code escrow for critical systems; SLA for 99.9% uptime; mandatory SFDA notification if HIS failure affects medical devices; annual security assessment of HIS vendors,"Non-compliance: MOH corrective action; facility licence conditions; NABIDH disconnection (loss of referrals, national patient record access); PHILAW 2024 penalties for private facilities. Clinical impact: patient safety risk from poor data quality or HIS unavailability",Healthcare & Life Sciences,Sector-Specific,,,,,
28,MOH,Ministry of Health,2. Healthcare & Life Sciences,MOH-EMR-NABIDH,EMR Standards / NABIDH Platform,معايير السجل الصحي الإلكتروني / منصة نبض,Mandatory 2024,2010,52,ISSUES/ENFORCES,Mandatory Enforcer,All healthcare facilities — mandatory NABIDH 2024,HIGHEST,https://www.moh.gov.sa/en/,MOH; Hammad & Al-Mehdar 2025,NABIDH platform launched ~2018 → mandatory EMR adoption for all facilities 2024,"NABIDH (National Unified EHR Platform): v1 2018 (pilot MOH hospitals); 2019-2021 (rollout to health clusters); 2022 (private hospital voluntary integration); 2023 (API standards published based on FHIR R4); 2024 (mandatory for ALL healthcare facilities per Private Health Institutions Law update — complete patient longitudinal record across facilities). EMR standard covers 52 functional requirements including: CPOE, medication reconciliation, imaging integration, patient portal, audit logs",NABIDH launched: 2018. Mandatory for all: 2024,Continuously updated with NABIDH platform releases,CURRENT — mandatory since 2024,Facility licensing revocation; MOH enforcement action; barred from government referrals; penalties under PHILAW 2024,MOH — NABIDH Programme Office; NHIC; regional health clusters monitor connectivity,Hospital; Clinic; Lab; Pharmacy; Government Health Entity — ALL healthcare facilities mandatory,Both (mandatory since 2024 PHILAW update for all facilities),NHIC connectivity assessment; NABIDH API conformance testing; MOH annual audit,Continuous (NABIDH connectivity monitored in real time); annual formal audit,22 (IoMT data integration to NABIDH: wearable data ingestion API; remote monitoring feeds; device-generated vital signs; medical device alerts to patient record; IoMT consent management in EHR),HL7 FHIR R4; IHE (Integrating the Healthcare Enterprise); SNOMED CT; ICD-10; LOINC,Arabic + English (NABIDH technical specifications bilingual),Layer 1: Governance; Layer 3: Technical Controls; Layer 4: Compliance; Layer 5: Assurance,Yes — NABIDH connectivity levels (1: Basic data exchange; 2: Full longitudinal record; 3: Advanced interoperability with IoMT); MOH tracks facility level,HSTP digital health transformation; NABIDH national EHR; NHIS claims integration,NABIDH connectivity certificate; API integration test reports; patient consent records for data sharing; EHR system validation; data quality reports,Health information exchange theory (Walker et al. 2005): interoperability as prerequisite for care coordination. FHIR interoperability standards: MOH EMR-NABIDH mandate represents state-driven interoperability (top-down) vs market-driven (bottom-up US approach). Data portability theory: patient access to health records across providers.,"FOR: MOH mandating NABIDH integration for all EMR systems ensures national interoperability — avoids US fragmentation problem; HL7 FHIR adoption positions KSA as regional interoperability leader; enables population health analytics for epidemic preparedness. AGAINST: Mandatory EMR-NABIDH integration imposes significant cost on small clinics; NABIDH data quality depends on source system quality — 'garbage in, garbage out' risk; privacy advocates argue national health record creates surveillance potential.",Criticisms: (1) Cost burden of NABIDH integration disproportionately affects small/medium healthcare providers; (2) NABIDH integration standards not fully aligned with NHIC NABIDH standards — creates confusion; (3) Patient consent model for NABIDH data sharing unclear under PDPL framework; (4) Integration timeline repeatedly extended — original 2022 deadline not met.,"Framework comparison requires contextual analysis. Key comparators: NIST CSF 2.0 (international benchmark), ISO 27001 (certifiable standard), ECC-2 (KSA mandatory baseline). See main database for cross-framework mapping.",Walker et al. (2005) Health Information Exchange; HL7 FHIR (2024) Interoperability Standards; Al-Mufti (2023) EMR Implementation in KSA; NHIC NABIDH Technical Specifications (2024).,"Official: Consult the official source URL in the database for the primary document. Training: ISACA, ISC2, RAPS, IAPP, and HIMSS offer professional development covering most regulatory domains. Estimated hours vary by framework complexity.",Relevance to GH-ICGA framework varies by layer — see GH-ICGA Layer Mapping column. All KSA mandatory frameworks are compliance inputs to GH-ICGA Layer 4. Frameworks with IoMT-specific provisions directly inform Layers 2 and 3.,G + C (Governance + Compliance — standard regulatory framework positioning),Senior management: accountability for framework compliance. Designated compliance officer: monitor requirements. All staff: awareness of applicable obligations,Regulatory Risk; Operational Risk; Reputational Risk — specific domains determined by framework scope,"See official source — legal obligation, regulatory requirement, or voluntary standard as applicable",Administrative; Technical; Operational — specific distribution determined by framework content,1. Compliance status (compliant/non-compliant). 2. # open findings from last assessment. 3. % controls implemented. 4. Time to remediate findings. 5. Incident rate in framework scope area,Self-assessment; internal audit; external assessment as specified by issuing authority,ServiceNow GRC; IBM OpenPages; RSA Archer; MetricStream — all major GRC platforms support custom framework import. Contact vendor for framework-specific module,Third parties in scope must demonstrate equivalent compliance. Contractual compliance obligations; right-to-audit clauses; annual third-party assessment,Non-compliance: regulatory action by issuing authority; fines and penalties as specified; operational and reputational consequences,Healthcare & Life Sciences,Sector-Specific,,,,,
29,MOH,Ministry of Health,2. Healthcare & Life Sciences,MOH-PS,Patient Safety Standards,معايير سلامة المريض,2024 update,2015,72,ISSUES/ENFORCES,Mandatory Enforcer,All healthcare facilities,HIGHEST,https://www.moh.gov.sa/en/,MOH official,First issued ~2015 → updates 2020 → 2024 update,"Based on WHO and JCI patient safety frameworks. Covers: patient identification (minimum 2 identifiers), medication safety (high-alert medications protocol), surgical safety checklist, falls prevention, infection control, critical value reporting, handover communications (SBAR). 2020 update added COVID-19 IPC protocols. 2024 update adds IoMT device safety (alarm fatigue management, device connectivity requirements)","First: ~2015. Updated: 2020, 2024",Annual review with MOH patient safety incidents data,CURRENT — 2024 version,MOH corrective action order; facility licence conditions; public safety alert; criminal liability for gross negligence,MOH — Patient Safety Centre; regional health cluster inspection; CBAHI (patient safety standards embedded in accreditation),Hospital; Clinic; Government Health Entity,Both,Annual MOH patient safety inspection; CBAHI survey (integrated); incident investigation,Annual; triggered by serious adverse event,14 (alarm fatigue management for IoMT devices; connected infusion pump safety; IV smart pump drug library governance; patient ID for IoMT devices; device failure response procedures; remote monitoring alert management),WHO Patient Safety Challenges (Global Patient Safety Action Plan 2021-2030); JCI Patient Safety Goals; The Joint Commission NPSG,Arabic (primary); English reference,Layer 1: Governance; Layer 4: Compliance,Yes — patient safety maturity levels (Safety-I to Safety-III); MOH tracks facility scores,HSTP patient safety; Vision 2030 quality targets; National Patient Safety Centre,Incident reporting system data; patient safety walkround records; fall prevention audit; medication safety audit; CBAHI patient safety standards compliance,Patient safety theory (Reason 2000): Swiss cheese model — multiple layers of defence against medical error. Quality management theory (Donabedian 1988): structure-process-outcome model for healthcare quality. Reporting culture theory: just culture approach to patient safety incident reporting.,FOR: MOH Patient Safety Standards (2023 update) align with WHO Patient Safety Framework; mandatory incident reporting creates learning culture; explicit medical device safety provisions link to SFDA MDR vigilance requirements. AGAINST: Reporting culture in KSA healthcare still developing — fear of punitive response inhibits voluntary reporting; no published patient safety statistics to benchmark improvement.,Criticisms: (1) Patient safety incident reporting rates in KSA hospitals remain below international benchmarks; (2) No published national patient safety database — CBAHI collects data but limited public transparency; (3) IoMT device safety incidents not clearly mapped to either MOH-PS or SFDA MDR reporting — gap in GH-ICGA coverage; (4) Just culture principles not yet embedded in KSA labour law — staff fear termination for honest error reporting.,"Framework comparison requires contextual analysis. Key comparators: NIST CSF 2.0 (international benchmark), ISO 27001 (certifiable standard), ECC-2 (KSA mandatory baseline). See main database for cross-framework mapping.",Reason (2000) Human Error; Donabedian (1988) Quality of Care Assessment; WHO Patient Safety Framework (2021); Al-Shehri et al. (2023) Patient Safety in Saudi Hospitals; CBAHI Annual Report (2024).,"Official: Consult the official source URL in the database for the primary document. Training: ISACA, ISC2, RAPS, IAPP, and HIMSS offer professional development covering most regulatory domains. Estimated hours vary by framework complexity.",GH-ICGA positions MOH-PS as the PATIENT SAFETY layer — directly intersects with IoMT governance because medical device failures are patient safety events. Thesis argues the gap between MOH-PS incident reporting and SFDA MDR vigilance reporting is a critical governance gap for connected medical devices.,G + C (Governance + Compliance — standard regulatory framework positioning),Senior management: accountability for framework compliance. Designated compliance officer: monitor requirements. All staff: awareness of applicable obligations,Regulatory Risk; Operational Risk; Reputational Risk — specific domains determined by framework scope,"See official source — legal obligation, regulatory requirement, or voluntary standard as applicable",Administrative; Technical; Operational — specific distribution determined by framework content,1. Compliance status (compliant/non-compliant). 2. # open findings from last assessment. 3. % controls implemented. 4. Time to remediate findings. 5. Incident rate in framework scope area,Self-assessment; internal audit; external assessment as specified by issuing authority,ServiceNow GRC; IBM OpenPages; RSA Archer; MetricStream — all major GRC platforms support custom framework import. Contact vendor for framework-specific module,Third parties in scope must demonstrate equivalent compliance. Contractual compliance obligations; right-to-audit clauses; annual third-party assessment,Non-compliance: regulatory action by issuing authority; fines and penalties as specified; operational and reputational consequences,Healthcare & Life Sciences,Sector-Specific,,,,,
30,MOH,Ministry of Health,2. Healthcare & Life Sciences,MOH-PR,Patient Rights Charter,ميثاق حقوق المريض,2019,2012,45,ISSUES/ENFORCES,Mandatory Enforcer,All healthcare facilities,HIGH,https://www.moh.gov.sa/en/,MOH official,First Charter ~2012 → updated 2019,"45 rights and obligations. Key provisions: informed consent (including electronic consent for telemedicine), right to access medical records (digital access through NABIDH patient portal), right to privacy of health information, right to refuse treatment, right to second opinion. 2019 update added digital health rights (right to data portability from health apps)",First: ~2012. Updated: 2019,Review 2024-2025 expected to add AI/algorithmic transparency rights,CURRENT — 2019 version,Penalties defined by issuing authority; consult official source. NCA ECC-2 penalties apply if relevant,Issuing authority and/or designated inspection department,As specified in framework scope — consult official document,Both (unless specified otherwise in official document),As defined by issuing authority,As defined by issuing authority,To be determined from framework text — see official source,See framework preamble for international standard references,Arabic (primary in KSA); English may be available,Mapped to relevant GH-ICGA layer — requires manual assessment,See official framework for maturity model details,Aligned with relevant Vision 2030 programme — see framework context,As specified in framework compliance requirements,Institutional theory and regulatory governance theory underpin all KSA regulatory frameworks. Specific theoretical basis varies by framework — consult academic literature via Google Scholar using framework name as search term.,"Academic debate varies by framework maturity and international adoption. For KSA-specific debate, consult: Almohammad & Wills (2021) J Cybersecurity; Al-Khater et al. (2020) IEEE Access; Baker McKenzie KSA regulatory updates.",Known criticisms include: implementation gap between standards and practice; resource constraints for smaller healthcare entities; rapid technology evolution outpacing regulation. Consult framework evaluation literature for specifics.,"Framework comparison requires contextual analysis. Key comparators: NIST CSF 2.0 (international benchmark), ISO 27001 (certifiable standard), ECC-2 (KSA mandatory baseline). See main database for cross-framework mapping.","Key foundational references: Almohammad A & Wills G (2021) J Cybersecurity; NIST (2024) CSF 2.0; ISO (2022) 27001; NCA official documents (nca.gov.sa); Baker McKenzie Global Data & Cyber Handbook 2025. Consult SFDA, MOH, and NCA websites for framework-specific official citations.","Official: Consult the official source URL in the database for the primary document. Training: ISACA, ISC2, RAPS, IAPP, and HIMSS offer professional development covering most regulatory domains. Estimated hours vary by framework complexity.",Relevance to GH-ICGA framework varies by layer — see GH-ICGA Layer Mapping column. All KSA mandatory frameworks are compliance inputs to GH-ICGA Layer 4. Frameworks with IoMT-specific provisions directly inform Layers 2 and 3.,G + C (Governance + Compliance — standard regulatory framework positioning),Senior management: accountability for framework compliance. Designated compliance officer: monitor requirements. All staff: awareness of applicable obligations,Regulatory Risk; Operational Risk; Reputational Risk — specific domains determined by framework scope,"See official source — legal obligation, regulatory requirement, or voluntary standard as applicable",Administrative; Technical; Operational — specific distribution determined by framework content,1. Compliance status (compliant/non-compliant). 2. # open findings from last assessment. 3. % controls implemented. 4. Time to remediate findings. 5. Incident rate in framework scope area,Self-assessment; internal audit; external assessment as specified by issuing authority,ServiceNow GRC; IBM OpenPages; RSA Archer; MetricStream — all major GRC platforms support custom framework import. Contact vendor for framework-specific module,Third parties in scope must demonstrate equivalent compliance. Contractual compliance obligations; right-to-audit clauses; annual third-party assessment,Non-compliance: regulatory action by issuing authority; fines and penalties as specified; operational and reputational consequences,Healthcare & Life Sciences,Sector-Specific,,,,,
31,MOH,Ministry of Health,2. Healthcare & Life Sciences,MOH-TELE,Telemedicine Regulations,لائحة الرعاية الصحية عن بعد,Late 2023,2020,38,ISSUES/ENFORCES,Mandatory Enforcer,All licensed telemedicine platform operators,HIGHEST,https://www.moh.gov.sa/en/,Hammad & Al-Mehdar 2025,COVID-19 emergency 2020 → comprehensive regulations late 2023,"2020 (emergency COVID telehealth rules): basic platform licensing, identity verification, audio/video minimum standards. Late 2023 (comprehensive update, 38 controls): platform licensing requirements; patient identity verification; cross-border telemedicine restrictions; controlled substance prescribing rules (limited); data protection (PDPL alignment); patient consent requirements; clinical documentation standards; coverage by health insurance; quality assurance audit requirements",Emergency 2020. Comprehensive: late 2023,Annual update; cross-border rules under active development 2025,CURRENT — late 2023 comprehensive version,Platform licence revocation; SAR 1M–5M fine (scale by offence); clinical professional disciplinary action by SCFHS,MOH — eHealth General Directorate; CST (platform technical licensing); CCHI (insurance coverage compliance); SCFHS (clinical standards),Hospital (outpatient teleconsultation); Clinic; Health IT Vendor (platform developers); Health Insurer (reimbursement compliance),Both,MOH platform licence audit (annual); CCHI insurance compliance check; SCFHS clinical quality audit,Annual licence renewal; random clinical quality audit,15 (remote patient monitoring integration; wearable device data for teleconsultation; IoMT vital sign display during video consultation; remote device prescription; teleICU monitoring requirements),WHO Telemedicine Guidelines (2010/updated); ISO 13131:2021 (Telehealth); ATA (American Telemedicine Association) standards (reference),Arabic (primary); English technical guidance,Layer 1: Governance; Layer 4: Compliance,No formal maturity model; licence tiers (basic/advanced platform),HSTP telemedicine targets (30% uptake); SEHA Virtual Hospital; Vision 2030 healthcare access targets,Platform licence certificate; patient consent records; clinical documentation standards compliance; identity verification log; prescription records; NABIDH integration evidence,Telehealth adoption theory (LeRouge & Garfield 2013): patient acceptance + clinician integration. Access theory: geography-equity argument for remote care. Digital health governance theory: platform regulation vs clinical regulation tension.,FOR: COVID-19 proved telehealth viability at scale in KSA (Al-Ataawi et al. 2022); Sehhaty platform shows government commitment; removes geographic barriers consistent with Vision 2030 equity goals; IoMT remote monitoring reimbursement framework emerging. AGAINST: Clinical quality in telehealth still debated (Hollander & Sites 2020); data sovereignty for cross-border telehealth unresolved; prescription via teleconsultation remains restricted limiting scope; rural connectivity gaps undermine access equity goal,Regulations still lag technology — VR/AR clinical applications not addressed; AI-assisted teleconsultation regulatory pathway unclear; cross-border telehealth restrictions create market fragmentation; no minimum quality standards for audio/video equipment; liability for technical failure not yet codified in regulation,MOH-TELE vs NHS TECS (UK): NHS TECS has DTAC (Digital Technology Assessment Criteria) for all digital health tools — KSA equivalent absent. MOH-TELE vs FCC/CMS telehealth (US): US has federal reimbursement codes (CPT); KSA CCHI integration still developing. MOH-TELE vs WHO Telemedicine Guidelines: broadly aligned but KSA-specific cross-border rules more restrictive than WHO recommends,Al-Ataawi A et al. (2022) 'Telehealth in Saudi Arabia During COVID-19' J Healthcare Informatics Res; Hollander JE & Sites FD (2020) 'Telehealth Beyond COVID-19' NEJM 2020; Hammad & Al-Mehdar (2025) Saudi Healthcare Regulatory Update; WHO (2010) Telemedicine Opportunities and Developments; LeRouge C & Garfield MJ (2013) 'Crossing the Telemedicine Chasm' JAMIA 20(6),Official: MOH website. Training: American Telemedicine Association (ATA) online courses; SEHA Virtual Hospital training programme; WHO telehealth competency framework. Estimated: 25 hours,GH-ICGA Layer 1 (Governance) — telehealth regulation defines the governance boundary for remote IoMT monitoring. Thesis relevance: GH-ICGA must address the governance overlap between MOH-TELE (clinical platform regulation) and NCA-ECC (cybersecurity) and PDPL (patient data) for remote monitoring scenarios,G + C (platform governance + clinical compliance),CEO: platform licence accountability. Medical Director: clinical quality governance. DPO: PDPL compliance for patient data on platform. Technical Director: platform security. Pharmacy Director: prescription management compliance,Clinical Risk (misdiagnosis via teleconsultation); Regulatory Risk (licence breach); Privacy Risk (patient data); Operational Risk (platform availability); Cross-Border Risk (international teleconsultation restrictions),REGULATORY — MOH licensing requirement. Non-compliance: platform licence revocation; SAR 1M–5M fine; SCFHS action against clinicians,Administrative (A): 20 controls — licensing; consent; documentation; clinical protocols. Technical (T): 12 controls — platform security; identity verification; audio/video standards. Operational (O): 6 controls — prescribing workflow; referral protocol; audit trail,"1. Patient consent collection rate (target 100% before each session). 2. Platform availability SLA compliance (target >99.5%). 3. Identity verification success rate. 4. # clinical complaints per 1,000 teleconsultations. 5. Documentation compliance rate (target 100% consultation notes in NABIDH). 6. Prescription audit pass rate",Annual MOH licence renewal audit; CCHI insurance coverage compliance check; patient record documentation audit; platform security assessment; identity verification system test; NABIDH integration test,Sehhaty / Mawid (MOH official platforms); Nabdh; Cura Health; Vezeeta; Nubaqraa — all require MOH licensing. Security tools: FortiWeb (WAF); Auth0/Okta (identity); TeleVisit security frameworks,Platform vendors: MOH-licensed telemedicine solution mandatory; PDPL DPA required; NABIDH API certification; patient data not to be shared with third-party analytics without consent; international teleconsultation providers must be MOH-approved,Unlicensed platform: immediate shutdown; SAR fine; clinical professional disciplinary action. Licence revocation: complete business cessation. Patient harm via telemedicine: malpractice liability; SCFHS investigation,Healthcare & Life Sciences; ICT/Digital (platform providers),Sector-Specific,,,,,
