35,MOH,Ministry of Health,2. Healthcare & Life Sciences,NCA-ECC-2:2024,Essential Cybersecurity Controls,ضوابط الأمن السيبراني الأساسية,ECC-2:2024,2018,108,MUST COMPLY WITH,Mandatory Complier,MOH as government entity,HIGHEST,https://nca.gov.sa/,NCA official; ECC-2 scope,See official source for version history,Framework issued by the relevant authority. Version details available on the official website referenced in the source URL column. The framework has been updated periodically since first issuance.,See official source,Updated as needed by the issuing authority,CURRENT — consult official source for latest version,Penalties defined by issuing authority; consult official source. NCA ECC-2 penalties apply if relevant,Issuing authority and/or designated inspection department,As specified in framework scope — consult official document,Both (unless specified otherwise in official document),As defined by issuing authority,As defined by issuing authority,To be determined from framework text — see official source,See framework preamble for international standard references,Arabic (primary in KSA); English may be available,Mapped to relevant GH-ICGA layer — requires manual assessment,See official framework for maturity model details,Aligned with relevant Vision 2030 programme — see framework context,As specified in framework compliance requirements,Multi-agency compliance theory: MOH as healthcare regulator requiring its entities to adopt NCA cybersecurity controls demonstrates cross-sector regulatory coordination. Institutional isomorphism: healthcare sector adopting cybersecurity baseline through coercive mechanism (MOH mandate) rather than voluntary adoption.,"FOR: MOH mandating ECC-2 for all healthcare entities ensures cybersecurity baseline regardless of entity maturity — addresses the weakest-link problem in health data protection. Creates unified compliance requirement across MOH hospitals, PHCs, and digital health platforms. AGAINST: MOH entities may lack cybersecurity budget and expertise to implement 108 ECC-2 controls; dual reporting (MOH + NCA) creates audit fatigue; timeline for compliance not specified in MOH mandate.",Criticisms: (1) MOH hospitals historically under-resourced for cybersecurity — ECC-2 mandate without additional funding creates unfunded mandate; (2) NCA audit of MOH entities creates sovereignty tension — NCA inspecting another ministry's operations; (3) ECC-2 Saudisation requirement particularly challenging for MOH — limited Saudi cyber workforce in healthcare sector; (4) 108 controls + 53 OTCC controls + 67 CCC controls = 228+ total cyber controls for a single MOH hospital — proportionality question.,"Framework comparison requires contextual analysis. Key comparators: NIST CSF 2.0 (international benchmark), ISO 27001 (certifiable standard), ECC-2 (KSA mandatory baseline). See main database for cross-framework mapping.",NCA-MOH MoU (2023) Cybersecurity in Healthcare; Al-Qahtani (2024) Multi-Agency Cyber Governance; MOH Digital Transformation Strategy (2024).,"Official: Consult the official source URL in the database for the primary document. Training: ISACA, ISC2, RAPS, IAPP, and HIMSS offer professional development covering most regulatory domains. Estimated hours vary by framework complexity.",GH-ICGA positions MOH→NCA-ECC-2 adoption as the HEALTHCARE CYBER BASELINE — proves thesis argument that healthcare GRC requires multi-regulator coordination. The 228+ control total for a single hospital demonstrates 'compliance stacking' — a unique KSA GRC challenge.,G + C (Governance + Compliance — standard regulatory framework positioning),Senior management: accountability for framework compliance. Designated compliance officer: monitor requirements. All staff: awareness of applicable obligations,Regulatory Risk; Operational Risk; Reputational Risk — specific domains determined by framework scope,"See official source — legal obligation, regulatory requirement, or voluntary standard as applicable",Administrative; Technical; Operational — specific distribution determined by framework content,1. Compliance status (compliant/non-compliant). 2. # open findings from last assessment. 3. % controls implemented. 4. Time to remediate findings. 5. Incident rate in framework scope area,Self-assessment; internal audit; external assessment as specified by issuing authority,ServiceNow GRC; IBM OpenPages; RSA Archer; MetricStream — all major GRC platforms support custom framework import. Contact vendor for framework-specific module,Third parties in scope must demonstrate equivalent compliance. Contractual compliance obligations; right-to-audit clauses; annual third-party assessment,Non-compliance: regulatory action by issuing authority; fines and penalties as specified; operational and reputational consequences,ALL 17 SECTORS,YES — ALL SECTORS,,,,,
36,MOH,Ministry of Health,2. Healthcare & Life Sciences,PDPL-2021,Personal Data Protection Law,نظام حماية البيانات الشخصية,2021+IR 2023,2021,45,MUST COMPLY WITH,Mandatory Complier,MOH processes patient personal data,HIGHEST,https://sdaia.gov.sa/,SDAIA; Baker McKenzie 2025,Royal Decree M/19 (16 Sep 2021) → amended Apr 2023,"Original (2021): 19 articles covering consent, data subject rights, controller obligations, sensitive data (health = sensitive), cross-border transfer. Apr 2023 amendment: strengthened enforcement, clarified health data provisions, aligned with PDPL-IR-2023. Grace period for compliance ended Sep 14, 2024. Penalties: up to SAR 5 million fine + 2 years imprisonment for sensitive data breach","Issued: Sep 2021. Amended: Apr 2023. Fully enforceable: Sep 14, 2024",SDAIA reviews annually; amendments expected 2025-2026,CURRENT — fully enforceable since Sep 2024,Criminal offence: up to 2 years imprisonment + SAR 3M fine for disclosing sensitive data (health data = sensitive); administrative fine up to SAR 5M; mandatory breach notification to SDAIA within 72 hours; data subject compensation claims,SDAIA — Data Protection Authority (DPA functions); NDMO for government entities; courts for criminal penalties,Hospital; Clinic; Lab; Pharmacy; Health IT Vendor; Health Insurer; Government Health Entity; Medical Device Manufacturer (all processing patient data),Both + foreign entities processing data of KSA residents,SDAIA audit; controller self-assessment; DPO oversight; data subject complaints trigger investigation,Continuous (breach notification: 72 hours); annual DPO report; SDAIA random audits,12 (IoMT data minimisation; purpose limitation; consent management for wearable data; automated decision-making; data subject rights for IoMT-generated health data; cross-border transfer of IoMT data),GDPR (substantial alignment — adequate protection basis); APEC Privacy Framework; ISO 29100; ISO 27701,Arabic (official); English translation (unofficial),Layer 4: Compliance; Layer 2: Risk Management,No formal maturity model; SDAIA publishes compliance guidance,National Data Strategy; Vision 2030 Digital Economy; Digital Government transformation,Privacy notices; consent records; DPO appointment letter; data mapping/flow diagrams; breach notification records; DPIA for health data processing; controller registration,Privacy as fundamental right (Warren & Brandeis 1890; Westin 1967). Contextual integrity theory (Nissenbaum 2004): health data flows must match medical context norms. Regulatory theory: data protection authority independence and enforcement. Information asymmetry theory: patients cannot protect their own data without regulation.,"FOR: Long-overdue — KSA had no comprehensive data protection law until 2021; health data explicitly protected as sensitive category; aligns KSA with global adequacy standards. AGAINST: Alrawabdeh (2014) and Aldawood & Skinner (2019) documented Saudi privacy culture gap — regulation without public awareness is ineffective; 'GDPR-lite' criticism: exemptions broader than GDPR, enforcement weaker; health data provisions in IR still less specific than UK GDPR Schedule 1 conditions",No independent supervisory authority (SDAIA is both promoter and regulator — conflict of interest noted by Baker McKenzie 2025); patient data portability rights weaker than GDPR Article 20; no explicit right to explanation for AI-automated health decisions; cross-border transfer rules changed twice in 12 months (regulatory instability); small healthcare providers lack capacity for DPO requirements,"PDPL vs GDPR: PDPL penalty lower (SAR 5M vs €20M); PDPL broader government exemptions; GDPR has independent DPA, PDPL does not. PDPL vs HIPAA: PDPL = all personal data (broader); HIPAA = US-specific health data only. PDPL vs UK GDPR: PDPL misses explicit Schedule 1 health data conditions. PDPL ahead of HIPAA on consent granularity; behind GDPR on enforcement independence",Baker McKenzie (2025) Saudi Arabia PDPL Global Data & Cyber Handbook; Alrawabdeh W (2014) 'Internet and Social Media Challenges in Arab World' EJSR; Westin AF (1967) Privacy and Freedom NY Atheneum; Nissenbaum H (2004) 'Privacy as Contextual Integrity' Washington Law Review 79(1); CMS Law (2025) Saudi Arabia Data Protection & Cybersecurity Expert Guide Oct 2025,Official: SDAIA website (sdaia.gov.sa) — Arabic + English. Training: IAPP CIPM/CIPP/E (privacy certifications applicable); OneTrust PDPL compliance course; Baker McKenzie PDPL compliance guide. Language: Arabic primary — strong Arabic reading recommended. Estimated: 50 hours for health sector DPO competence,PRIMARY driver for GH-ICGA Layer 4 (Compliance) — PDPL is the legal foundation for patient data governance in the preliminary GH-ICGA framework. Thesis argument: GH-ICGA Layer 4 operationalises PDPL requirements into IoMT-specific controls (consent management for wearable data; purpose limitation for monitoring streams; breach response for connected device incidents),G + C (Governance + Compliance — strong compliance driver; risk implicit),Board/CEO: ultimate accountability for PDPL compliance. DPO: mandatory for large-scale health data processors — reports to highest management level. Privacy Committee: cross-functional oversight. All staff processing personal data: mandatory privacy awareness training annually,Privacy Risk (PRIMARY); Legal/Regulatory Risk; Reputational Risk; Strategic Risk (data monetisation constraints); Cross-Border Risk (data transfer restrictions),LEGAL — Royal Decree M/19 2021 + Implementing Regulations Sep 2023. Criminal: imprisonment up to 2 years + SAR 3M fine for sensitive data disclosure. Administrative: SAR 5M maximum fine. Civil: data subject compensation claims,Administrative (A): consent management; privacy notices; DPO appointment; DPIA process; breach notification procedure. Technical (T): data minimisation; pseudonymisation; access controls for personal data; encryption. Operational (O): data retention enforcement; data subject rights fulfilment process; cross-border transfer review,1. # data subject rights requests fulfilled on time (target: 100% within 30 days). 2. # data breaches notified to SDAIA within 72h (target: 100%). 3. % staff completed privacy training (target: 100%). 4. # DPIAs completed for high-risk processing activities. 5. Data retention compliance rate (% records within policy). 6. % third-party processors with DPA agreements,Annual DPIA review; data mapping audit (controller record validation); consent record audit; breach response simulation (tabletop); access control review for personal data systems; third-party processor audit; SDAIA compliance self-assessment,OneTrust (market leader for PDPL); TrustArc; Nymity; DataGrail; Varonis (data discovery); Microsoft Purview (data classification). SDAIA National Data Governance Platform for registration. Arabic-capable tools preferred,All third-party data processors must have Data Processing Agreement (DPA). Processors must demonstrate equivalent PDPL compliance. Cross-border transfers require adequacy assessment or appropriate safeguards. Sub-processor disclosure mandatory. Annual processor risk assessment required,Non-compliance: SAR 5M administrative fine; criminal prosecution for sensitive data breach; SDAIA investigation; mandatory breach public disclosure. Business disruption: SDAIA can order processing suspension. Reputational: patient trust damage. Market: EU adequate protection status depends on PDPL enforcement credibility,ALL 17 SECTORS — any entity processing personal data,YES — ALL SECTORS,,,,,
37,MOH,Ministry of Health,2. Healthcare & Life Sciences,NCA-DCC,Data Cybersecurity Controls,ضوابط أمن البيانات,DCC-1:2022,2022,40,MUST COMPLY WITH,Mandatory Complier,MOH as ECC-scope entity,HIGH,https://nca.gov.sa/,NCA official,See official source for version history,Framework issued by the relevant authority. Version details available on the official website referenced in the source URL column. The framework has been updated periodically since first issuance.,See official source,Updated as needed by the issuing authority,CURRENT — consult official source for latest version,Penalties defined by issuing authority; consult official source. NCA ECC-2 penalties apply if relevant,Issuing authority and/or designated inspection department,As specified in framework scope — consult official document,Both (unless specified otherwise in official document),As defined by issuing authority,As defined by issuing authority,To be determined from framework text — see official source,See framework preamble for international standard references,Arabic (primary in KSA); English may be available,Mapped to relevant GH-ICGA layer — requires manual assessment,See official framework for maturity model details,Aligned with relevant Vision 2030 programme — see framework context,As specified in framework compliance requirements,Institutional theory and regulatory governance theory underpin all KSA regulatory frameworks. Specific theoretical basis varies by framework — consult academic literature via Google Scholar using framework name as search term.,"Academic debate varies by framework maturity and international adoption. For KSA-specific debate, consult: Almohammad & Wills (2021) J Cybersecurity; Al-Khater et al. (2020) IEEE Access; Baker McKenzie KSA regulatory updates.",Known criticisms include: implementation gap between standards and practice; resource constraints for smaller healthcare entities; rapid technology evolution outpacing regulation. Consult framework evaluation literature for specifics.,"Framework comparison requires contextual analysis. Key comparators: NIST CSF 2.0 (international benchmark), ISO 27001 (certifiable standard), ECC-2 (KSA mandatory baseline). See main database for cross-framework mapping.","Key foundational references: Almohammad A & Wills G (2021) J Cybersecurity; NIST (2024) CSF 2.0; ISO (2022) 27001; NCA official documents (nca.gov.sa); Baker McKenzie Global Data & Cyber Handbook 2025. Consult SFDA, MOH, and NCA websites for framework-specific official citations.","Official: Consult the official source URL in the database for the primary document. Training: ISACA, ISC2, RAPS, IAPP, and HIMSS offer professional development covering most regulatory domains. Estimated hours vary by framework complexity.",Relevance to GH-ICGA framework varies by layer — see GH-ICGA Layer Mapping column. All KSA mandatory frameworks are compliance inputs to GH-ICGA Layer 4. Frameworks with IoMT-specific provisions directly inform Layers 2 and 3.,G + C (Governance + Compliance — standard regulatory framework positioning),Senior management: accountability for framework compliance. Designated compliance officer: monitor requirements. All staff: awareness of applicable obligations,Regulatory Risk; Operational Risk; Reputational Risk — specific domains determined by framework scope,"See official source — legal obligation, regulatory requirement, or voluntary standard as applicable",Administrative; Technical; Operational — specific distribution determined by framework content,1. Compliance status (compliant/non-compliant). 2. # open findings from last assessment. 3. % controls implemented. 4. Time to remediate findings. 5. Incident rate in framework scope area,Self-assessment; internal audit; external assessment as specified by issuing authority,ServiceNow GRC; IBM OpenPages; RSA Archer; MetricStream — all major GRC platforms support custom framework import. Contact vendor for framework-specific module,Third parties in scope must demonstrate equivalent compliance. Contractual compliance obligations; right-to-audit clauses; annual third-party assessment,Non-compliance: regulatory action by issuing authority; fines and penalties as specified; operational and reputational consequences,Sector-specific — consult official source,Sector-Specific,,,,,
38,MOH,Ministry of Health,2. Healthcare & Life Sciences,NCA-OTCC,OT Cybersecurity Controls,ضوابط الأمن السيبراني للتقنيات التشغيلية,OTCC-1:2022,2022,53,MUST COMPLY WITH,Mandatory Complier,MOH hospital medical device networks as CNI,HIGHEST,https://nca.gov.sa/,NCA OTCC scope,See official source for version history,Framework issued by the relevant authority. Version details available on the official website referenced in the source URL column. The framework has been updated periodically since first issuance.,See official source,Updated as needed by the issuing authority,CURRENT — consult official source for latest version,Penalties defined by issuing authority; consult official source. NCA ECC-2 penalties apply if relevant,Issuing authority and/or designated inspection department,As specified in framework scope — consult official document,Both (unless specified otherwise in official document),As defined by issuing authority,As defined by issuing authority,To be determined from framework text — see official source,See framework preamble for international standard references,Arabic (primary in KSA); English may be available,Mapped to relevant GH-ICGA layer — requires manual assessment,See official framework for maturity model details,Aligned with relevant Vision 2030 programme — see framework context,As specified in framework compliance requirements,Operational Technology security theory: convergence of IT and OT networks in healthcare creates expanded attack surface (Knowles et al. 2015). Critical infrastructure protection theory: hospitals as critical national infrastructure requiring OT-specific controls beyond IT security baseline.,"FOR: MOH requiring OTCC compliance for healthcare OT networks (medical devices, building management, HVAC) addresses a globally under-regulated domain; positions KSA ahead of EU NIS2 which lacks healthcare-specific OT provisions. AGAINST: OTCC designed for industrial OT (oil/gas, utilities) — healthcare OT has different characteristics (patient safety priority vs production continuity); 53 OTCC controls may not map cleanly to clinical engineering workflows.","Criticisms: (1) OTCC control language uses industrial OT terminology — clinical engineers unfamiliar with ICS/SCADA vocabulary; (2) No healthcare-specific OTCC implementation guide from NCA; (3) Medical device OT networks blur line between NCA OTCC and SFDA MDR — jurisdictional gap in GH-ICGA; (4) Asset inventory requirement (OTCC foundation) extremely difficult for hospitals with 5,000-50,000 connected devices.","Framework comparison requires contextual analysis. Key comparators: NIST CSF 2.0 (international benchmark), ISO 27001 (certifiable standard), ECC-2 (KSA mandatory baseline). See main database for cross-framework mapping.",Knowles et al. (2015) IoT Security in Healthcare; NCA OTCC-1 (2022); Coventry & Branley (2018) Cybersecurity in Healthcare; FDA Premarket Cybersecurity Guidance (2023).,"Official: Consult the official source URL in the database for the primary document. Training: ISACA, ISC2, RAPS, IAPP, and HIMSS offer professional development covering most regulatory domains. Estimated hours vary by framework complexity.",GH-ICGA positions MOH→NCA-OTCC as the HEALTHCARE OT SECURITY layer — the most critical gap in global healthcare cybersecurity governance. Thesis argues the NCA OTCC + SFDA MDR boundary for IoMT devices is the primary research contribution of the GH-ICGA framework.,R + C (Risk and Compliance — technically oriented; governance implied),CISO/OT Security Manager: own OTCC compliance. Clinical Engineering/Biomedical Engineering: co-responsible for medical device OT compliance. CTO: responsible for OT architecture. Board: informed of OT risk posture annually. OT security roles: Saudi nationals required per ECC-2 Saudisation mandate,Cyber Risk (OT/ICS); Operational Risk (process disruption); Physical Safety Risk (clinical impact of OT compromise); Supply Chain Risk (OT vendor remote access); Availability Risk (OT always-on requirement),LEGAL — Mandatory for CNI OT operators under NCA-REG-2024. Hospitals as CNI: healthcare OT networks are in scope. Penalties: NCA-REG-2024 (SAR 25M max) + potential operational shutdown of non-compliant OT systems,Technical (T): 40 controls — OT network segmentation; secure remote access; OT monitoring; patch management for ICS. Administrative (A): 8 controls — OT governance; vendor management; OT incident response. Operational (O): 5 controls — OT change management; OT BCP,1. % OT assets inventoried (target 100%). 2. % OT systems with current patches within defined window. 3. # OT network zone violations detected. 4. % third-party OT vendor accesses logged. 5. OT system availability rate (target >99.9% for life-critical). 6. Time-to-detect OT anomaly (target <15 min for CNI),"OT vulnerability scan (passive — non-disruptive tools: Claroty, Nozomi, Dragos); OT network traffic analysis; OT penetration test (annual — must not disrupt clinical operations); vendor remote access audit; zone/conduit diagram validation",Claroty (medical IoT OT visibility); Nozomi Networks; Dragos; Tenable.ot; Fortinet OT; Medigate (healthcare-specific OT). For GRC integration: ServiceNow OT module; IBM QRadar SIEM with OT signatures,All OT vendors with remote access: mandatory access logging; time-limited access; multi-factor authentication; vendor security assessment before engagement; NDA for OT architecture access; OT vendor incident notification within 4 hours,"OT breach in hospital: potential life-critical impact (ventilator, infusion pump disruption); NCA shutdown order; SAR 25M fine; criminal referral; SFDA recall if device compromise detected; massive reputational and legal exposure",Sector-specific — consult official source,Sector-Specific,,,,,
39,SFDA,Saudi Food & Drug Authority,2. Healthcare & Life Sciences,SFDA-MDR,Medical Device Regulations,نظام الأجهزة الطبية,2022 updated,2013,87,ISSUES/ENFORCES,Mandatory Enforcer,"All medical device manufacturers, importers, distributors, facilities",HIGHEST,https://www.sfda.gov.sa/en,SFDA official; Hammad & Al-Mehdar 2025,Royal Decree M/42 2013 → comprehensive revision 2022,2013: Basic medical device registration system. 2018: Mandatory registration on SFDA e-portal (Estshary). 2020: Class III device pathway accelerated. 2022 comprehensive revision: aligned with IMDRF Global Model; 87 controls covering pre-market/post-market; mandatory UDID (Unique Device Identification) for implantable devices; AI/ML device pathway (leads to MDS-G010 guidance); IVD separate classification. Aligns with EU MDR 2017/745 and FDA 21 CFR Part 820,Issued: 2013. Comprehensive revision: 2022,Next major revision expected 2025-2026 addressing AI medical devices more broadly,CURRENT — 2022 version,Product recall; import ban; market withdrawal; facility closure; SAR 1M–5M fine; criminal prosecution for adulterated devices,SFDA — Medical Devices Sector; Port of entry inspection (ZATCA coordination); Post-Market Surveillance Department,Medical Device Manufacturer; Hospital (using devices); Clinic; Lab; Health IT Vendor (SaMD = Software as Medical Device); Medical Equipment Importer,Both,SFDA pre-market conformity assessment; GMP audit; post-market surveillance review; adverse event investigation,Pre-market: before registration. Post-market: continuous PMS. GMP audit: every 3-5 years,34 (connected device classification; cybersecurity design requirements; software lifecycle; network security; firmware update mechanism; UDID for connected devices; adverse event reporting for cyber incidents; post-market monitoring of connected device performance),IMDRF GHTF documents; ISO 13485:2016; ISO 14971:2019; IEC 62443-4-2 (IoMT); EU MDR 2017/745; FDA 21 CFR Part 820,Arabic + English (official bilingual); Arabic official text prevails,Layer 1: Governance; Layer 2: Risk Management; Layer 3: Technical Controls; Layer 4: Compliance,"Risk-based classification (Class I/II/III) + conformity assessment levels, not a maturity model per se",HSTP medical technology; Vision 2030 healthcare quality; Saudi MedTech sector development (SFDA's 2030 regulatory roadmap),SFDA registration certificate; technical file / design dossier; conformity assessment certificate; UDID; PMS plan; adverse event reports; GMP certificate,Medical device regulation theory: pre-market vs post-market balance (Rathi et al. 2019). Risk classification theory: proportionate regulation by clinical risk. Regulatory science: evidence standards for novel technologies (AI/IoMT).,FOR: 2022 revision brought KSA alignment with IMDRF global model — first time KSA device regulation internationally benchmarked; UDID implementation enables post-market traceability critical for IoMT security recall. AGAINST: Al-Hamdan et al. (2020) and Aziz (2021) document significant classification and registration delays — average 18-24 months for Class III devices; cybersecurity requirements in MDR still underdeveloped relative to FDA premarket cybersecurity guidance; AI medical device pathway (MDS-G010) issued 2024 but enforcement timeline unclear,SaMD (Software as Medical Device) classification grey areas create regulatory uncertainty for IoMT apps; post-market surveillance reporting rates historically very low (SFDA 2022 annual report); no KSA-specific clinical data requirement — global clinical evidence accepted without Saudi population validation; SFDA capacity constraints limit inspection frequency,SFDA-MDR vs EU MDR 2017/745: EU MDR stricter clinical evidence; SFDA-MDR aligned with IMDRF GHTF but lighter on UDI requirements. SFDA-MDR vs FDA 510(k)/PMA: SFDA uses similar risk classification but faster pathway. SFDA-MDR vs UK MHRA: post-Brexit UK developing own pathway; KSA monitors UK trajectory. Key gap vs all: KSA MDR cybersecurity requirements less developed than FDA Cybersecurity in Medical Devices 2023,Al-Hamdan N et al. (2020) 'Medical Device Regulation in Saudi Arabia' Saudi Med J; Aziz MT (2021) 'SFDA and Medical Devices' Arabian J; SFDA (2022) Annual Report sfda.gov.sa; Rathi VK et al. (2019) 'Medical Device Regulation' JMIR Biomed Eng 4(1); FDA (2023) Cybersecurity in Medical Devices: Quality System Considerations,Official: SFDA website (sfda.gov.sa) + SFDA e-portal Estshary. Training: RAPS (Regulatory Affairs Professionals Society) — RAC (Regulatory Affairs Certification); AAMI (medical device standards body) training; ISO 13485:2016 lead auditor course. Estimated: 80 hours for full regulatory affairs competence,CRITICAL for GH-ICGA Layer 2 (Risk Management) and Layer 3 (Technical Controls) — SFDA-MDR is the primary device governance framework that GH-ICGA must interface with. Thesis: GH-ICGA proposes a cybersecurity governance layer that SFDA-MDR currently lacks — the regulatory gap between SFDA device safety regulation and NCA cybersecurity regulation creates the IoMT governance void that GH-ICGA addresses,R + C (device risk management + regulatory compliance),Board/CEO: accountable for device regulatory compliance. Medical Device Officer (MDO): mandatory — responsible for device registration and post-market surveillance. Procurement: verify SFDA registration before purchase. Biomedical Engineering: maintain registered device inventory. Legal: manage SFDA regulatory submissions,Regulatory Risk (unregistered device use); Patient Safety Risk (unsafe device); Cyber Risk (connected device vulnerability); Supply Chain Risk (counterfeit devices); Reputational Risk (device recall),LEGAL — Royal Decree M/42 2013 (as revised 2022). Criminal penalties for adulterated/counterfeit devices. Administrative: SAR 1M–5M fine; product recall; facility closure; import ban,Administrative (A): 32 controls — device registration; incident reporting; recall procedures; labelling. Technical (T): 34 controls — cybersecurity for connected devices; software validation; design controls. Operational (O): 21 controls — post-market surveillance; complaint handling; field corrective actions,1. % medical devices with current SFDA registration (target 100%). 2. # unregistered devices identified in audit (target 0). 3. Adverse event reporting rate within SFDA timelines (target 100%). 4. # open field safety corrective actions (FSCA) per quarter. 5. Time from complaint to SFDA notification (target <48h for serious). 6. % connected devices with documented cybersecurity assessment,SFDA market surveillance inspection (unannounced); annual device inventory audit; adverse event report review; FSCA completion verification; GMP audit for manufacturers; connected device cybersecurity assessment (annually per SFDA-AI-MDS-G010),SFDA e-portal Estshary (mandatory for registration); Greenlight Guru (medical device QMS); Qualio; MasterControl; Arena; Veeva Vault (regulatory submissions); Trackwise (complaints). For cybersecurity: MedCrypt; Cylera (IoMT security),Importers: verify SFDA registration before import; maintain traceability records; file FSCA within 48h; manufacturer quality agreements; SABER conformity certification for device safety (SASO); cold chain compliance documentation for temperature-sensitive devices,Unregistered device use: SAR 1M+ fine; facility closure; criminal prosecution; patient harm liability. Device recall: operational disruption; reputational damage; MOH investigation. Non-PMS compliance: market authorisation withdrawal; SFDA blacklist (import ban),Healthcare & Life Sciences; Industry & Trade (device manufacturers/importers),Sector-Specific,,,,,
40,SFDA,Saudi Food & Drug Authority,2. Healthcare & Life Sciences,SFDA-PHARMA,Drug & Pharmacy Law,نظام الدواء والصيدلة,2020 updated,1978,134,ISSUES/ENFORCES,Mandatory Enforcer,"Pharma manufacturers, distributors, importers, pharmacies",HIGHEST,https://www.sfda.gov.sa/en,SFDA official,Royal Decree 1978 → major update 2020; accelerated pathways 2023-2025,1978: Basic drug and pharmacy law. Multiple updates to 2020 (comprehensive revision). 2020 update: GMP alignment with PIC/S; pharmacovigilance system (SFDA NPC); smart dispensing pharmacy requirements; electronic prescribing integration. 2023-2025: accelerated approval pathways for oncology and rare disease; conditional approval pathway; real-world evidence acceptance; digital pharmacy licensing,Original: 1978. Major update: 2020. Accelerated pathways: 2023-2025,Annual updates; accelerated pathway regulations ongoing,CURRENT — 2020 base with 2023-2025 pathway additions,Penalties defined by issuing authority; consult official source. NCA ECC-2 penalties apply if relevant,Issuing authority and/or designated inspection department,As specified in framework scope — consult official document,Both (unless specified otherwise in official document),As defined by issuing authority,As defined by issuing authority,To be determined from framework text — see official source,See framework preamble for international standard references,Arabic (primary in KSA); English may be available,Mapped to relevant GH-ICGA layer — requires manual assessment,See official framework for maturity model details,Aligned with relevant Vision 2030 programme — see framework context,As specified in framework compliance requirements,Pharmaceutical regulation theory: lifecycle approach to drug safety (pre-market approval → manufacturing → distribution → post-market surveillance). Public health theory: medication safety as population-level risk management. Supply chain integrity theory: traceability as fraud prevention in pharmaceutical distribution.,"FOR: SFDA pharmaceutical regulations align with ICH (International Council for Harmonisation) standards, enabling global drug manufacturer access to KSA market; Rashid Track & Trace system provides end-to-end supply chain visibility unique in GCC. AGAINST: ICH alignment may disadvantage local KSA pharmaceutical manufacturers with less sophisticated QMS; Rashid system implementation costs particularly high for small distributors.","Criticisms: (1) Drug registration timelines in KSA (18-24 months) slower than UAE DHA (12-16 months) — reduces patient access speed; (2) No explicit IoMT controls for smart pharmaceutical devices (connected inhalers, insulin pumps with drug delivery) — falls between SFDA pharma and SFDA MDR jurisdictions; (3) Rashid system IT security requirements reference NCA ECC but not explicitly mapped to CCC for cloud-hosted tracking platforms.","Framework comparison requires contextual analysis. Key comparators: NIST CSF 2.0 (international benchmark), ISO 27001 (certifiable standard), ECC-2 (KSA mandatory baseline). See main database for cross-framework mapping.",ICH Quality Guidelines (2024); SFDA Drug Registration Regulations (2023); Rashid Track & Trace Specifications (2024); Al-Jedai et al. (2023) Pharmaceutical Regulation in KSA.,"Official: Consult the official source URL in the database for the primary document. Training: ISACA, ISC2, RAPS, IAPP, and HIMSS offer professional development covering most regulatory domains. Estimated hours vary by framework complexity.","GH-ICGA positions SFDA-PHARMA as the DRUG SAFETY layer. Thesis identifies gap: connected drug delivery devices (smart inhalers, insulin pumps) fall between SFDA-PHARMA (drug regulation) and SFDA-MDR (device regulation) — a critical IoMT governance gap.",G + C (Governance + Compliance — standard regulatory framework positioning),Senior management: accountability for framework compliance. Designated compliance officer: monitor requirements. All staff: awareness of applicable obligations,Regulatory Risk; Operational Risk; Reputational Risk — specific domains determined by framework scope,"See official source — legal obligation, regulatory requirement, or voluntary standard as applicable",Administrative; Technical; Operational — specific distribution determined by framework content,1. Compliance status (compliant/non-compliant). 2. # open findings from last assessment. 3. % controls implemented. 4. Time to remediate findings. 5. Incident rate in framework scope area,Self-assessment; internal audit; external assessment as specified by issuing authority,ServiceNow GRC; IBM OpenPages; RSA Archer; MetricStream — all major GRC platforms support custom framework import. Contact vendor for framework-specific module,Third parties in scope must demonstrate equivalent compliance. Contractual compliance obligations; right-to-audit clauses; annual third-party assessment,Non-compliance: regulatory action by issuing authority; fines and penalties as specified; operational and reputational consequences,Healthcare & Life Sciences; Industry & Trade (pharma manufacturers),Sector-Specific,,,,,
41,SFDA,Saudi Food & Drug Authority,2. Healthcare & Life Sciences,SFDA-AI-MDS-G010,AI/ML in Medical Devices (MDS-G010),المبادئ التوجيهية للذكاء الاصطناعي في الأجهزة الطبية,2024,2024,N/A,ISSUES/ENFORCES,Mandatory Enforcer,Medical device manufacturers using AI/ML; healthcare AI devices,HIGHEST,https://www.sfda.gov.sa/en,SFDA 2024; Healthcare World Nov 2025,First KSA AI medical device guidance 2024 (no predecessor),"KSA's first AI/ML medical device regulatory guidance. Key provisions: predetermined change control plan for adaptive algorithms; transparency requirements for AI decision-making in diagnosis; risk-based approach (high-risk: AI for cancer screening, sepsis detection, ICU monitoring); post-market performance monitoring for AI models; labelling requirements for AI-enabled devices; clinical validation requirements specific to Saudi patient populations. Aligns with FDA AI/ML Action Plan and EU AI Act healthcare provisions",Issued: 2024,Expected updates 2025-2026 as regulatory science matures,"CURRENT — 2024, first version",Device registration refusal; market withdrawal of non-compliant AI devices; criminal liability if patient harm results,SFDA — Medical Devices Sector (AI/Digital Health Sub-unit); SFDA post-market surveillance,Medical Device Manufacturer (AI devices); Hospital (deploying AI diagnostics); Health IT Vendor (SaMD/AI algorithms); Radiology; Pathology,Both,Pre-market AI/ML review (integrated with MDR); post-market AI performance monitoring,Pre-market: before deployment. Post-market: continuous algorithm monitoring,18 (AI-enabled IoMT: algorithm validation on diverse populations; real-world performance monitoring; adaptive algorithm change control; AI transparency for clinical users; cybersecurity of AI inference engines in connected devices),FDA AI/ML Action Plan (Feb 2021); EU AI Act (high-risk AI in medical devices); IMDRF AI/ML SaMD working group; ISO/IEC JTC 1/SC 42 (AI standards),Arabic + English (guidance document bilingual),Layer 2: Risk Management; Layer 3: Technical Controls; Layer 4: Compliance,Risk tier classification (not maturity levels): Tier I/II/III by clinical risk,SDAIA AI Strategy; HSTP AI-enabled diagnostics; SFDA 2030 Digital Health Regulatory Roadmap,Algorithm validation report; clinical evidence study; Predetermined Change Control Plan; real-world performance monitoring data; labelling compliance; cybersecurity assessment,Algorithmic accountability theory (Diakopoulos 2016): transparency and explainability for high-stakes AI. Regulatory theory for emerging tech: iterative governance (sandbox approach). Medical AI safety theory: validation vs real-world performance divergence.,FOR: First-mover advantage in GCC region for AI device regulation; aligns with FDA AI/ML Action Plan and EU AI Act direction; Topol (2019) argues clinical AI requires national regulatory clarity to enable safe adoption. AGAINST: Guidance issued 2024 but no enforcement date confirmed — Parikh et al. (2019) document regulatory ambiguity as the primary barrier to clinical AI deployment; black-box AI in diagnostics conflicts with explainability requirements — WHO (2021) AI ethics in health highlights tension; small hospitals cannot validate AI devices on Saudi patient populations alone,Non-binding guidance only — no mandatory pre-market AI review yet; no SFDA AI/ML device registry; Saudi patient population representativeness in global AI training data poorly addressed; limited SFDA technical expertise in AI/ML evaluation documented; no clinical trial requirement specific to AI performance in Saudi clinical settings,SFDA MDS-G010 vs FDA AI/ML SaMD Action Plan (2021): FDA has iterative change protocol; SFDA guidance analogous but less detailed. vs EU AI Act (2024): EU AI Act classifies medical AI as high-risk with mandatory conformity assessment — SFDA guidance voluntary. vs MHRA AI guidance (UK): UK AI/ML SaMD multi-stakeholder framework more advanced. KSA is behind FDA/EU but ahead of most GCC peers,Topol EJ (2019) 'High-Performance Medicine' Nature Medicine 25(1); Parikh RB et al. (2019) 'Regulation of Predictive Algorithms' JAMA; WHO (2021) Ethics and Governance of AI for Health; SFDA (2024) MDS-G010 AI/ML Medical Devices; FDA (2021) AI/ML-Based SaMD Action Plan,Official: SFDA MDS-G010 document (sfda.gov.sa). Training: Stanford AI in Medicine (Coursera); AI4Health (WHO-ITU); RAPS AI in regulatory affairs course; AAMI AI in medical devices webinar series. Estimated: 50 hours,"GH-ICGA Layer 2 (Risk Management) — AI-enabled IoMT devices require specific risk governance that both SFDA-MDR and NCA-ECC miss. GH-ICGA thesis positions AI medical device governance as a cross-layer challenge spanning all 5 layers: governance (L1), AI risk assessment (L2), algorithm security (L3), regulatory compliance (L4), AI assurance (L5)",R + C (risk-based AI device governance + compliance),CEO: accountable for AI device compliance. MDO (Medical Device Officer): AI device registration. AI Safety Officer (new role — AI devices): responsible for algorithm monitoring. Clinical users: mandatory training on AI device limitations,Patient Safety Risk (AI misdiagnosis); Algorithmic Risk; Regulatory Risk; Reputational Risk (AI failure); Supply Chain Risk (AI model provenance),GUIDELINE (2024) — enforcement timeline pending. Expected to become MANDATORY requirement for AI device registration by 2025-2026. Pre-market AI review by SFDA will be required for Class II+,Technical (T): algorithm validation; change control; monitoring; cybersecurity. Administrative (A): transparency documentation; clinical evidence dossier; labelling. Operational (O): post-market performance monitoring; clinician training; incident reporting,1. # AI devices with completed pre-market cybersecurity assessment. 2. Algorithm performance drift detection rate. 3. # AI-related adverse events reported to SFDA. 4. % AI devices with Predetermined Change Control Plan. 5. Clinician AI training completion rate,Pre-market: algorithm validation on diverse datasets (including Saudi patients); cybersecurity testing; safety/risk assessment (ISO 14971). Post-market: real-world performance monitoring; drift detection; adverse event analysis,SFDA e-portal Estshary (device registration). AI monitoring: WhyLabs; Evidently AI; Fiddler AI (algorithm monitoring). Quality: Greenlight Guru AI module; Qualio. Cybersecurity for AI: MedCrypt,AI model developers: must provide algorithm documentation; training data provenance; bias assessment. Cloud AI inference providers: data residency compliance; model security; audit logs. CROs for AI clinical validation: ICH E6 GCP + AI-specific validation protocol,Non-compliant AI device: SFDA market withdrawal; patient harm liability; criminal prosecution if negligent. Regulatory lag: AI devices deployed before guidance formalisation risk retroactive enforcement action. Reputational: AI-caused clinical error = significant media and regulatory scrutiny,Healthcare & Life Sciences; Industry & Trade (AI device manufacturers),Sector-Specific,,,,,
42,SFDA,Saudi Food & Drug Authority,2. Healthcare & Life Sciences,SFDA-GMP,Good Manufacturing Practice,ممارسات التصنيع الجيدة,Updated 2021,2005,68,ISSUES/ENFORCES,Mandatory Enforcer,Drug and medical device manufacturers,HIGH,https://www.sfda.gov.sa/en,SFDA official,Based on PIC/S GMP guide; KSA-specific updates 2021; ISO 13485 cross-reference,"Aligned with PIC/S PE 009 Guide to GMP. Major elements: Quality Management System, personnel qualification, premises/equipment, documentation, production, quality control, contract manufacturing, complaints/recall. 2021 KSA update: Arabic GMP inspection forms; Saudi-specific requirements for halal APIs and excipients; enhanced requirements for biologics and ATMPs",Based on PIC/S GMP (continuously updated). KSA update: 2021,Updated with PIC/S revisions; next expected 2024-2025,CURRENT — 2021 KSA version,Penalties defined by issuing authority; consult official source. NCA ECC-2 penalties apply if relevant,Issuing authority and/or designated inspection department,As specified in framework scope — consult official document,Both (unless specified otherwise in official document),As defined by issuing authority,As defined by issuing authority,To be determined from framework text — see official source,See framework preamble for international standard references,Arabic (primary in KSA); English may be available,Mapped to relevant GH-ICGA layer — requires manual assessment,See official framework for maturity model details,Aligned with relevant Vision 2030 programme — see framework context,As specified in framework compliance requirements,Institutional theory and regulatory governance theory underpin all KSA regulatory frameworks. Specific theoretical basis varies by framework — consult academic literature via Google Scholar using framework name as search term.,"Academic debate varies by framework maturity and international adoption. For KSA-specific debate, consult: Almohammad & Wills (2021) J Cybersecurity; Al-Khater et al. (2020) IEEE Access; Baker McKenzie KSA regulatory updates.",Known criticisms include: implementation gap between standards and practice; resource constraints for smaller healthcare entities; rapid technology evolution outpacing regulation. Consult framework evaluation literature for specifics.,"Framework comparison requires contextual analysis. Key comparators: NIST CSF 2.0 (international benchmark), ISO 27001 (certifiable standard), ECC-2 (KSA mandatory baseline). See main database for cross-framework mapping.","Key foundational references: Almohammad A & Wills G (2021) J Cybersecurity; NIST (2024) CSF 2.0; ISO (2022) 27001; NCA official documents (nca.gov.sa); Baker McKenzie Global Data & Cyber Handbook 2025. Consult SFDA, MOH, and NCA websites for framework-specific official citations.","Official: Consult the official source URL in the database for the primary document. Training: ISACA, ISC2, RAPS, IAPP, and HIMSS offer professional development covering most regulatory domains. Estimated hours vary by framework complexity.",Relevance to GH-ICGA framework varies by layer — see GH-ICGA Layer Mapping column. All KSA mandatory frameworks are compliance inputs to GH-ICGA Layer 4. Frameworks with IoMT-specific provisions directly inform Layers 2 and 3.,G + C (Governance + Compliance — standard regulatory framework positioning),Senior management: accountability for framework compliance. Designated compliance officer: monitor requirements. All staff: awareness of applicable obligations,Regulatory Risk; Operational Risk; Reputational Risk — specific domains determined by framework scope,"See official source — legal obligation, regulatory requirement, or voluntary standard as applicable",Administrative; Technical; Operational — specific distribution determined by framework content,1. Compliance status (compliant/non-compliant). 2. # open findings from last assessment. 3. % controls implemented. 4. Time to remediate findings. 5. Incident rate in framework scope area,Self-assessment; internal audit; external assessment as specified by issuing authority,ServiceNow GRC; IBM OpenPages; RSA Archer; MetricStream — all major GRC platforms support custom framework import. Contact vendor for framework-specific module,Third parties in scope must demonstrate equivalent compliance. Contractual compliance obligations; right-to-audit clauses; annual third-party assessment,Non-compliance: regulatory action by issuing authority; fines and penalties as specified; operational and reputational consequences,Healthcare & Life Sciences; Industry & Trade,Sector-Specific,,,,,
43,SFDA,Saudi Food & Drug Authority,2. Healthcare & Life Sciences,SFDA-PMS,Post-Market Surveillance Requirements,متطلبات مراقبة ما بعد التسويق,2022,2022,25,ISSUES/ENFORCES,Mandatory Enforcer,All medical device market authorisation holders and facilities,HIGHEST,https://www.sfda.gov.sa/en,SFDA official,First comprehensive PMS regulation 2022,38 control obligations covering: Vigilance reporting timelines (serious incidents: 48 hours; non-serious: 10 days to SFDA); Field Safety Corrective Actions (FSCA) notification; periodic safety update reports; post-market clinical follow-up requirements; trend analysis obligations. IoMT application: connected medical device cyber incidents classified as device safety incidents requiring PMS reporting if patient harm potential exists,Issued: 2022,Review 2025,CURRENT — 2022,Penalties defined by issuing authority; consult official source. NCA ECC-2 penalties apply if relevant,Issuing authority and/or designated inspection department,As specified in framework scope — consult official document,Both (unless specified otherwise in official document),As defined by issuing authority,As defined by issuing authority,To be determined from framework text — see official source,See framework preamble for international standard references,Arabic (primary in KSA); English may be available,Mapped to relevant GH-ICGA layer — requires manual assessment,See official framework for maturity model details,Aligned with relevant Vision 2030 programme — see framework context,As specified in framework compliance requirements,Post-market surveillance theory (WHO framework): ongoing safety monitoring after product approval. Signal detection theory: pharmacovigilance and medical device vigilance as statistical signal detection from adverse event reports. Regulatory learning theory: PMS data feeds back into pre-market requirements.,FOR: SFDA PMS aligns with IMDRF post-market surveillance guidelines; mandatory adverse event reporting creates national safety database; explicit link between PMS findings and device re-classification decisions. AGAINST: PMS reporting compliance in KSA healthcare facilities remains below international benchmarks; no published aggregate PMS statistics to assess system effectiveness.,Criticisms: (1) Adverse event under-reporting is the primary weakness — estimated <10% of medical device incidents reported to SFDA; (2) No electronic PMS reporting system — still largely paper/email-based; (3) PMS for IoMT/SaMD devices lacks specific guidance — how do you conduct vigilance for AI diagnostic software? (4) No integration between SFDA PMS and MOH Patient Safety reporting — duplicate reporting creates gaps.,"Framework comparison requires contextual analysis. Key comparators: NIST CSF 2.0 (international benchmark), ISO 27001 (certifiable standard), ECC-2 (KSA mandatory baseline). See main database for cross-framework mapping.",WHO Post-Market Surveillance Framework (2023); IMDRF GRRP Guidance (2022); SFDA PMS Guidelines (2023); Al-Shaikh (2024) Medical Device Vigilance in KSA.,"Official: Consult the official source URL in the database for the primary document. Training: ISACA, ISC2, RAPS, IAPP, and HIMSS offer professional development covering most regulatory domains. Estimated hours vary by framework complexity.","GH-ICGA positions SFDA-PMS as the POST-MARKET SAFETY layer. The thesis identifies SFDA PMS ↔ MOH Patient Safety reporting gap as a critical governance failure for IoMT — an AI diagnostic misdiagnosis is simultaneously a medical device incident (SFDA) and a patient safety event (MOH) but reported through separate, unlinked systems.",G + C (Governance + Compliance — standard regulatory framework positioning),Senior management: accountability for framework compliance. Designated compliance officer: monitor requirements. All staff: awareness of applicable obligations,Regulatory Risk; Operational Risk; Reputational Risk — specific domains determined by framework scope,"See official source — legal obligation, regulatory requirement, or voluntary standard as applicable",Administrative; Technical; Operational — specific distribution determined by framework content,1. Compliance status (compliant/non-compliant). 2. # open findings from last assessment. 3. % controls implemented. 4. Time to remediate findings. 5. Incident rate in framework scope area,Self-assessment; internal audit; external assessment as specified by issuing authority,ServiceNow GRC; IBM OpenPages; RSA Archer; MetricStream — all major GRC platforms support custom framework import. Contact vendor for framework-specific module,Third parties in scope must demonstrate equivalent compliance. Contractual compliance obligations; right-to-audit clauses; annual third-party assessment,Non-compliance: regulatory action by issuing authority; fines and penalties as specified; operational and reputational consequences,Healthcare & Life Sciences,Sector-Specific,,,,,
