gulation. Consult framework evaluation literature for specifics.,"Framework comparison requires contextual analysis. Key comparators: NIST CSF 2.0 (international benchmark), ISO 27001 (certifiable standard), ECC-2 (KSA mandatory baseline). See main database for cross-framework mapping.","Key foundational references: Almohammad A & Wills G (2021) J Cybersecurity; NIST (2024) CSF 2.0; ISO (2022) 27001; NCA official documents (nca.gov.sa); Baker McKenzie Global Data & Cyber Handbook 2025. Consult SFDA, MOH, and NCA websites for framework-specific official citations.","Official: Consult the official source URL in the database for the primary document. Training: ISACA, ISC2, RAPS, IAPP, and HIMSS offer professional development covering most regulatory domains. Estimated hours vary by framework complexity.",Relevance to GH-ICGA framework varies by layer — see GH-ICGA Layer Mapping column. All KSA mandatory frameworks are compliance inputs to GH-ICGA Layer 4. Frameworks with IoMT-specific provisions directly inform Layers 2 and 3.,G + C (Governance + Compliance — standard regulatory framework positioning),Senior management: accountability for framework compliance. Designated compliance officer: monitor requirements. All staff: awareness of applicable obligations,Regulatory Risk; Operational Risk; Reputational Risk — specific domains determined by framework scope,"See official source — legal obligation, regulatory requirement, or voluntary standard as applicable",Administrative; Technical; Operational — specific distribution determined by framework content,1. Compliance status (compliant/non-compliant). 2. # open findings from last assessment. 3. % controls implemented. 4. Time to remediate findings. 5. Incident rate in framework scope area,Self-assessment; internal audit; external assessment as specified by issuing authority,ServiceNow GRC; IBM OpenPages; RSA Archer; MetricStream — all major GRC platforms support custom framework import. Contact vendor for framework-specific module,Third parties in scope must demonstrate equivalent compliance. Contractual compliance obligations; right-to-audit clauses; annual third-party assessment,Non-compliance: regulatory action by issuing authority; fines and penalties as specified; operational and reputational consequences,Sector-specific — consult official source,Sector-Specific,,,,,
48,SFDA,Saudi Food & Drug Authority,2. Healthcare & Life Sciences,NCA-ECC-2:2024,Essential Cybersecurity Controls,ضوابط الأمن السيبراني الأساسية,ECC-2:2024,2018,108,MUST COMPLY WITH,Mandatory Complier,SFDA as government entity,HIGHEST,https://nca.gov.sa/,NCA ECC scope,See official source for version history,Framework issued by the relevant authority. Version details available on the official website referenced in the source URL column. The framework has been updated periodically since first issuance.,See official source,Updated as needed by the issuing authority,CURRENT — consult official source for latest version,Penalties defined by issuing authority; consult official source. NCA ECC-2 penalties apply if relevant,Issuing authority and/or designated inspection department,As specified in framework scope — consult official document,Both (unless specified otherwise in official document),As defined by issuing authority,As defined by issuing authority,To be determined from framework text — see official source,See framework preamble for international standard references,Arabic (primary in KSA); English may be available,Mapped to relevant GH-ICGA layer — requires manual assessment,See official framework for maturity model details,Aligned with relevant Vision 2030 programme — see framework context,As specified in framework compliance requirements,"Multi-agency compliance theory: SFDA requiring its regulated entities (medical device manufacturers, importers, distributors) to comply with NCA ECC-2 demonstrates cross-sector regulatory coordination in product safety. Product lifecycle security: cybersecurity as integral to medical device safety throughout lifecycle.","FOR: SFDA mandating ECC-2 for regulated entities ensures that medical device supply chain participants maintain cybersecurity baseline — prevents supply chain attacks compromising device integrity. AGAINST: Medical device manufacturers are primarily product safety companies, not IT organisations — imposing 108 ECC-2 IT controls on a device manufacturer is disproportionate to their actual IT footprint.","Criticisms: (1) Small medical device importers may have 5-10 employees — full ECC-2 compliance is disproportionate; (2) ECC-2 is designed for IT environments, not manufacturing/QMS environments; (3) Creates confusion between ECC-2 (SFDA mandate for entity IT) and SFDA MDR cybersecurity requirements (for the device itself) — two different scopes.","Framework comparison requires contextual analysis. Key comparators: NIST CSF 2.0 (international benchmark), ISO 27001 (certifiable standard), ECC-2 (KSA mandatory baseline). See main database for cross-framework mapping.",SFDA-NCA Regulatory Coordination (2024); FDA Premarket Cybersecurity Guidance (2023); EU MDR Annex I Cybersecurity Requirements; IMDRF Cybersecurity WG (2024).,"Official: Consult the official source URL in the database for the primary document. Training: ISACA, ISC2, RAPS, IAPP, and HIMSS offer professional development covering most regulatory domains. Estimated hours vary by framework complexity.",Relevance to GH-ICGA framework varies by layer — see GH-ICGA Layer Mapping column. All KSA mandatory frameworks are compliance inputs to GH-ICGA Layer 4. Frameworks with IoMT-specific provisions directly inform Layers 2 and 3.,G + C (Governance + Compliance — standard regulatory framework positioning),Senior management: accountability for framework compliance. Designated compliance officer: monitor requirements. All staff: awareness of applicable obligations,Regulatory Risk; Operational Risk; Reputational Risk — specific domains determined by framework scope,"See official source — legal obligation, regulatory requirement, or voluntary standard as applicable",Administrative; Technical; Operational — specific distribution determined by framework content,1. Compliance status (compliant/non-compliant). 2. # open findings from last assessment. 3. % controls implemented. 4. Time to remediate findings. 5. Incident rate in framework scope area,Self-assessment; internal audit; external assessment as specified by issuing authority,ServiceNow GRC; IBM OpenPages; RSA Archer; MetricStream — all major GRC platforms support custom framework import. Contact vendor for framework-specific module,Third parties in scope must demonstrate equivalent compliance. Contractual compliance obligations; right-to-audit clauses; annual third-party assessment,Non-compliance: regulatory action by issuing authority; fines and penalties as specified; operational and reputational consequences,ALL 17 SECTORS,YES — ALL SECTORS,,,,,
49,SFDA,Saudi Food & Drug Authority,2. Healthcare & Life Sciences,PDPL-2021,Personal Data Protection Law,نظام حماية البيانات الشخصية,2021+IR 2023,2021,45,MUST COMPLY WITH,Mandatory Complier,SFDA processes clinical trial and device registration data,HIGH,https://sdaia.gov.sa/,SDAIA,Royal Decree M/19 (16 Sep 2021) → amended Apr 2023,"Original (2021): 19 articles covering consent, data subject rights, controller obligations, sensitive data (health = sensitive), cross-border transfer. Apr 2023 amendment: strengthened enforcement, clarified health data provisions, aligned with PDPL-IR-2023. Grace period for compliance ended Sep 14, 2024. Penalties: up to SAR 5 million fine + 2 years imprisonment for sensitive data breach","Issued: Sep 2021. Amended: Apr 2023. Fully enforceable: Sep 14, 2024",SDAIA reviews annually; amendments expected 2025-2026,CURRENT — fully enforceable since Sep 2024,Criminal offence: up to 2 years imprisonment + SAR 3M fine for disclosing sensitive data (health data = sensitive); administrative fine up to SAR 5M; mandatory breach notification to SDAIA within 72 hours; data subject compensation claims,SDAIA — Data Protection Authority (DPA functions); NDMO for government entities; courts for criminal penalties,Hospital; Clinic; Lab; Pharmacy; Health IT Vendor; Health Insurer; Government Health Entity; Medical Device Manufacturer (all processing patient data),Both + foreign entities processing data of KSA residents,SDAIA audit; controller self-assessment; DPO oversight; data subject complaints trigger investigation,Continuous (breach notification: 72 hours); annual DPO report; SDAIA random audits,12 (IoMT data minimisation; purpose limitation; consent management for wearable data; automated decision-making; data subject rights for IoMT-generated health data; cross-border transfer of IoMT data),GDPR (substantial alignment — adequate protection basis); APEC Privacy Framework; ISO 29100; ISO 27701,Arabic (official); English translation (unofficial),Layer 4: Compliance; Layer 2: Risk Management,No formal maturity model; SDAIA publishes compliance guidance,National Data Strategy; Vision 2030 Digital Economy; Digital Government transformation,Privacy notices; consent records; DPO appointment letter; data mapping/flow diagrams; breach notification records; DPIA for health data processing; controller registration,Privacy as fundamental right (Warren & Brandeis 1890; Westin 1967). Contextual integrity theory (Nissenbaum 2004): health data flows must match medical context norms. Regulatory theory: data protection authority independence and enforcement. Information asymmetry theory: patients cannot protect their own data without regulation.,"FOR: Long-overdue — KSA had no comprehensive data protection law until 2021; health data explicitly protected as sensitive category; aligns KSA with global adequacy standards. AGAINST: Alrawabdeh (2014) and Aldawood & Skinner (2019) documented Saudi privacy culture gap — regulation without public awareness is ineffective; 'GDPR-lite' criticism: exemptions broader than GDPR, enforcement weaker; health data provisions in IR still less specific than UK GDPR Schedule 1 conditions",No independent supervisory authority (SDAIA is both promoter and regulator — conflict of interest noted by Baker McKenzie 2025); patient data portability rights weaker than GDPR Article 20; no explicit right to explanation for AI-automated health decisions; cross-border transfer rules changed twice in 12 months (regulatory instability); small healthcare providers lack capacity for DPO requirements,"PDPL vs GDPR: PDPL penalty lower (SAR 5M vs €20M); PDPL broader government exemptions; GDPR has independent DPA, PDPL does not. PDPL vs HIPAA: PDPL = all personal data (broader); HIPAA = US-specific health data only. PDPL vs UK GDPR: PDPL misses explicit Schedule 1 health data conditions. PDPL ahead of HIPAA on consent granularity; behind GDPR on enforcement independence",Baker McKenzie (2025) Saudi Arabia PDPL Global Data & Cyber Handbook; Alrawabdeh W (2014) 'Internet and Social Media Challenges in Arab World' EJSR; Westin AF (1967) Privacy and Freedom NY Atheneum; Nissenbaum H (2004) 'Privacy as Contextual Integrity' Washington Law Review 79(1); CMS Law (2025) Saudi Arabia Data Protection & Cybersecurity Expert Guide Oct 2025,Official: SDAIA website (sdaia.gov.sa) — Arabic + English. Training: IAPP CIPM/CIPP/E (privacy certifications applicable); OneTrust PDPL compliance course; Baker McKenzie PDPL compliance guide. Language: Arabic primary — strong Arabic reading recommended. Estimated: 50 hours for health sector DPO competence,PRIMARY driver for GH-ICGA Layer 4 (Compliance) — PDPL is the legal foundation for patient data governance in the preliminary GH-ICGA framework. Thesis argument: GH-ICGA Layer 4 operationalises PDPL requirements into IoMT-specific controls (consent management for wearable data; purpose limitation for monitoring streams; breach response for connected device incidents),G + C (Governance + Compliance — strong compliance driver; risk implicit),Board/CEO: ultimate accountability for PDPL compliance. DPO: mandatory for large-scale health data processors — reports to highest management level. Privacy Committee: cross-functional oversight. All staff processing personal data: mandatory privacy awareness training annually,Privacy Risk (PRIMARY); Legal/Regulatory Risk; Reputational Risk; Strategic Risk (data monetisation constraints); Cross-Border Risk (data transfer restrictions),LEGAL — Royal Decree M/19 2021 + Implementing Regulations Sep 2023. Criminal: imprisonment up to 2 years + SAR 3M fine for sensitive data disclosure. Administrative: SAR 5M maximum fine. Civil: data subject compensation claims,Administrative (A): consent management; privacy notices; DPO appointment; DPIA process; breach notification procedure. Technical (T): data minimisation; pseudonymisation; access controls for personal data; encryption. Operational (O): data retention enforcement; data subject rights fulfilment process; cross-border transfer review,1. # data subject rights requests fulfilled on time (target: 100% within 30 days). 2. # data breaches notified to SDAIA within 72h (target: 100%). 3. % staff completed privacy training (target: 100%). 4. # DPIAs completed for high-risk processing activities. 5. Data retention compliance rate (% records within policy). 6. % third-party processors with DPA agreements,Annual DPIA review; data mapping audit (controller record validation); consent record audit; breach response simulation (tabletop); access control review for personal data systems; third-party processor audit; SDAIA compliance self-assessment,OneTrust (market leader for PDPL); TrustArc; Nymity; DataGrail; Varonis (data discovery); Microsoft Purview (data classification). SDAIA National Data Governance Platform for registration. Arabic-capable tools preferred,All third-party data processors must have Data Processing Agreement (DPA). Processors must demonstrate equivalent PDPL compliance. Cross-border transfers require adequacy assessment or appropriate safeguards. Sub-processor disclosure mandatory. Annual processor risk assessment required,Non-compliance: SAR 5M administrative fine; criminal prosecution for sensitive data breach; SDAIA investigation; mandatory breach public disclosure. Business disruption: SDAIA can order processing suspension. Reputational: patient trust damage. Market: EU adequate protection status depends on PDPL enforcement credibility,ALL 17 SECTORS — any entity processing personal data,YES — ALL SECTORS,,,,,
50,CBAHI,Central Board for Accreditation of HCI,2. Healthcare & Life Sciences,CBAHI-HAS-H,Hospital Accreditation Standards (5th Ed.),معايير اعتماد المستشفيات,5th Edition 2021,2008,298,ISSUES/ENFORCES,Voluntary,Hospitals — voluntary but required for MOH facility operation,HIGHEST,https://www.cbahi.gov.sa/,CBAHI official; WHO/WEF PHSSR 2025,1st Ed 2008 → 2nd Ed 2011 → 3rd Ed 2014 → 4th Ed 2017 → 5th Ed 2021,"5th Edition (2021, current): 298 standards across domains including Patient and Family Rights, Patient Assessment, Care of Patients, Patient Safety, Medication Management, Infection Prevention, Facility Management, Staff Qualifications, Quality Improvement, Governance, HIT (Health Information Technology). HIT standards require EMR, NABIDH connectivity, access control, audit trails, downtime procedures. IoMT requirements added in 5th Ed: medical device integration standards, alarm management policy. Benchmarked against JCI standards",1st Ed: 2008. 2nd: 2011. 3rd: 2014. 4th: 2017. 5th: 2021,6th Edition expected 2025-2026,CURRENT — 5th Edition 2021,Accreditation denial; accreditation suspension; accreditation withdrawal; MOH facility licensing risk (accreditation linked to licensing in practice),CBAHI — Accreditation Survey Teams (peer surveyors); MOH recognises CBAHI as national accreditor,Hospital (primary scope); Teaching Hospital; Specialist Centre,Both (voluntary but operationally required),Full survey every 3 years; midterm (focused) survey at 18 months; unannounced visits; corrective action follow-up,Full survey: 3-year cycle. Midterm: 18 months. Unannounced: possible at any time,22 (HIT standards: medical device integration; alarm management policy mandatory; device maintenance logs; remote monitoring governance; downtime procedures; device inventory; cybersecurity of clinical systems as patient safety issue),JCI (Joint Commission International) — benchmarked; ISQua (accreditation body accreditor); WHO Safe Hospitals Framework,Arabic + English (official bilingual),Layer 1: Governance; Layer 4: Compliance; Layer 5: Assurance,Yes — Met / Partially Met / Not Met / Not Applicable per standard; overall accreditation decision = scored threshold,HSTP quality transformation; Vision 2030 healthcare quality targets (JCI/CBAHI accreditation for all tertiary hospitals),Survey self-assessment; policy documentation; staff credential files; patient safety incident log; HIT system audit; quality improvement data; patient satisfaction scores,Healthcare quality theory (Donabedian 1988): structure-process-outcome triad. Accreditation theory: third-party external validation as quality assurance mechanism. Institutional isomorphism: accreditation spreads best practices through mimetic pressure. Patient safety culture theory (Reason 2000): systems-based safety vs blame culture.,FOR: Almutairi et al. (2015) and Alkhenizan & Shaw (2011) find CBAHI-accredited hospitals show measurable quality improvements; national benchmark grounded in Saudi context unlike JCI (US-centric); patient safety improvements documented post-accreditation. AGAINST: Voluntariness undermines universal coverage — only ~40% of KSA hospitals accredited (WHO PHSSR 2025); Greenfield & Braithwaite (2008) meta-analysis questions causal link between accreditation and patient outcomes; survey preparation creates compliance-oriented behaviours rather than genuine quality improvement (gaming risk),3-year survey cycle too long for rapid IoMT deployment environment; HIT standards in 5th Ed still less specific than HIMSS EMRAM Stage 6/7 requirements; cybersecurity standards within CBAHI remain minimal compared to NCA ECC-2 requirements; no dedicated IoMT governance chapter despite medical device proliferation; rural and small facility accreditation rates critically low,CBAHI vs JCI: JCI globally recognised; CBAHI nationally recognised — JCI preferred for medical tourism attracting hospitals. CBAHI vs HIMSS EMRAM: CBAHI is broad clinical; HIMSS EMRAM is digital health maturity-specific. CBAHI vs DNV: CBAHI similar survey model. CBAHI + HIMSS EMRAM combined = most comprehensive digital health quality picture for KSA hospitals,Alkhenizan A & Shaw C (2011) 'Impact of Accreditation' Ann Saudi Med 31(4); Almutairi AF et al. (2015) 'Perceptions of CBAHI Accreditation' BMC Health Services Research; Donabedian A (1988) 'Quality of Care' JAMA 260(12); Greenfield D & Braithwaite J (2008) 'Health Sector Accreditation Research' Int J Quality Health Care 20(3); WHO (2025) PHSSR Saudi Arabia,Official: CBAHI website (cbahi.gov.sa) — standards available to purchase. Training: CBAHI education programmes; surveyor training; CPHQ (Certified Professional in Healthcare Quality). Estimated: 60 hours for surveyors; 20 hours for department leads,GH-ICGA Layer 5 (Assurance) — CBAHI accreditation is the primary assurance mechanism for hospital IoMT governance. Thesis argument: CBAHI-HAS-H 5th Ed HIT standards need a cybersecurity annex for IoMT — GH-ICGA Layer 5 Assurance proposes an IoMT-specific evaluation framework that could be adopted within CBAHI survey process,G + C (Governance through quality standards + voluntary compliance assurance),CEO: accountable for accreditation status. CMO: responsible for clinical quality chapter compliance. CNO: responsible for nursing quality. Quality/Patient Safety Director: coordinates accreditation programme. Department heads: responsible for chapter compliance in their domain. Accreditation Committee: cross-functional governance body,Quality Risk; Patient Safety Risk; Operational Risk (accreditation loss = licensing risk); Reputational Risk; Regulatory Risk (MOH links accreditation to licensing in practice),VOLUNTARY — but operationally MANDATORY (MOH licensing increasingly linked to CBAHI in practice; required for JCI-referencing hospitals and private insurance network participation),Administrative (A): 150 controls — policies; procedures; governance; quality committees. Technical (T): 48 controls — HIT; equipment maintenance; diagnostic systems. Operational (O): 100 controls — clinical procedures; patient safety practices; staff competencies,"1. CBAHI survey score per chapter (target ≥90% Met). 2. # sentinel events per 1,000 admissions. 3. Falls rate per 1,000 patient days. 4. Hospital-acquired infection rate. 5. Patient satisfaction score (target >90%). 6. Medication error rate. 7. HIT compliance score (CBAHI HIT chapter)",CBAHI full survey (3-year cycle — 2-3 day on-site assessment); midterm survey (18 months — focused chapters); unannounced visit (possible any time); tracer methodology; document review; staff interviews; facility inspection,Accreditation Manager (CBAHI online platform); EQUIP (quality management platform); PowerDMS; Quantros; Press Ganey (patient satisfaction); Meditech/Epic quality modules; RL6 (incident reporting),"Contractors and service providers in clinical areas must meet CBAHI competency requirements; credentialing of contracted physicians required; outsourced services (laundry, catering, security) must meet CBAHI environment chapter standards; medical device vendors: CBAHI biomedical equipment requirements",Accreditation denial: cannot be MOH referral centre; excluded from major insurance networks; reputational damage. Accreditation suspension: immediate revenue impact from insurance exclusion. MOH can condition facility licence on CBAHI compliance,Healthcare & Life Sciences,Sector-Specific,,,,,
