56,CBAHI,Central Board for Accreditation of HCI,2. Healthcare & Life Sciences,MOH-HIS,Health Information System Standards,معايير نظم المعلومات الصحية,2024 update,2010,112,MUST COMPLY WITH,Mandatory Complier,CBAHI-accredited facilities must comply with MOH HIS,HIGH,https://www.moh.gov.sa/en/,MOH standards cross-reference,First issued ~2010 → comprehensive update 2022 → mandatory EMR update 2024,"Progressive standards covering: 2010 (basic EHR requirements); 2015 (interoperability standards added); 2018 (NABIDH integration); 2020 (mobile health and telemedicine modules); 2022 (comprehensive 112-control update covering clinical systems, imaging, medication management, emergency, genomics, training); 2024 (mandatory EMR adoption for all MOH and private facilities aligned with NABIDH)",First: 2010. Major update: 2022. EMR mandate: 2024,Updated with NABIDH platform releases; typically annual minor updates,CURRENT — 2024 version mandatory,MOH facility licensing at risk; mandatory corrective action plan; financial penalties under Private Health Institutions Law; non-compliant systems barred from NABIDH connectivity,MOH — Directorate of Health Information (DHI); regional health cluster inspection teams; NHIC for interoperability compliance,Hospital; Clinic; Lab; Pharmacy (hospital); Government Health Entity,Both (mandatory for MOH facilities; mandatory for private facilities since 2024 PHILAW update),MOH annual IT audit; NHIC interoperability assessment; CBAHI HIT standards review during accreditation,Annual MOH audit; NABIDH connectivity continuous monitoring,28 (medical device integration into HIS; alarm management; device data capture; IoMT workflow integration; remote monitoring data ingestion; device incident reporting to HIS),HL7 FHIR R4 (interoperability); IHE profiles (integration); ISO 27799:2016 (health ISMS); DICOM (imaging),Arabic (primary); English technical annexes,Layer 1: Governance; Layer 3: Technical Controls; Layer 4: Compliance,Yes — 4 implementation levels (Basic / Intermediate / Advanced / Optimised); MOH tracks facility scores,HSTP digital health; NABIDH national EHR; Vision 2030 healthcare quality targets,HIS system documentation; NABIDH connectivity certificate; interoperability test results; staff training records; system access audit logs; downtime contingency procedure,Health informatics systems theory (Shortliffe & Cimino 2006): HIS as sociotechnical infrastructure. Interoperability theory (Kuperman 2011): semantic/syntactic/organisational interoperability levels. Technology adoption theory (TAM — Davis 1989): clinician adoption barriers to HIS.,FOR: National standardisation essential for patient safety continuity (WHO 2019); NABIDH integration creates longitudinal patient record unavailable previously; IoMT data integration requirements push hospitals toward modern system architectures. AGAINST: Ajami & Bagheri-Tadi (2013) and Aloudah et al. (2021) document implementation-reality gap in Saudi HIS — standards exist but compliance varies widely by facility tier; urban-rural HIS capacity gap poorly addressed; cybersecurity requirements within HIS standards historically weak (pre-NABIDH),112 controls create compliance burden without proportionality guidance; small private clinics face disproportionate compliance cost; no formal HIS certification programme — self-declaration accepted; IoMT data standards not harmonised with international HL7 FHIR profiles; historical emphasis on data capture over data quality,MOH-HIS vs NHS Digital standards (UK): NHS has legally binding DCB standards with enforcement; MOH-HIS is ministerial directive with softer enforcement. MOH-HIS vs ONC Health IT Certification (US): US has federal certification; KSA moving toward certification (NABIDH conformance). MOH-HIS + NHIC-NABIDH = closest to ONC approach in KSA,Ajami S & Bagheri-Tadi T (2013) 'Barriers for Adopting Electronic Health Records' MateriaSociomed 25(3); Aloudah N et al. (2021) 'Digital Health in Saudi Arabia' Sustainability 13(12); Shortliffe EH & Cimino JJ (2006) Biomedical Informatics Springer; WHO (2019) Classification of Digital Interventions; Alotaibi YK & Federico F (2017) 'Impact of Health IT on Patient Safety' Saudi Medical J 38(12),Official: MOH website (moh.gov.sa). Training: CPHIMS (Certified Professional Health Informatics & Information Management); HL7 FHIR Foundational (HL7.org); NHIC developer portal training (nhic.gov.sa). Language: Arabic primary. Estimated: 45 hours,GH-ICGA Layer 1 (Governance) + Layer 3 (Technical Controls) — MOH-HIS is the clinical data governance backbone that the preliminary GH-ICGA IoMT framework must integrate with. Thesis: GH-ICGA IoMT data model must align with HIS data standards to ensure IoMT device data flows into the national health record coherently,G + R + C (all three — clinical information governance foundation),Hospital CEO: accountable for HIS compliance. CIO/CMIO: responsible for HIS implementation and NABIDH connectivity. Clinical Informatics Committee: govern HIS standards adoption. Department Heads: responsible for clinical data quality in their systems,Operational Risk (HIS downtime = clinical risk); Patient Safety Risk; Data Quality Risk; Regulatory Risk (MOH non-compliance); Interoperability Risk (NABIDH disconnection),REGULATORY — MOH ministerial directive. Mandatory for all licensed facilities. Non-compliance risks licensing. Enforced through MOH annual audit and NABIDH connectivity monitoring,Technical (T): 68 controls — system architecture; integration; security; availability; backup. Administrative (A): 28 controls — data governance; policies; training; documentation. Operational (O): 16 controls — downtime procedures; change management; vendor management,1. NABIDH connectivity status (connected/disconnected). 2. HIS downtime incidents per month (target <4h/month). 3. Data quality score (completeness/accuracy) from NABIDH reports. 4. % clinical staff trained on HIS (target 100%). 5. Time-to-restore HIS after failure (target <4h). 6. # HIS security incidents per quarter,Annual MOH HIS audit (documentation + system walk-through); NABIDH API conformance test (NHIC automated); data quality audit; HIS security assessment; downtime procedure simulation; user access review,Epic; Cerner (Oracle Health); Philips Tasy EMR; Meditech; InterSystems HealthShare — all NABIDH-integrated. For compliance: ServiceNow Healthcare; Qualio (quality management); NHIC developer portal for FHIR conformance testing,HIS vendors: must be MOH-approved/NABIDH-certified; contractual data protection obligations; source code escrow for critical systems; SLA for 99.9% uptime; mandatory SFDA notification if HIS failure affects medical devices; annual security assessment of HIS vendors,"Non-compliance: MOH corrective action; facility licence conditions; NABIDH disconnection (loss of referrals, national patient record access); PHILAW 2024 penalties for private facilities. Clinical impact: patient safety risk from poor data quality or HIS unavailability",Healthcare & Life Sciences,Sector-Specific,,,,,
57,SCFHS,Saudi Commission for Health Specialties,2. Healthcare & Life Sciences,SCFHS-PLF,Professional Licensing Framework,إطار الترخيص المهني,Updated 2024,1992,N/A,ISSUES/ENFORCES,Mandatory Enforcer,All healthcare professionals practising in KSA,HIGH,https://scfhs.org.sa/en,SCFHS official; Devex 2025,Established 1992 → national revalidation framework launched 2024,1992 (establishment): basic licensing for physicians. Progressive expansion to all health professions. 2015: online licensing portal. 2018: expanded to allied health. 2024: National Healthcare Practitioners Revalidation Framework (regular revalidation every 3-5 years); digital licensing wallet launched; cybersecurity awareness included in CPD requirements; international credential recognition agreements expanded to 45 countries,Established: 1992. Revalidation framework: 2024,Annual updates; revalidation cycle ongoing,CURRENT — 2024 enhanced version,Practice without licence: criminal prosecution; fine; deportation (expats); licence revocation for professional misconduct,SCFHS — Licensing Affairs Dept; SCFHS Scrutiny Committee for misconduct; MOH regional health directorates enforce unlicensed practice,Hospital; Clinic; Lab; Pharmacy; Health IT Vendor (clinical staff); Government Health Entity,Both (all employers of healthcare professionals),Credential verification before licence; CPD audit; revalidation assessment (from 2024); employer spot check,Licence renewal: every 2-5 years by profession. Revalidation: every 3-5 years. CPD: annual,3 (digital health competency: IoMT device training; telehealth clinical certification; cybersecurity awareness CPD credits),GMC (UK) revalidation model; ACGME (US) competency framework; WHO health workforce standards,Arabic + English (bilingual licensing portal),Layer 1: Governance,No maturity model; licence tier by specialty and grade,HSTP human capital; Vision 2030 healthcare workforce Saudisation; SCFHS 2025-2030 strategy,Licence certificate; educational credentials; CPD completion records; revalidation portfolio; employer verification letter,Institutional theory and regulatory governance theory underpin all KSA regulatory frameworks. Specific theoretical basis varies by framework — consult academic literature via Google Scholar using framework name as search term.,"Academic debate varies by framework maturity and international adoption. For KSA-specific debate, consult: Almohammad & Wills (2021) J Cybersecurity; Al-Khater et al. (2020) IEEE Access; Baker McKenzie KSA regulatory updates.",Known criticisms include: implementation gap between standards and practice; resource constraints for smaller healthcare entities; rapid technology evolution outpacing regulation. Consult framework evaluation literature for specifics.,"Framework comparison requires contextual analysis. Key comparators: NIST CSF 2.0 (international benchmark), ISO 27001 (certifiable standard), ECC-2 (KSA mandatory baseline). See main database for cross-framework mapping.","Key foundational references: Almohammad A & Wills G (2021) J Cybersecurity; NIST (2024) CSF 2.0; ISO (2022) 27001; NCA official documents (nca.gov.sa); Baker McKenzie Global Data & Cyber Handbook 2025. Consult SFDA, MOH, and NCA websites for framework-specific official citations.","Official: Consult the official source URL in the database for the primary document. Training: ISACA, ISC2, RAPS, IAPP, and HIMSS offer professional development covering most regulatory domains. Estimated hours vary by framework complexity.",Relevance to GH-ICGA framework varies by layer — see GH-ICGA Layer Mapping column. All KSA mandatory frameworks are compliance inputs to GH-ICGA Layer 4. Frameworks with IoMT-specific provisions directly inform Layers 2 and 3.,G + C (workforce governance + professional compliance),Hospital CEO: ensure all clinical staff are SCFHS-licensed. Medical Director: verify clinician licences before deployment. HR Director: track licence expiry and CPD compliance. Department Heads: confirm staff qualifications,Legal Risk (unlicensed practice = criminal); Operational Risk (workforce shortage); Reputational Risk; Patient Safety Risk (unqualified practitioner),LEGAL — Health Law requires all healthcare professionals to be SCFHS-licensed. Unlicensed practice: criminal prosecution; deportation for expats; employer fine. MHRSD enforces via Qiwa portal,Administrative (A): licence verification process; CPD tracking system; credential file management; revalidation programme. Operational (O): locum supervision; temporary licence management,1. % clinical staff with current SCFHS licence (target 100%). 2. # expired licences identified (target 0). 3. CPD credit compliance rate (target 100%). 4. Time-to-verify new hire licence (target <24h via SCFHS portal). 5. % staff completed digital health CPD credits,Monthly SCFHS licence status check (via SCFHS verification portal); annual CPD record audit; Qiwa workforce compliance check; SCFHS random inspection,SCFHS Mumaris+ portal (licence verification); Qiwa platform (workforce compliance); hospital HRIS integration with SCFHS API; CPD tracking modules in Cornerstone OnDemand; Workday HR,Locum agencies: must provide SCFHS-licensed staff only; contractual warranty of licence currency; immediate notification of licence suspension; credentialing verification required before each assignment,Unlicensed practitioner: criminal prosecution of practitioner + employer; MOH investigation; facility licence risk; CCHI insurance coverage may be invalid for unlicensed clinician services; massive malpractice liability exposure,Healthcare & Life Sciences,Sector-Specific,,,,,
