63,SCFHS,Saudi Commission for Health Specialties,2. Healthcare & Life Sciences,PDPL-2021,Personal Data Protection Law,نظام حماية البيانات الشخصية,2021+IR 2023,2021,45,MUST COMPLY WITH,Mandatory Complier,SCFHS processes health professional licence data,MODERATE,https://sdaia.gov.sa/,SDAIA,Royal Decree M/19 (16 Sep 2021) → amended Apr 2023,"Original (2021): 19 articles covering consent, data subject rights, controller obligations, sensitive data (health = sensitive), cross-border transfer. Apr 2023 amendment: strengthened enforcement, clarified health data provisions, aligned with PDPL-IR-2023. Grace period for compliance ended Sep 14, 2024. Penalties: up to SAR 5 million fine + 2 years imprisonment for sensitive data breach","Issued: Sep 2021. Amended: Apr 2023. Fully enforceable: Sep 14, 2024",SDAIA reviews annually; amendments expected 2025-2026,CURRENT — fully enforceable since Sep 2024,Criminal offence: up to 2 years imprisonment + SAR 3M fine for disclosing sensitive data (health data = sensitive); administrative fine up to SAR 5M; mandatory breach notification to SDAIA within 72 hours; data subject compensation claims,SDAIA — Data Protection Authority (DPA functions); NDMO for government entities; courts for criminal penalties,Hospital; Clinic; Lab; Pharmacy; Health IT Vendor; Health Insurer; Government Health Entity; Medical Device Manufacturer (all processing patient data),Both + foreign entities processing data of KSA residents,SDAIA audit; controller self-assessment; DPO oversight; data subject complaints trigger investigation,Continuous (breach notification: 72 hours); annual DPO report; SDAIA random audits,12 (IoMT data minimisation; purpose limitation; consent management for wearable data; automated decision-making; data subject rights for IoMT-generated health data; cross-border transfer of IoMT data),GDPR (substantial alignment — adequate protection basis); APEC Privacy Framework; ISO 29100; ISO 27701,Arabic (official); English translation (unofficial),Layer 4: Compliance; Layer 2: Risk Management,No formal maturity model; SDAIA publishes compliance guidance,National Data Strategy; Vision 2030 Digital Economy; Digital Government transformation,Privacy notices; consent records; DPO appointment letter; data mapping/flow diagrams; breach notification records; DPIA for health data processing; controller registration,Privacy as fundamental right (Warren & Brandeis 1890; Westin 1967). Contextual integrity theory (Nissenbaum 2004): health data flows must match medical context norms. Regulatory theory: data protection authority independence and enforcement. Information asymmetry theory: patients cannot protect their own data without regulation.,"FOR: Long-overdue — KSA had no comprehensive data protection law until 2021; health data explicitly protected as sensitive category; aligns KSA with global adequacy standards. AGAINST: Alrawabdeh (2014) and Aldawood & Skinner (2019) documented Saudi privacy culture gap — regulation without public awareness is ineffective; 'GDPR-lite' criticism: exemptions broader than GDPR, enforcement weaker; health data provisions in IR still less specific than UK GDPR Schedule 1 conditions",No independent supervisory authority (SDAIA is both promoter and regulator — conflict of interest noted by Baker McKenzie 2025); patient data portability rights weaker than GDPR Article 20; no explicit right to explanation for AI-automated health decisions; cross-border transfer rules changed twice in 12 months (regulatory instability); small healthcare providers lack capacity for DPO requirements,"PDPL vs GDPR: PDPL penalty lower (SAR 5M vs €20M); PDPL broader government exemptions; GDPR has independent DPA, PDPL does not. PDPL vs HIPAA: PDPL = all personal data (broader); HIPAA = US-specific health data only. PDPL vs UK GDPR: PDPL misses explicit Schedule 1 health data conditions. PDPL ahead of HIPAA on consent granularity; behind GDPR on enforcement independence",Baker McKenzie (2025) Saudi Arabia PDPL Global Data & Cyber Handbook; Alrawabdeh W (2014) 'Internet and Social Media Challenges in Arab World' EJSR; Westin AF (1967) Privacy and Freedom NY Atheneum; Nissenbaum H (2004) 'Privacy as Contextual Integrity' Washington Law Review 79(1); CMS Law (2025) Saudi Arabia Data Protection & Cybersecurity Expert Guide Oct 2025,Official: SDAIA website (sdaia.gov.sa) — Arabic + English. Training: IAPP CIPM/CIPP/E (privacy certifications applicable); OneTrust PDPL compliance course; Baker McKenzie PDPL compliance guide. Language: Arabic primary — strong Arabic reading recommended. Estimated: 50 hours for health sector DPO competence,PRIMARY driver for GH-ICGA Layer 4 (Compliance) — PDPL is the legal foundation for patient data governance in the preliminary GH-ICGA framework. Thesis argument: GH-ICGA Layer 4 operationalises PDPL requirements into IoMT-specific controls (consent management for wearable data; purpose limitation for monitoring streams; breach response for connected device incidents),G + C (Governance + Compliance — strong compliance driver; risk implicit),Board/CEO: ultimate accountability for PDPL compliance. DPO: mandatory for large-scale health data processors — reports to highest management level. Privacy Committee: cross-functional oversight. All staff processing personal data: mandatory privacy awareness training annually,Privacy Risk (PRIMARY); Legal/Regulatory Risk; Reputational Risk; Strategic Risk (data monetisation constraints); Cross-Border Risk (data transfer restrictions),LEGAL — Royal Decree M/19 2021 + Implementing Regulations Sep 2023. Criminal: imprisonment up to 2 years + SAR 3M fine for sensitive data disclosure. Administrative: SAR 5M maximum fine. Civil: data subject compensation claims,Administrative (A): consent management; privacy notices; DPO appointment; DPIA process; breach notification procedure. Technical (T): data minimisation; pseudonymisation; access controls for personal data; encryption. Operational (O): data retention enforcement; data subject rights fulfilment process; cross-border transfer review,1. # data subject rights requests fulfilled on time (target: 100% within 30 days). 2. # data breaches notified to SDAIA within 72h (target: 100%). 3. % staff completed privacy training (target: 100%). 4. # DPIAs completed for high-risk processing activities. 5. Data retention compliance rate (% records within policy). 6. % third-party processors with DPA agreements,Annual DPIA review; data mapping audit (controller record validation); consent record audit; breach response simulation (tabletop); access control review for personal data systems; third-party processor audit; SDAIA compliance self-assessment,OneTrust (market leader for PDPL); TrustArc; Nymity; DataGrail; Varonis (data discovery); Microsoft Purview (data classification). SDAIA National Data Governance Platform for registration. Arabic-capable tools preferred,All third-party data processors must have Data Processing Agreement (DPA). Processors must demonstrate equivalent PDPL compliance. Cross-border transfers require adequacy assessment or appropriate safeguards. Sub-processor disclosure mandatory. Annual processor risk assessment required,Non-compliance: SAR 5M administrative fine; criminal prosecution for sensitive data breach; SDAIA investigation; mandatory breach public disclosure. Business disruption: SDAIA can order processing suspension. Reputational: patient trust damage. Market: EU adequate protection status depends on PDPL enforcement credibility,ALL 17 SECTORS — any entity processing personal data,YES — ALL SECTORS,,,,,
64,CCHI,Council of Cooperative Health Insurance,2. Healthcare & Life Sciences,CCHI-HIR,Cooperative Health Insurance Law & Regulations,نظام الضمان الصحي التعاوني,Updated 2024,1999,50,ISSUES/ENFORCES,Mandatory Enforcer,"All employers (private+expatriate), health insurers, healthcare providers",HIGH,https://cchi.gov.sa/,Hammad & Al-Mehdar 2025; CCHI official,"Royal Decree M/10 1999 → major updates 2006, 2012, 2020, 2024","1999 (establishment): mandatory health insurance for expat workers. 2006: expanded to all private sector employees. 2012: new insurance products approved. 2020: mental health coverage mandated (first time). 2024: major update — chronic disease management coverage expanded; digital health services coverage (telemedicine, remote monitoring reimbursement); mandatory e-prior authorisation; employer penalties for non-compliance strengthened; universal health insurance roadmap integration","Established: 1999. Major updates: 2006, 2012, 2020, 2024",Biennial review; NHI integration updates annually,CURRENT — 2024 version,"Employer: daily SAR 1,000 fine per uninsured employee; Insurer: licence suspension; Provider: exclusion from insurance network; CCHI enforcement actions",CCHI — Compliance and Monitoring Dept; MOL (Ministry of Labour) for employer compliance; MOH coordinates for provider network,Hospital; Clinic; Lab; Pharmacy; Health Insurer; Government Health Entity (coordination for national expansion),Private sector (employers and private health facilities/insurers primarily),CCHI employer compliance audit; insurer financial audit; provider quality audit; digital claims system audit,Annual insurer audit; employer compliance continuous (via MOL Qiwa portal); provider: biennial,8 (remote monitoring reimbursement coding; IoMT-enabled chronic disease management coverage; telehealth claim codes; digital health benefit verification; real-time eligibility checking for IoMT monitoring services),International Health Insurance Standards (WHO); EU Directive 2011/24/EU (cross-border healthcare reference); ISO 27001 for insurer data,Arabic (primary); English guidance,Layer 1: Governance; Layer 4: Compliance,No formal maturity model; licence tier classification for insurers,Universal Health Coverage (Vision 2030); National Health Insurance rollout; HSTP financing transformation,Insurance policy certificate; employer group insurance contract; UBP coverage compliance checklist; digital claims processing system documentation; CCHI audit reports,Institutional theory and regulatory governance theory underpin all KSA regulatory frameworks. Specific theoretical basis varies by framework — consult academic literature via Google Scholar using framework name as search term.,"Academic debate varies by framework maturity and international adoption. For KSA-specific debate, consult: Almohammad & Wills (2021) J Cybersecurity; Al-Khater et al. (2020) IEEE Access; Baker McKenzie KSA regulatory updates.",Known criticisms include: implementation gap between standards and practice; resource constraints for smaller healthcare entities; rapid technology evolution outpacing regulation. Consult framework evaluation literature for specifics.,"Framework comparison requires contextual analysis. Key comparators: NIST CSF 2.0 (international benchmark), ISO 27001 (certifiable standard), ECC-2 (KSA mandatory baseline). See main database for cross-framework mapping.","Key foundational references: Almohammad A & Wills G (2021) J Cybersecurity; NIST (2024) CSF 2.0; ISO (2022) 27001; NCA official documents (nca.gov.sa); Baker McKenzie Global Data & Cyber Handbook 2025. Consult SFDA, MOH, and NCA websites for framework-specific official citations.","Official: Consult the official source URL in the database for the primary document. Training: ISACA, ISC2, RAPS, IAPP, and HIMSS offer professional development covering most regulatory domains. Estimated hours vary by framework complexity.",Relevance to GH-ICGA framework varies by layer — see GH-ICGA Layer Mapping column. All KSA mandatory frameworks are compliance inputs to GH-ICGA Layer 4. Frameworks with IoMT-specific provisions directly inform Layers 2 and 3.,G + C (health insurance governance + mandatory compliance),CEO: accountable for CCHI licence compliance. Chief Actuarial Officer: UBP benefit modelling. Chief Medical Officer: clinical coverage governance. Compliance Officer: CCHI regulatory monitoring. Digital Claims Manager: DCS compliance,Regulatory Risk; Financial Risk (claims reserves); Operational Risk (claims processing); Reputational Risk; Clinical Quality Risk,"LEGAL — Cooperative Health Insurance Law (RD M/10 1999 as amended). CCHI is regulator and enforcement body. Fines: SAR 1,000/day per uninsured employee (employer); insurer: licence suspension",Administrative (A): policy issuance; claims adjudication; network management; fraud detection. Technical (T): e-prior authorisation; digital claims system; NABIDH integration. Operational (O): provider network management; complaints handling; member services,1. Claims processing time (target: 15 days clean claims). 2. Fraud detection rate (% fraudulent claims identified). 3. UBP coverage compliance rate (% mandatory benefits paid). 4. Provider network coverage (% insured members within 30 min of network facility). 5. e-prior authorisation system uptime (target >99%). 6. Member complaint resolution time,Annual CCHI compliance audit; financial examination (claims reserves); provider network adequacy audit; UBP coverage verification; e-prior authorisation system audit; fraud and abuse detection programme review,CCHI ADEEM system (mandatory for insurers — electronic claims); Oracle Health Insurance; Majesco (insurance core); Change Healthcare (claims processing). Analytics: SAS Healthcare Analytics; IBM Watson for Health Insurance. Fraud: Cotiviti; Gainwell Technologies,Healthcare providers in CCHI network: must meet CCHI quality standards; CBAHI accreditation preferred; contract compliance monitoring; fraud audit rights; data sharing for quality measurement; NABIDH connectivity requirement for network participation,Employer non-compliance: daily fine + work visa ban. Insurer non-compliance: licence suspension; market exclusion; mandatory remediation. Provider non-compliance: network exclusion; reimbursement suspension; CCHI investigation,Healthcare & Life Sciences; Financial Regulators (insurers),Sector-Specific,,,,,
65,CCHI,Council of Cooperative Health Insurance,2. Healthcare & Life Sciences,CCHI-UBP,Unified Health Benefits Package,حزمة المزايا الصحية الموحدة,2024-2025,2018,35,ISSUES/ENFORCES,Mandatory Enforcer,All health insurers and healthcare providers,HIGH,https://cchi.gov.sa/,Hammad & Al-Mehdar 2025,First UBP 2018 → expanded 2022 → major expansion 2024-2025,2018 (first): basic coverage package for expat workers. 2022: expanded to include mental health (inpatient + 12 outpatient sessions); physiotherapy. 2024-2025: major expansion — fertility treatment; hearing aids; advanced cancer therapy; telemedicine reimbursement; remote patient monitoring for chronic disease; AI-assisted diagnosis coverage (first-in-region). Unified across all insurers — providers must deliver all UBP services,"First: 2018. Updated: 2022, 2024-2025",Annual update aligned with actuarial review,CURRENT — 2024-2025 expanded version,Penalties defined by issuing authority; consult official source. NCA ECC-2 penalties apply if relevant,Issuing authority and/or designated inspection department,As specified in framework scope — consult official document,Both (unless specified otherwise in official document),As defined by issuing authority,As defined by issuing authority,To be determined from framework text — see official source,See framework preamble for international standard references,Arabic (primary in KSA); English may be available,Mapped to relevant GH-ICGA layer — requires manual assessment,See official framework for maturity model details,Aligned with relevant Vision 2030 programme — see framework context,As specified in framework compliance requirements,Institutional theory and regulatory governance theory underpin all KSA regulatory frameworks. Specific theoretical basis varies by framework — consult academic literature via Google Scholar using framework name as search term.,"Academic debate varies by framework maturity and international adoption. For KSA-specific debate, consult: Almohammad & Wills (2021) J Cybersecurity; Al-Khater et al. (2020) IEEE Access; Baker McKenzie KSA regulatory updates.",Known criticisms include: implementation gap between standards and practice; resource constraints for smaller healthcare entities; rapid technology evolution outpacing regulation. Consult framework evaluation literature for specifics.,"Framework comparison requires contextual analysis. Key comparators: NIST CSF 2.0 (international benchmark), ISO 27001 (certifiable standard), ECC-2 (KSA mandatory baseline). See main database for cross-framework mapping.","Key foundational references: Almohammad A & Wills G (2021) J Cybersecurity; NIST (2024) CSF 2.0; ISO (2022) 27001; NCA official documents (nca.gov.sa); Baker McKenzie Global Data & Cyber Handbook 2025. Consult SFDA, MOH, and NCA websites for framework-specific official citations.","Official: Consult the official source URL in the database for the primary document. Training: ISACA, ISC2, RAPS, IAPP, and HIMSS offer professional development covering most regulatory domains. Estimated hours vary by framework complexity.",Relevance to GH-ICGA framework varies by layer — see GH-ICGA Layer Mapping column. All KSA mandatory frameworks are compliance inputs to GH-ICGA Layer 4. Frameworks with IoMT-specific provisions directly inform Layers 2 and 3.,G + C (Governance + Compliance — standard regulatory framework positioning),Senior management: accountability for framework compliance. Designated compliance officer: monitor requirements. All staff: awareness of applicable obligations,Regulatory Risk; Operational Risk; Reputational Risk — specific domains determined by framework scope,"See official source — legal obligation, regulatory requirement, or voluntary standard as applicable",Administrative; Technical; Operational — specific distribution determined by framework content,1. Compliance status (compliant/non-compliant). 2. # open findings from last assessment. 3. % controls implemented. 4. Time to remediate findings. 5. Incident rate in framework scope area,Self-assessment; internal audit; external assessment as specified by issuing authority,ServiceNow GRC; IBM OpenPages; RSA Archer; MetricStream — all major GRC platforms support custom framework import. Contact vendor for framework-specific module,Third parties in scope must demonstrate equivalent compliance. Contractual compliance obligations; right-to-audit clauses; annual third-party assessment,Non-compliance: regulatory action by issuing authority; fines and penalties as specified; operational and reputational consequences,Healthcare & Life Sciences; Financial Regulators,Sector-Specific,,,,,
66,CCHI,Council of Cooperative Health Insurance,2. Healthcare & Life Sciences,CCHI-DCS,Digital Claims Processing Standards,معايير المطالبات الرقمية,2024-2025,2024,20,ISSUES/ENFORCES,Mandatory Enforcer,All health insurers and healthcare providers,MODERATE,https://cchi.gov.sa/,Hammad & Al-Mehdar 2025,Mandatory digital claims 2024-2025 (First issue),"End-to-end digital claims processing requirement. Phase 1 (2024): e-submission mandatory for major insurers (>50,000 covered lives). Phase 2 (2025): all insurers. Requirements: CCHI-standard JSON/XML claims format; 48-hour claims acknowledgement; 15-day clean claims payment; NABIDH integration for clinical data validation; real-time claims status visibility; audit trail for all claim decisions",Phase 1: 2024. Phase 2: 2025,Annual update,CURRENT — phased rollout 2024-2025,Penalties defined by issuing authority; consult official source. NCA ECC-2 penalties apply if relevant,Issuing authority and/or designated inspection department,As specified in framework scope — consult official document,Both (unless specified otherwise in official document),As defined by issuing authority,As defined by issuing authority,To be determined from framework text — see official source,See framework preamble for international standard references,Arabic (primary in KSA); English may be available,Mapped to relevant GH-ICGA layer — requires manual assessment,See official framework for maturity model details,Aligned with relevant Vision 2030 programme — see framework context,As specified in framework compliance requirements,Institutional theory and regulatory governance theory underpin all KSA regulatory frameworks. Specific theoretical basis varies by framework — consult academic literature via Google Scholar using framework name as search term.,"Academic debate varies by framework maturity and international adoption. For KSA-specific debate, consult: Almohammad & Wills (2021) J Cybersecurity; Al-Khater et al. (2020) IEEE Access; Baker McKenzie KSA 