83,SAMA,Saudi Central Bank,5. Financial Regulators,SAMA-CSF,Cybersecurity Framework,إطار الأمن السيبراني,2017,2017,114,ISSUES/ENFORCES,Mandatory Enforcer,"Banks, insurance, financing, fintechs — 5 pillars/29 objectives/114 sub-controls",MODERATE,https://www.sama.gov.sa/en-US/RulesInstructions/CyberSecurity/,SAMA official; Wattlecorp Aug 2025,Issued May 2017 (one version; periodic guidance updates),"5 cybersecurity domains: Leadership & Governance, Risk Management & Compliance, Operations & Technology, Third Party, Customer Awareness. 29 objectives, 114 sub-controls. 6 maturity levels (Initial → Optimised). Annual self-assessment required. External assessment every 3 years. SAMA inspectors conduct surprise audits. Health insurance companies are SAMA/IAIC-regulated and must comply. Aligned with NIST CSF, ISO 27001, PCI DSS",Issued: May 2017. Annual self-assessment cycle,Update expected to align with NCA ECC-2:2024 and NIST CSF 2.0,CURRENT — 2017 version; update anticipated 2025,SAMA enforcement: business closure (extreme); SAR multimillion fines (unspecified ceiling); mandatory remediation plan; licence suspension; public warning,SAMA — Supervisory Technology Department; Insurance Authority (IAIC) for insurers; annual assessment reviewed by SAMA supervisors,Health Insurer (primary — SAMA/IAIC regulated); Private Hospital (if fintech/payment services regulated),Private financial sector (SAMA-regulated entities only; SAMA itself as public entity also complies with NCA ECC),Annual self-assessment; SAMA external assessment every 3 years; SAMA surprise inspection (risk-based),Annual self-assessment; external: every 3 years,6 (health insurance systems security: claims system security; API security for health insurance billing; connected payment device security; third-party health IT vendor management),NIST CSF (primary); ISO 27001:2022; PCI DSS v4 (payment); SWIFT CSP (correspondent banking); COBIT 2019 (IT governance),Arabic (primary); English translation available,Layer 1: Governance; Layer 2: Risk Management; Layer 4: Compliance,Yes — 6 maturity levels (1=Initial to 6=Optimising); sub-domain level maturity scoring; annual self-scoring required,Financial Sector Development Programme (FSDP); Vision 2030 Fintech strategy; health insurance digital transformation,Annual self-assessment report; risk register; policy documentation; penetration test reports; BCP test evidence; staff training records; third-party vendor assessment,Financial sector cybersecurity regulation theory: systemic risk and contagion (BCBS 2018). Risk-based supervision theory: maturity-based regulatory oversight. Sector-specific regulation vs cross-sector regulation debate.,"FOR: Only KSA framework with a formal 6-level maturity model — enables progression benchmarking; annual self-assessment creates continuous improvement culture; aligns with G-7 Fundamental Elements of Cybersecurity (financial sector). AGAINST: 2017 vintage — predates cloud-native architectures, AI-driven fraud, and health insurance sector IoT expansion; Wattlecorp (2025) notes SAMA-CSF and NCA-ECC-2 overlap creates dual compliance burden for health insurers; self-assessment without mandatory external verification undermines assurance value","Issued 2017 — significantly outdated vs NCA ECC-2 (2024) — no major revision in 7 years; SAMA-CSF and NCA-ECC-2 alignment not formally documented creating uncertainty; health insurance entities must navigate SAMA-CSF, NCA-ECC-2, PDPL, and CCHI-DCS simultaneously — regulatory fragmentation; IoT/connected insurance product security not addressed","SAMA-CSF vs NCA-ECC-2: SAMA-CSF sector-specific (financial), ECC-2 cross-sector — ECC-2 now dominant for cybersecurity, SAMA-CSF supplementary. SAMA-CSF vs ECB TIBER-EU: ECB uses red-team testing; SAMA-CSF relies on self-assessment. SAMA-CSF vs PRA CBEST (UK): similar financial sector model. SAMA-CSF maturity model (6 levels) more granular than ECC-2 (5 levels)",SAMA (2017) Cybersecurity Framework sama.gov.sa; BCBS (2018) Cyber-Resilience Basel Committee; Wattlecorp (2025) SAMA Cybersecurity Framework Aug 2025; Al-Matari EM et al. (2014) 'Corporate Governance in Saudi Arabia' Asian Social Science 10(9); Convene (2025) SAMA Cybersecurity Framework Overview Aug 2025,Official: SAMA website (sama.gov.sa) — free PDF. Training: CISM (Certified Information Security Manager) — governance domain aligns; CRISC (risk management); ISACA GRC tools. Estimated: 30 hours,GH-ICGA Layer 1 (Governance) — SAMA-CSF maturity model is an important reference for GH-ICGA's own maturity assessment design. Thesis: GH-ICGA proposes adapting SAMA-CSF's 6-level maturity model for healthcare IoT governance assessment — the financial sector's approach provides a tested template for health sector adoption,G + R + C (sector-specific GRC framework — most comprehensive in GRC terms for financial sector),Board: approve cybersecurity strategy annually; review maturity scores; ensure CISO reporting line. CISO: present quarterly to board; own SAMA-CSF self-assessment. Risk Committee: integrate cyber risk into enterprise risk. Compliance: monitor SAMA regulatory requirements,Cyber Risk; Operational Risk; Third-Party Risk; Systemic Risk (financial sector contagion); Reputational Risk; Regulatory Risk,REGULATORY — SAMA mandatory circular for all regulated entities. Enforced through SAMA supervisory examinations. No published maximum fine but SAMA can: suspend licence; impose mandatory remediation; require capital buffer for cyber risk,Administrative (A): 45 controls — governance; policy; awareness; HR. Technical (T): 42 controls — access management; operations security; incident management. Physical (P): 12 controls — physical security. Operational (O): 15 controls — vendor management; BCP,1. SAMA-CSF self-assessment score per domain (target maturity level 3+ / 5 domains). 2. Maturity progression year-on-year. 3. # critical cyber incidents per year. 4. Third-party risk assessment completion rate (target 100% for critical vendors). 5. % staff passed cybersecurity awareness training. 6. Penetration test findings remediation rate,Annual SAMA-CSF self-assessment (mandatory — submitted to SAMA); SAMA supervisory examination (every 3 years for major entities); external penetration test (annual); Red team exercise (every 2-3 years for Tier 1 entities); BCP/DR test (annual),IBM OpenPages; RSA Archer; CURA Risk Management; ServiceNow GRC; MetricStream; Resolver. KSA: Maqsam (local GRC tool); FICO Advisors. SAMA-approved assessment tools,All critical third parties: SAMA-CSF compliance assessment before engagement; annual reassessment; contractual right-to-audit; exit plan mandatory; cloud providers: SAMA Cloud Policy compliance; No data sharing without due diligence; sub-outsourcing requires SAMA prior approval for material activities,Low maturity (Level 1-2 in critical domains): SAMA mandatory remediation plan + deadline; regulatory capital impact consideration; reputational risk from SAMA disclosure. Licence conditions possible. For health insurers: CCHI + SAMA dual-regulator scrutiny,Financial Regulators; Healthcare & Life Sciences (health insurers),Sector-Specific,,,,,
