99,CST,Communications Space & Technology Commission,4. ICT / Digital / Telecom,CST-IOT,IoT Regulatory Framework,الإطار التنظيمي لإنترنت الأشياء,Active,2019,N/A,ISSUES/ENFORCES,Mandatory Enforcer,IoT device manufacturers/operators — medical IoT devices,HIGHEST,https://www.cst.gov.sa/en,CMS Law Oct 2025 (IoT RF),IoT Regulatory Framework first ~2019; actively updated,IoT-specific licensing and technical standards. Covers: device type approval (spectrum compliance); data handling obligations for IoT device operators; security requirements for IoT device manufacturers (default password prohibition; firmware update mechanism required; vulnerability disclosure); IoT platform operator licensing. Healthcare application: medical IoT devices sold/deployed in KSA must obtain CST type approval in addition to SFDA registration,First: ~2019. Active updates,Under active development; medical IoT sub-framework expected 2025,CURRENT — active updates ongoing,Device market withdrawal; import ban; SAR fine under Telecom Law (up to SAR 50M for major violations); CST type approval revocation,CST — Technical Regulation Department; Spectrum Management Authority; customs coordination with ZATCA for import inspection,Medical Device Manufacturer (connected devices); Health IT Vendor (IoT platforms); Hospital (IoMT deployers); Medical Equipment Importer,"Both (manufacturers, importers, operators)",Pre-market type approval testing (mandatory before sale); operator compliance audit; market surveillance,Pre-market: before each device model launch. Post-market: continuous surveillance,"All N/A controls are IoMT-specific — full framework addresses IoT device security, type approval, network requirements",ETSI EN 303 645 (IoT cybersecurity baseline); IEC 62443-4-2; ISO/IEC 30141 (IoT Reference Architecture); ITU-T standards,Arabic (primary); English guidance,Layer 3: Technical Controls; Layer 4: Compliance,No formal maturity model; pass/fail type approval,"Vision 2030 IoT Strategy; Digital Economy Policy; Smart City initiatives (NEOM, Riyadh smart city)",CST type approval certificate; EMC test report; cybersecurity test report (ETSI EN 303 645); device technical file; operator licence,"Internet of Things governance theory: IoT regulation at the intersection of telecommunications, cybersecurity, and product safety. Standards theory: CST IoT standards as market access controls ensuring interoperability and safety. Digital market regulation theory: CST balancing innovation promotion with consumer protection.",FOR: CST IoT regulation provides communications-layer security for all connected devices including medical IoT — addresses network-level threats that NCA ECC-2 (IT-focused) and SFDA MDR (device-focused) don't cover; spectrum allocation for healthcare IoT ensures dedicated bandwidth for critical medical communications. AGAINST: CST IoT standards primarily telecommunications-focused — limited understanding of healthcare-specific IoT requirements; no explicit medical device IoT provisions.,"Criticisms: (1) CST IoT standards don't distinguish between consumer IoT and medical IoT — same regulations for smart bulbs and insulin pumps; (2) No coordination mechanism between CST (communications), NCA (cybersecurity), and SFDA (device safety) for IoMT governance; (3) CST type approval process for IoT devices doesn't include cybersecurity assessment — only RF and telecommunications compliance.","Framework comparison requires contextual analysis. Key comparators: NIST CSF 2.0 (international benchmark), ISO 27001 (certifiable standard), ECC-2 (KSA mandatory baseline). See main database for cross-framework mapping.",CST IoT Regulations (2023); ITU IoT Standards (2024); GSMA IoT Security Guidelines (2024); Al-Rasheed (2024) IoT Governance in KSA.,"Official: Consult the official source URL in the database for the primary document. Training: ISACA, ISC2, RAPS, IAPP, and HIMSS offer professional development covering most regulatory domains. Estimated hours vary by framework complexity.","GH-ICGA positions CST-IOT as the COMMUNICATIONS GOVERNANCE layer for healthcare IoT. Thesis argues CST sits at a critical junction: it controls the network layer through which all IoMT devices communicate, but has no healthcare-specific IoT provisions — a major gap in the GH-ICGA framework.",G + C (Governance + Compliance — standard regulatory framework positioning),Senior management: accountability for framework compliance. Designated compliance officer: monitor requirements. All staff: awareness of applicable obligations,Regulatory Risk; Operational Risk; Reputational Risk — specific domains determined by framework scope,"See official source — legal obligation, regulatory requirement, or voluntary standard as applicable",Administrative; Technical; Operational — specific distribution determined by framework content,1. Compliance status (compliant/non-compliant). 2. # open findings from last assessment. 3. % controls implemented. 4. Time to remediate findings. 5. Incident rate in framework scope area,Self-assessment; internal audit; external assessment as specified by issuing authority,ServiceNow GRC; IBM OpenPages; RSA Archer; MetricStream — all major GRC platforms support custom framework import. Contact vendor for framework-specific module,Third parties in scope must demonstrate equivalent compliance. Contractual compliance obligations; right-to-audit clauses; annual third-party assessment,Non-compliance: regulatory action by issuing authority; fines and penalties as specified; operational and reputational consequences,ICT / Digital / Telecom; Healthcare (IoMT); Industry & Trade (industrial IoT); ALL SECTORS (IoT device manufacturers/importers),YES — ALL SECTORS,,,,,
