140,IAIC,Insurance Authority,5. Financial Regulators,SAMA-CSF,SAMA Cybersecurity Framework,إطار الأمن السيبراني للبنك المركزي,2017,2017,114,MUST COMPLY WITH,Mandatory Complier,Insurance companies as SAMA/IAIC-regulated,MODERATE,https://www.sama.gov.sa/,SAMA; IAIC coordination,Issued May 2017 (one version; periodic guidance updates),"5 cybersecurity domains: Leadership & Governance, Risk Management & Compliance, Operations & Technology, Third Party, Customer Awareness. 29 objectives, 114 sub-controls. 6 maturity levels (Initial → Optimised). Annual self-assessment required. External assessment every 3 years. SAMA inspectors conduct surprise audits. Health insurance companies are SAMA/IAIC-regulated and must comply. Aligned with NIST CSF, ISO 27001, PCI DSS",Issued: May 2017. Annual self-assessment cycle,Update expected to align with NCA ECC-2:2024 and NIST CSF 2.0,CURRENT — 2017 version; update anticipated 2025,SAMA enforcement: business closure (extreme); SAR multimillion fines (unspecified ceiling); mandatory remediation plan; licence suspension; public warning,SAMA — Supervisory Technology Department; Insurance Authority (IAIC) for insurers; annual assessment reviewed by SAMA supervisors,Health Insurer (primary — SAMA/IAIC regulated); Private Hospital (if fintech/payment services regulated),Private financial sector (SAMA-regulated entities only; SAMA itself as public entity also complies with NCA ECC),Annual self-assessment; SAMA external assessment every 3 years; SAMA surprise inspection (risk-based),Annual self-assessment; external: every 3 years,6 (health insurance systems security: claims system security; API security for health insurance billing; connected payment device security; third-party health IT vendor management),NIST CSF (primary); ISO 27001:2022; PCI DSS v4 (payment); SWIFT CSP (correspondent banking); COBIT 2019 (IT governance),Arabic (primary); English translation available,Layer 1: Governance; Layer 2: Risk Management; Layer 4: Compliance,Yes — 6 maturity levels (1=Initial to 6=Optimising); sub-domain level maturity scoring; annual self-scoring required,Financial Sector Development Programme (FSDP); Vision 2030 Fintech strategy; health insurance digital transformation,Annual self-assessment report; risk register; policy documentation; penetration test reports; BCP test evidence; staff training records; third-party vendor assessment,Financial sector cybersecurity regulation theory: systemic risk and contagion (BCBS 2018). Risk-based supervision theory: maturity-based regulatory oversight. Sector-specific regulation vs cross-sector regulation debate.,"FOR: Only KSA framework with a formal 6-level maturity model — enables progression benchmarking; annual self-assessment creates continuous improvement culture; aligns with G-7 Fundamental Elements of Cybersecurity (financial sector). AGAINST: 2017 vintage — predates cloud-native architectures, AI-driven fraud, and health insurance sector IoT expansion; Wattlecorp (2025) notes SAMA-CSF and NCA-ECC-2 overlap creates dual compliance burden for health insurers; self-assessment without mandatory external verification undermines assurance value","Issued 2017 — significantly outdated vs NCA ECC-2 (2024) — no major revision in 7 years; SAMA-CSF and NCA-ECC-2 alignment not formally documented creating uncertainty; health insurance entities must navigate SAMA-CSF, NCA-ECC-2, PDPL, and CCHI-DCS simultaneously — regulatory fragmentation; IoT/connected insurance product security not addressed","SAMA-CSF vs NCA-ECC-2: SAMA-CSF sector-specific (financial), ECC-2 cross-sector — ECC-2 now dominant for cybersecurity, SAMA-CSF supplementary. SAMA-CSF vs ECB TIBER-EU: ECB uses red-team testing; SAMA-CSF relies on self-assessment. SAMA-CSF vs PRA CBEST (UK): similar financial sector model. SAMA-CSF maturity model (6 levels) more granular than ECC-2 (5 levels)",SAMA (2017) Cybersecurity Framework sama.gov.sa; BCBS (2018) Cyber-Resilience Basel Committee; Wattlecorp (2025) SAMA Cybersecurity Framework Aug 2025; Al-Matari EM et al. (2014) 'Corporate Governance in Saudi Arabia' Asian Social Science 10(9); Convene (2025) SAMA Cybersecurity Framework Overview Aug 2025,Official: SAMA website (sama.gov.sa) — free PDF. Training: CISM (Certified Information Security Manager) — governance domain aligns; CRISC (risk management); ISACA GRC tools. Estimated: 30 hours,GH-ICGA Layer 1 (Governance) — SAMA-CSF maturity model is an important reference for GH-ICGA's own maturity assessment design. Thesis: GH-ICGA proposes adapting SAMA-CSF's 6-level maturity model for healthcare IoT governance assessment — the financial sector's approach provides a tested template for health sector adoption,G + R + C (sector-specific GRC framework — most comprehensive in GRC terms for financial sector),Board: approve cybersecurity strategy annually; review maturity scores; ensure CISO reporting line. CISO: present quarterly to board; own SAMA-CSF self-assessment. Risk Committee: integrate cyber risk into enterprise risk. Compliance: monitor SAMA regulatory requirements,Cyber Risk; Operational Risk; Third-Party Risk; Systemic Risk (financial sector contagion); Reputational Risk; Regulatory Risk,REGULATORY — SAMA mandatory circular for all regulated entities. Enforced through SAMA supervisory examinations. No published maximum fine but SAMA can: suspend licence; impose mandatory remediation; require capital buffer for cyber risk,Administrative (A): 45 controls — governance; policy; awareness; HR. Technical (T): 42 controls — access management; operations security; incident management. Physical (P): 12 controls — physical security. Operational (O): 15 controls — vendor management; BCP,1. SAMA-CSF self-assessment score per domain (target maturity level 3+ / 5 domains). 2. Maturity progression year-on-year. 3. # critical cyber incidents per year. 4. Third-party risk assessment completion rate (target 100% for critical vendors). 5. % staff passed cybersecurity awareness training. 6. Penetration test findings remediation rate,Annual SAMA-CSF self-assessment (mandatory — submitted to SAMA); SAMA supervisory examination (every 3 years for major entities); external penetration test (annual); Red team exercise (every 2-3 years for Tier 1 entities); BCP/DR test (annual),IBM OpenPages; RSA Archer; CURA Risk Management; ServiceNow GRC; MetricStream; Resolver. KSA: Maqsam (local GRC tool); FICO Advisors. SAMA-approved assessment tools,All critical third parties: SAMA-CSF compliance assessment before engagement; annual reassessment; contractual right-to-audit; exit plan mandatory; cloud providers: SAMA Cloud Policy compliance; No data sharing without due diligence; sub-outsourcing requires SAMA prior approval for material activities,Low maturity (Level 1-2 in critical domains): SAMA mandatory remediation plan + deadline; regulatory capital impact consideration; reputational risk from SAMA disclosure. Licence conditions possible. For health insurers: CCHI + SAMA dual-regulator scrutiny,Financial Regulators; Healthcare & Life Sciences (health insurers),Sector-Specific,,,,,
141,IAIC,Insurance Authority,5. Financial Regulators,NCA-ECC-2:2024,Essential Cybersecurity Controls,ضوابط الأمن السيبراني الأساسية,ECC-2:2024,2018,108,MUST COMPLY WITH,Mandatory Complier,IAIC as government entity,MODERATE,https://nca.gov.sa/,NCA ECC scope,See official source for version history,Framework issued by the relevant authority. Version details available on the official website referenced in the source URL column. The framework has been updated periodically since first issuance.,See official source,Updated as needed by the issuing authority,CURRENT — consult official source for latest version,Penalties defined by issuing authority; consult official source. NCA ECC-2 penalties apply if relevant,Issuing authority and/or designated inspection department,As specified in framework scope — consult official document,Both (unless specified otherwise in official document),As defined by issuing authority,As defined by issuing authority,To be determined from framework text — see official source,See framework preamble for international standard references,Arabic (primary in KSA); English may be available,Mapped to relevant GH-ICGA layer — requires manual assessment,See official framework for maturity model details,Aligned with relevant Vision 2030 programme — see framework context,As specified in framework compliance requirements,Institutional theory and regulatory governance theory underpin all KSA regulatory frameworks. Specific theoretical basis varies by framework — consult academic literature via Google Scholar using framework name as search term.,"Academic debate varies by framework maturity and international adoption. For KSA-specific debate, consult: Almohammad & Wills (2021) J Cybersecurity; Al-Khater et al. (2020) IEEE Access; Baker McKenzie KSA regulatory updates.",Known criticisms include: implementation gap between standards and practice; resource constraints for smaller healthcare entities; rapid technology evolution outpacing regulation. Consult framework evaluation literature for specifics.,"Framework comparison requires contextual analysis. Key comparators: NIST CSF 2.0 (international benchmark), ISO 27001 (certifiable standard), ECC-2 (KSA mandatory baseline). See main database for cross-framework mapping.","Key foundational references: Almohammad A & Wills G (2021) J Cybersecurity; NIST (2024) CSF 2.0; ISO (2022) 27001; NCA official documents (nca.gov.sa); Baker McKenzie Global Data & Cyber Handbook 2025. Consult SFDA, MOH, and NCA websites for framework-specific official citations.","Official: Consult the official source URL in the database for the primary document. Training: ISACA, ISC2, RAPS, IAPP, and HIMSS offer professional development covering most regulatory domains. Estimated hours vary by framework complexity.",Relevance to GH-ICGA framework varies by layer — see GH-ICGA Layer Mapping column. All KSA mandatory frameworks are compliance inputs to GH-ICGA Layer 4. Frameworks with IoMT-specific provisions directly inform Layers 2 and 3.,G + C (Governance + Compliance — standard regulatory framework positioning),Senior management: accountability for framework compliance. Designated compliance officer: monitor requirements. All staff: awareness of applicable obligations,Regulatory Risk; Operational Risk; Reputational Risk — specific domains determined by framework scope,"See official source — legal obligation, regulatory requirement, or voluntary standard as applicable",Administrative; Technical; Operational — specific distribution determined by framework content,1. Compliance status (compliant/non-compliant). 2. # open findings from last assessment. 3. % controls implemented. 4. Time to remediate findings. 5. Incident rate in framework scope area,Self-assessment; internal audit; external assessment as specified by issuing authority,ServiceNow GRC; IBM OpenPages; RSA Archer; MetricStream — all major GRC platforms support custom framework import. Contact vendor for framework-specific module,Third parties in scope must demonstrate equivalent compliance. Contractual compliance obligations; right-to-audit clauses; annual third-party assessment,Non-compliance: regulatory action by issuing authority; fines and penalties as specified; operational and reputational consequences,ALL 17 SECTORS,YES — ALL SECTORS,,,,,
142,IAIC,Insurance Authority,5. Financial Regulators,PDPL-2021,Personal Data Protection Law,نظام حماية البيانات الشخصية,2021+IR 2023,2021,45,MUST COMPLY WITH,Mandatory Complier,Insurers process policyholder health data,HIGH,https://sdaia.gov.sa/,SDAIA; Chambers TMT 2026,Royal Decree M/19 (16 Sep 2021) → amended Apr 2023,"Original (2021): 19 articles covering consent, data subject rights, controller obligations, sensitive data (health = sensitive), cross-border transfer. Apr 2023 amendment: strengthened enforcement, clarified health data provisions, aligned with PDPL-IR-2023. Grace period for compliance ended Sep 14, 2024. Penalties: up to SAR 5 million fine + 2 years imprisonment for sensitive data breach","Issued: Sep 2021. Amended: Apr 2023. Fully enforceable: Sep 14, 2024",SDAIA reviews annually; amendments expected 2025-2026,CURRENT — fully enforceable since Sep 2024,Criminal offence: up to 2 years imprisonment + SAR 3M fine for disclosing sensitive data (health data = sensitive); administrative fine up to SAR 5M; mandatory breach notification to SDAIA within 72 hours; data subject compensation claims,SDAIA — Data Protection Authority (DPA functions); NDMO for government entities; courts for criminal penalties,Hospital; Clinic; Lab; Pharmacy; Health IT Vendor; Health Insurer; Government Health Entity; Medical Device Manufacturer (all processing patient data),Both + foreign entities processing data of KSA residents,SDAIA audit; controller self-assessment; DPO oversight; data subject complaints trigger investigation,Continuous (breach notification: 72 hours); annual DPO report; SDAIA random audits,12 (IoMT data minimisation; purpose limitation; consent management for wearable data; automated decision-making; data subject rights for IoMT-generated health data; cross-border transfer of IoMT data),GDPR (substantial alignment — adequate protection basis); APEC Privacy Framework; ISO 29100; ISO 27701,Arabic (official); English translation (unofficial),Layer 4: Compliance; Layer 2: Risk Management,No formal maturity model; SDAIA publishes compliance guidance,National Data Strategy; Vision 2030 Digital Economy; Digital Government transformation,Privacy notices; consent records; DPO appointment letter; data mapping/flow diagrams; breach notification records; DPIA for health data processing; controller registration,Privacy as fundamental right (Warren & Brandeis 1890; Westin 1967). Contextual integrity theory (Nissenbaum 2004): health data flows must match medical context norms. Regulatory theory: data protection authority independence and enforcement. Information asymmetry theory: patients cannot protect their own data without regulation.,"FOR: Long-overdue — KSA had no comprehensive data protection law until 2021; health data explicitly protected as sensitive category; aligns KSA with global adequacy standards. AGAINST: Alrawabdeh (2014) and Aldawood & Skinner (2019) documented Saudi privacy culture gap — regulation without public awareness is ineffective; 'GDPR-lite' criticism: exemptions broader than GDPR, enforcement weaker; health data provisions in IR still less specific than UK GDPR Schedule 1 conditions",No independent supervisory authority (SDAIA is both promoter and regulator — conflict of interest noted by Baker McKenzie 2025); patient data portability rights weaker than GDPR Article 20; no explicit right to explanation for AI-automated health decisions; cross-border transfer rules changed twice in 12 months (regulatory instability); small healthcare providers lack capacity for DPO requirements,"PDPL vs GDPR: PDPL penalty lower (SAR 5M vs €20M); PDPL broader government exemptions; GDPR has independent DPA, PDPL does not. PDPL vs HIPAA: PDPL = all personal data (broader); HIPAA = US-specific health data only. PDPL vs UK GDPR: PDPL misses explicit Schedule 1 health data conditions. PDPL ahead of HIPAA on consent granularity; behind GDPR on enforcement independence",Baker McKenzie (2025) Saudi Arabia PDPL Global Data & Cyber Handbook; Alrawabdeh W (2014) 'Internet and Social Media Challenges in Arab World' EJSR; Westin AF (1967) Privacy and Freedom NY Atheneum; Nissenbaum H (2004) 'Privacy as Contextual Integrity' Washington Law Review 79(1); CMS Law (2025) Saudi Arabia Data Protection & Cybersecurity Expert Guide Oct 2025,Official: SDAIA website (sdaia.gov.sa) — Arabic + English. Training: IAPP CIPM/CIPP/E (privacy certifications applicable); OneTrust PDPL compliance course; Baker McKenzie PDPL compliance guide. Language: Arabic primary — strong Arabic reading recommended. Estimated: 50 hours for health sector DPO competence,PRIMARY driver for GH-ICGA Layer 4 (Compliance) — PDPL is the legal foundation for patient data governance in the preliminary GH-ICGA framework. Thesis argument: GH-ICGA Layer 4 operationalises PDPL requirements into IoMT-specific controls (consent management for wearable data; purpose limitation for monitoring streams; breach response for connected device incidents),G + C (Governance + Compliance — strong compliance driver; risk implicit),Board/CEO: ultimate accountability for PDPL compliance. DPO: mandatory for large-scale health data processors — reports to highest management level. Privacy Committee: cross-functional oversight. All staff processing personal data: mandatory privacy awareness training annually,Privacy Risk (PRIMARY); Legal/Regulatory Risk; Reputational Risk; Strategic Risk (data monetisation constraints); Cross-Border Risk (data transfer restrictions),LEGAL — Royal Decree M/19 2021 + Implementing Regulations Sep 2023. Criminal: imprisonment up to 2 years + SAR 3M fine for sensitive data disclosure. Administrative: SAR 5M maximum fine. Civil: data subject compensation claims,Administrative (A): consent management; privacy notices; DPO appointment; DPIA process; breach notification procedure. Technical (T): data minimisation; pseudonymisation; access controls for personal data; encryption. Operational (O): data retention enforcement; data subject rights fulfilment process; cross-border transfer review,1. # data subject rights requests fulfilled on time (target: 100% within 30 days). 2. # data breaches notified to SDAIA within 72h (target: 100%). 3. % staff completed privacy training (target: 100%). 4. # DPIAs completed for high-risk processing activities. 5. Data retention compliance rate (% records within policy). 6. % third-party processors with DPA agreements,Annual DPIA review; data mapping audit (controller record validation); consent record audit; breach response simulation (tabletop); access control review for personal data systems; third-party processor audit; SDAIA compliance self-assessment,OneTrust (market leader for PDPL); TrustArc; Nymity; DataGrail; Varonis (data discovery); Microsoft Purview (data classification). SDAIA National Data Governance Platform for registration. Arabic-capable tools preferred,All third-party data processors must have Data Processing Agreement (DPA). Processors must demonstrate equivalent PDPL compliance. Cross-border transfers require adequacy assessment or appropriate safeguards. Sub-processor disclosure mandatory. Annual processor risk assessment required,Non-compliance: SAR 5M administrative fine; criminal prosecution for sensitive data breach; SDAIA investigation; mandatory breach public disclosure. Business disruption: SDAIA can order processing suspension. Reputational: patient trust damage. Market: EU adequate protection status depends on PDPL enforcement credibility,ALL 17 SECTORS — any entity processing personal data,YES — ALL SECTORS,,,,,
