313,RHC1,Riyadh Health Cluster 1,17. Regional Health Clusters,MOH-HIS,Health Information System Standards,معايير نظم المعلومات الصحية,2024 update,2010,112,MUST COMPLY WITH,Mandatory Complier,Riyadh Health Cluster 1 hospitals — mandatory HIS compliance,HIGHEST,https://www.moh.gov.sa/en/,MOH official,First issued ~2010 → comprehensive update 2022 → mandatory EMR update 2024,"Progressive standards covering: 2010 (basic EHR requirements); 2015 (interoperability standards added); 2018 (NABIDH integration); 2020 (mobile health and telemedicine modules); 2022 (comprehensive 112-control update covering clinical systems, imaging, medication management, emergency, genomics, training); 2024 (mandatory EMR adoption for all MOH and private facilities aligned with NABIDH)",First: 2010. Major update: 2022. EMR mandate: 2024,Updated with NABIDH platform releases; typically annual minor updates,CURRENT — 2024 version mandatory,MOH facility licensing at risk; mandatory corrective action plan; financial penalties under Private Health Institutions Law; non-compliant systems barred from NABIDH connectivity,MOH — Directorate of Health Information (DHI); regional health cluster inspection teams; NHIC for interoperability compliance,Hospital; Clinic; Lab; Pharmacy (hospital); Government Health Entity,Both (mandatory for MOH facilities; mandatory for private facilities since 2024 PHILAW update),MOH annual IT audit; NHIC interoperability assessment; CBAHI HIT standards review during accreditation,Annual MOH audit; NABIDH connectivity continuous monitoring,28 (medical device integration into HIS; alarm management; device data capture; IoMT workflow integration; remote monitoring data ingestion; device incident reporting to HIS),HL7 FHIR R4 (interoperability); IHE profiles (integration); ISO 27799:2016 (health ISMS); DICOM (imaging),Arabic (primary); English technical annexes,Layer 1: Governance; Layer 3: Technical Controls; Layer 4: Compliance,Yes — 4 implementation levels (Basic / Intermediate / Advanced / Optimised); MOH tracks facility scores,HSTP digital health; NABIDH national EHR; Vision 2030 healthcare quality targets,HIS system documentation; NABIDH connectivity certificate; interoperability test results; staff training records; system access audit logs; downtime contingency procedure,Health informatics systems theory (Shortliffe & Cimino 2006): HIS as sociotechnical infrastructure. Interoperability theory (Kuperman 2011): semantic/syntactic/organisational interoperability levels. Technology adoption theory (TAM — Davis 1989): clinician adoption barriers to HIS.,FOR: National standardisation essential for patient safety continuity (WHO 2019); NABIDH integration creates longitudinal patient record unavailable previously; IoMT data integration requirements push hospitals toward modern system architectures. AGAINST: Ajami & Bagheri-Tadi (2013) and Aloudah et al. (2021) document implementation-reality gap in Saudi HIS — standards exist but compliance varies widely by facility tier; urban-rural HIS capacity gap poorly addressed; cybersecurity requirements within HIS standards historically weak (pre-NABIDH),112 controls create compliance burden without proportionality guidance; small private clinics face disproportionate compliance cost; no formal HIS certification programme — self-declaration accepted; IoMT data standards not harmonised with international HL7 FHIR profiles; historical emphasis on data capture over data quality,MOH-HIS vs NHS Digital standards (UK): NHS has legally binding DCB standards with enforcement; MOH-HIS is ministerial directive with softer enforcement. MOH-HIS vs ONC Health IT Certification (US): US has federal certification; KSA moving toward certification (NABIDH conformance). MOH-HIS + NHIC-NABIDH = closest to ONC approach in KSA,Ajami S & Bagheri-Tadi T (2013) 'Barriers for Adopting Electronic Health Records' MateriaSociomed 25(3); Aloudah N et al. (2021) 'Digital Health in Saudi Arabia' Sustainability 13(12); Shortliffe EH & Cimino JJ (2006) Biomedical Informatics Springer; WHO (2019) Classification of Digital Interventions; Alotaibi YK & Federico F (2017) 'Impact of Health IT on Patient Safety' Saudi Medical J 38(12),Official: MOH website (moh.gov.sa). Training: CPHIMS (Certified Professional Health Informatics & Information Management); HL7 FHIR Foundational (HL7.org); NHIC developer portal training (nhic.gov.sa). Language: Arabic primary. Estimated: 45 hours,GH-ICGA Layer 1 (Governance) + Layer 3 (Technical Controls) — MOH-HIS is the clinical data governance backbone that the preliminary GH-ICGA IoMT framework must integrate with. Thesis: GH-ICGA IoMT data model must align with HIS data standards to ensure IoMT device data flows into the national health record coherently,G + R + C (all three — clinical information governance foundation),Hospital CEO: accountable for HIS compliance. CIO/CMIO: responsible for HIS implementation and NABIDH connectivity. Clinical Informatics Committee: govern HIS standards adoption. Department Heads: responsible for clinical data quality in their systems,Operational Risk (HIS downtime = clinical risk); Patient Safety Risk; Data Quality Risk; Regulatory Risk (MOH non-compliance); Interoperability Risk (NABIDH disconnection),REGULATORY — MOH ministerial directive. Mandatory for all licensed facilities. Non-compliance risks licensing. Enforced through MOH annual audit and NABIDH connectivity monitoring,Technical (T): 68 controls — system architecture; integration; security; availability; backup. Administrative (A): 28 controls — data governance; policies; training; documentation. Operational (O): 16 controls — downtime procedures; change management; vendor management,1. NABIDH connectivity status (connected/disconnected). 2. HIS downtime incidents per month (target <4h/month). 3. Data quality score (completeness/accuracy) from NABIDH reports. 4. % clinical staff trained on HIS (target 100%). 5. Time-to-restore HIS after failure (target <4h). 6. # HIS security incidents per quarter,Annual MOH HIS audit (documentation + system walk-through); NABIDH API conformance test (NHIC automated); data quality audit; HIS security assessment; downtime procedure simulation; user access review,Epic; Cerner (Oracle Health); Philips Tasy EMR; Meditech; InterSystems HealthShare — all NABIDH-integrated. For compliance: ServiceNow Healthcare; Qualio (quality management); NHIC developer portal for FHIR conformance testing,HIS vendors: must be MOH-approved/NABIDH-certified; contractual data protection obligations; source code escrow for critical systems; SLA for 99.9% uptime; mandatory SFDA notification if HIS failure affects medical devices; annual security assessment of HIS vendors,"Non-compliance: MOH corrective action; facility licence conditions; NABIDH disconnection (loss of referrals, national patient record access); PHILAW 2024 penalties for private facilities. Clinical impact: patient safety risk from poor data quality or HIS unavailability",Healthcare & Life Sciences,Sector-Specific,,,,,
314,RHC1,Riyadh Health Cluster 1,17. Regional Health Clusters,MOH-EMR-NABIDH,EMR Standards / NABIDH,معايير السجل الصحي الإلكتروني / نبض,Mandatory 2024,2010,52,MUST COMPLY WITH,Mandatory Complier,Riyadh Health Cluster 1 — mandatory NABIDH connectivity 2024,HIGHEST,https://www.moh.gov.sa/en/,MOH/NHIC,NABIDH platform launched ~2018 → mandatory EMR adoption for all facilities 2024,"NABIDH (National Unified EHR Platform): v1 2018 (pilot MOH hospitals); 2019-2021 (rollout to health clusters); 2022 (private hospital voluntary integration); 2023 (API standards published based on FHIR R4); 2024 (mandatory for ALL healthcare facilities per Private Health Institutions Law update — complete patient longitudinal record across facilities). EMR standard covers 52 functional requirements including: CPOE, medication reconciliation, imaging integration, patient portal, audit logs",NABIDH launched: 2018. Mandatory for all: 2024,Continuously updated with NABIDH platform releases,CURRENT — mandatory since 2024,Facility licensing revocation; MOH enforcement action; barred from government referrals; penalties under PHILAW 2024,MOH — NABIDH Programme Office; NHIC; regional health clusters monitor connectivity,Hospital; Clinic; Lab; Pharmacy; Government Health Entity — ALL healthcare facilities mandatory,Both (mandatory since 2024 PHILAW update for all facilities),NHIC connectivity assessment; NABIDH API conformance testing; MOH annual audit,Continuous (NABIDH connectivity monitored in real time); annual formal audit,22 (IoMT data integration to NABIDH: wearable data ingestion API; remote monitoring feeds; device-generated vital signs; medical device alerts to patient record; IoMT consent management in EHR),HL7 FHIR R4; IHE (Integrating the Healthcare Enterprise); SNOMED CT; ICD-10; LOINC,Arabic + English (NABIDH technical specifications bilingual),Layer 1: Governance; Layer 3: Technical Controls; Layer 4: Compliance; Layer 5: Assurance,Yes — NABIDH connectivity levels (1: Basic data exchange; 2: Full longitudinal record; 3: Advanced interoperability with IoMT); MOH tracks facility level,HSTP digital health transformation; NABIDH national EHR; NHIS claims integration,NABIDH connectivity certificate; API integration test reports; patient consent records for data sharing; EHR system validation; data quality reports,Institutional theory and regulatory governance theory underpin all KSA regulatory frameworks. Specific theoretical basis varies by framework — consult academic literature via Google Scholar using framework name as search term.,"Academic debate varies by framework maturity and international adoption. For KSA-specific debate, consult: Almohammad & Wills (2021) J Cybersecurity; Al-Khater et al. (2020) IEEE Access; Baker McKenzie KSA regulatory updates.",Known criticisms include: implementation gap between standards and practice; resource constraints for smaller healthcare entities; rapid technology evolution outpacing regulation. Consult framework evaluation literature for specifics.,"Framework comparison requires contextual analysis. Key comparators: NIST CSF 2.0 (international benchmark), ISO 27001 (certifiable standard), ECC-2 (KSA mandatory baseline). See main database for cross-framework mapping.","Key foundational references: Almohammad A & Wills G (2021) J Cybersecurity; NIST (2024) CSF 2.0; ISO (2022) 27001; NCA official documents (nca.gov.sa); Baker McKenzie Global Data & Cyber Handbook 2025. Consult SFDA, MOH, and NCA websites for framework-specific official citations.","Official: Consult the official source URL in the database for the primary document. Training: ISACA, ISC2, RAPS, IAPP, and HIMSS offer professional development covering most regulatory domains. Estimated hours vary by framework complexity.",Relevance to GH-ICGA framework varies by layer — see GH-ICGA Layer Mapping column. All KSA mandatory frameworks are compliance inputs to GH-ICGA Layer 4. Frameworks with IoMT-specific provisions directly inform Layers 2 and 3.,G + C (Governance + Compliance — standard regulatory framework positioning),Senior management: accountability for framework compliance. Designated compliance officer: monitor requirements. All staff: awareness of applicable obligations,Regulatory Risk; Operational Risk; Reputational Risk — specific domains determined by framework scope,"See official source — legal obligation, regulatory requirement, or voluntary standard as applicable",Administrative; Technical; Operational — specific distribution determined by framework content,1. Compliance status (compliant/non-compliant). 2. # open findings from last assessment. 3. % controls implemented. 4. Time to remediate findings. 5. Incident rate in framework scope area,Self-assessment; internal audit; external assessment as specified by issuing authority,ServiceNow GRC; IBM OpenPages; RSA Archer; MetricStream — all major GRC platforms support custom framework import. Contact vendor for framework-specific module,Third parties in scope must demonstrate equivalent compliance. Contractual compliance obligations; right-to-audit clauses; annual third-party assessment,Non-compliance: regulatory action by issuing authority; fines and penalties as specified; operational and reputational consequences,Healthcare & Life Sciences,Sector-Specific,,,,,
315,RHC1,Riyadh Health Cluster 1,17. Regional Health Clusters,MOH-PS,Patient Safety Standards,معايير سلامة المريض,2024 update,2015,72,MUST COMPLY WITH,Mandatory Complier,Riyadh Health Cluster 1 — patient safety compliance,HIGHEST,https://www.moh.gov.sa/en/,MOH official,First issued ~2015 → updates 2020 → 2024 update,"Based on WHO and JCI patient safety frameworks. Covers: patient identification (minimum 2 identifiers), medication safety (high-alert medications protocol), surgical safety checklist, falls prevention, infection control, critical value reporting, handover communications (SBAR). 2020 update added COVID-19 IPC protocols. 2024 update adds IoMT device safety (alarm fatigue management, device connectivity requirements)","First: ~2015. Updated: 2020, 2024",Annual review with MOH patient safety incidents data,CURRENT — 2024 version,MOH corrective action order; facility licence conditions; public safety alert; criminal liability for gross negligence,MOH — Patient Safety Centre; regional health cluster inspection; CBAHI (patient safety standards embedded in accreditation),Hospital; Clinic; Government Health Entity,Both,Annual MOH patient safety inspection; CBAHI survey (integrated); incident investigation,Annual; triggered by serious adverse event,14 (alarm fatigue management for IoMT devices; connected infusion pump safety; IV smart pump drug library governance; patient ID for IoMT devices; device failure response procedures; remote monitoring alert management),WHO Patient Safety Challenges (Global Patient Safety Action Plan 2021-2030); JCI Patient Safety Goals; The Joint Commission NPSG,Arabic (primary); English reference,Layer 1: Governance; Layer 4: Compliance,Yes — patient safety maturity levels (Safety-I to Safety-III); MOH tracks facility scores,HSTP patient safety; Vision 2030 quality targets; National Patient Safety Centre,Incident reporting system data; patient safety walkround records; fall prevention audit; medication safety audit; CBAHI patient safe