 (Governance + Compliance — standard regulatory framework positioning),Senior management: accountability for framework compliance. Designated compliance officer: monitor requirements. All staff: awareness of applicable obligations,Regulatory Risk; Operational Risk; Reputational Risk — specific domains determined by framework scope,"See official source — legal obligation, regulatory requirement, or voluntary standard as applicable",Administrative; Technical; Operational — specific distribution determined by framework content,1. Compliance status (compliant/non-compliant). 2. # open findings from last assessment. 3. % controls implemented. 4. Time to remediate findings. 5. Incident rate in framework scope area,Self-assessment; internal audit; external assessment as specified by issuing authority,ServiceNow GRC; IBM OpenPages; RSA Archer; MetricStream — all major GRC platforms support custom framework import. Contact vendor for framework-specific module,Third parties in scope must demonstrate equivalent compliance. Contractual compliance obligations; right-to-audit clauses; annual third-party assessment,Non-compliance: regulatory action by issuing authority; fines and penalties as specified; operational and reputational consequences,Healthcare & Life Sciences,Sector-Specific,,,,,
325,RHC2,Riyadh Health Cluster 2,17. Regional Health Clusters,MOH-PS,Patient Safety Standards,معايير سلامة المريض,2024 update,2015,72,MUST COMPLY WITH,Mandatory Complier,Riyadh Health Cluster 2 — patient safety compliance,HIGHEST,https://www.moh.gov.sa/en/,MOH official,First issued ~2015 → updates 2020 → 2024 update,"Based on WHO and JCI patient safety frameworks. Covers: patient identification (minimum 2 identifiers), medication safety (high-alert medications protocol), surgical safety checklist, falls prevention, infection control, critical value reporting, handover communications (SBAR). 2020 update added COVID-19 IPC protocols. 2024 update adds IoMT device safety (alarm fatigue management, device connectivity requirements)","First: ~2015. Updated: 2020, 2024",Annual review with MOH patient safety incidents data,CURRENT — 2024 version,MOH corrective action order; facility licence conditions; public safety alert; criminal liability for gross negligence,MOH — Patient Safety Centre; regional health cluster inspection; CBAHI (patient safety standards embedded in accreditation),Hospital; Clinic; Government Health Entity,Both,Annual MOH patient safety inspection; CBAHI survey (integrated); incident investigation,Annual; triggered by serious adverse event,14 (alarm fatigue management for IoMT devices; connected infusion pump safety; IV smart pump drug library governance; patient ID for IoMT devices; device failure response procedures; remote monitoring alert management),WHO Patient Safety Challenges (Global Patient Safety Action Plan 2021-2030); JCI Patient Safety Goals; The Joint Commission NPSG,Arabic (primary); English reference,Layer 1: Governance; Layer 4: Compliance,Yes — patient safety maturity levels (Safety-I to Safety-III); MOH tracks facility scores,HSTP patient safety; Vision 2030 quality targets; National Patient Safety Centre,Incident reporting system data; patient safety walkround records; fall prevention audit; medication safety audit; CBAHI patient safety standards compliance,Institutional theory and regulatory governance theory underpin all KSA regulatory frameworks. Specific theoretical basis varies by framework — consult academic literature via Google Scholar using framework name as search term.,"Academic debate varies by framework maturity and international adoption. For KSA-specific debate, consult: Almohammad & Wills (2021) J Cybersecurity; Al-Khater et al. (2020) IEEE Access; Baker McKenzie KSA regulatory updates.",Known criticisms include: implementation gap between standards and practice; resource constraints for smaller healthcare entities; rapid technology evolution outpacing regulation. Consult framework evaluation literature for specifics.,"Framework comparison requires contextual analysis. Key comparators: NIST CSF 2.0 (international benchmark), ISO 27001 (certifiable standard), ECC-2 (KSA mandatory baseline). See main database for cross-framework mapping.","Key foundational references: Almohammad A & Wills G (2021) J Cybersecurity; NIST (2024) CSF 2.0; ISO (2022) 27001; NCA official documents (nca.gov.sa); Baker McKenzie Global Data & Cyber Handbook 2025. Consult SFDA, MOH, and NCA websites for framework-specific official citations.","Official: Consult the official source URL in the database for the primary document. Training: ISACA, ISC2, RAPS, IAPP, and HIMSS offer professional development covering most regulatory domains. Estimated hours vary by framework complexity.",Relevance to GH-ICGA framework varies by layer — see GH-ICGA Layer Mapping column. All KSA mandatory frameworks are compliance inputs to GH-ICGA Layer 4. Frameworks with IoMT-specific provisions directly inform Layers 2 and 3.,G + C (Governance + Compliance — standard regulatory framework positioning),Senior management: accountability for framework compliance. Designated compliance officer: monitor requirements. All staff: awareness of applicable obligations,Regulatory Risk; Operational Risk; Reputational Risk — specific domains determined by framework scope,"See official source — legal obligation, regulatory requirement, or voluntary standard as applicable",Administrative; Technical; Operational — specific distribution determined by framework content,1. Compliance status (compliant/non-compliant). 2. # open findings from last assessment. 3. % controls implemented. 4. Time to remediate findings. 5. Incident rate in framework scope area,Self-assessment; internal audit; external assessment as specified by issuing authority,ServiceNow GRC; IBM OpenPages; RSA Archer; MetricStream — all major GRC platforms support custom framework import. Contact vendor for framework-specific module,Third parties in scope must demonstrate equivalent compliance. Contractual compliance obligations; right-to-audit clauses; annual third-party assessment,Non-compliance: regulatory action by issuing authority; fines and penalties as specified; operational and reputational consequences,Healthcare & Life Sciences,Sector-Specific,,,,,
326,RHC2,Riyadh Health Cluster 2,17. Regional Health Clusters,MOH-PR,Patient Rights Charter,ميثاق حقوق المريض,2019,2012,45,MUST COMPLY WITH,Mandatory Complier,Riyadh Health Cluster 2 facilities,HIGH,https://www.moh.gov.sa/en/,MOH official,First Charter ~2012 → updated 2019,"45 rights and obligations. Key provisions: informed consent (including electronic consent for telemedicine), right to access medical records (digital access through NABIDH patient portal), right to privacy of health information, right to refuse treatment, right to second opinion. 2019 update added digital health rights (right to data portability from health apps)",First: ~2012. Updated: 2019,Review 2024-2025 expected to add AI/algorithmic transparency rights,CURRENT — 2019 version,Penalties defined by issuing authority; consult official source. NCA ECC-2 penalties apply if relevant,Issuing authority and/or designated inspection department,As specified in framework scope — consult official document,Both (unless specified otherwise in official document),As defined by issuing authority,As defined by issuing authority,To be determined from framework text — see official source,See framework preamble for international standard references,Arabic (primary in KSA); English may be available,Mapped to relevant GH-ICGA layer — requires manual assessment,See official framework for maturity model details,Aligned with relevant Vision 2030 programme — see framework context,As specified in framework compliance requirements,Institutional theory and regulatory governance theory underpin all KSA regulatory frameworks. Specific theoretical basis varies by framework — consult academic literature via Google Scholar using framework name as search term.,"Academic debate varies by framework maturity and international adoption. For KSA-specific debate, consult: Almohammad & Wills (2021) J Cybersecurity; Al-Khater et al. (2020) IEEE Access; Baker McKenzie KSA regulatory updates.",Known criticisms include: implementation gap between standards and practice; resource constraints for smaller healthcare entities; rapid technology evolution outpacing regulation. Consult framework evaluation literature for specifics.,"Framework comparison requires contextual analysis. Key comparators: NIST CSF 2.0 (international benchmark), ISO 27001 (certifiable standard), ECC-2 (KSA mandatory baseline). See main database for cross-framework mapping.","Key foundational references: Almohammad A & Wills G (2021) J Cybersecurity; NIST (2024) CSF 2.0; ISO (2022) 27001; NCA official documents (nca.gov.sa); Baker McKenzie Global Data & Cyber Handbook 2025. Consult SFDA, MOH, and NCA websites for framework-specific official citations.","Official: Consult the official source URL in the database for the primary document. Training: ISACA, ISC2, RAPS, IAPP, and HIMSS offer professional development covering most regulatory domains. Estimated hours vary by framework complexity.",Relevance to GH-ICGA framework varies by layer — see GH-ICGA Layer Mapping column. All KSA mandatory frameworks are compliance inputs to GH-ICGA Layer 4. Frameworks with IoMT-specific provisions directly inform Layers 2 and 3.,G + C (Governance + Compliance — standard regulatory framework positioning),Senior management: accountability for framework compliance. Designated compliance officer: monitor requirements. All staff: awareness of applicable obligations,Regulatory Risk; Operational Risk; Reputational Risk — specific domains determined by framework scope,"See official source — legal obligation, regulatory requirement, or voluntary standard as applicable",Administrative; Technical; Operational — specific distribution determined by framework content,1. Compliance status (compliant/non-compliant). 2. # open findings from last assessment. 3. % controls implemented. 4. Time to remediate findings. 5. Incident rate in framework scope area,Self-assessment; internal audit; external assessment as specified by issuing authority,ServiceNow GRC; IBM OpenPages; RSA Archer; MetricStream — all major GRC platforms support custom framework import. Contact vendor for framework-specific module,Third parties in scope must demonstrate equivalent compliance. Contractual compliance obligations; right-to-audit clauses; annual third-party assessment,Non-compliance: regulatory action by issuing authority; fines and penalties as specified; operational and reputational consequences,Healthcare & Life Sciences,Sector-Specific,,,,,
327,RHC2,Riyadh Health Cluster 2,17. Regional Health Clusters,CBAHI-HAS-H,Hospital Accreditation Standards,معايير اعتماد المستشفيات,5th Ed. 2021,2008,298,MUST COMPLY WITH,Voluntary,Riyadh Health Cluster 2 hospitals pursuing CBAHI accreditation,HIGHEST,https://www.cbahi.gov.sa/,CBAHI official,1st Ed 2008 → 2nd Ed 2011 → 3rd Ed 2014 → 4th Ed 2017 → 5th Ed 2021,"5th Edition (2021, current): 298 standards across domains including Patient and Family Rights, Patient Assessment, Care of Patients, Patient Safety, Medication Management, Infection Prevention, Facility Management, Staff Qualifications, Quality Improvement, Governance, HIT (Health Information Technology). HIT standards require EMR, NABIDH connectivity, access control, audit trails, downtime procedures. IoMT requirements added in 5th Ed: medical device integration standards, alarm management policy. Benchmarked against JCI standards",1st Ed: 2008. 2nd: 2011. 3rd: 2014. 4th: 2017. 5th: 2021,6th Edition expected 2025-2026,CURRENT — 5th Edition 2021,Accreditation denial; accreditation suspension; accreditation withdrawal; MOH facility licensing risk (accreditation linked to licensing in practice),CBAHI — Accreditation Survey Teams (peer surveyors); MOH recognises CBAHI as national accreditor,Hospital (primary scope); Teaching Hospital; Specialist Centre,Both (voluntary but operationally required),Full survey every 3 years; midterm (focused) survey at 18 months; unannounced visits; corrective action follow-up,Full survey: 3-year cycle. Midterm: 18 months. Unannounced: possible at any time,22 (HIT standards: medical device integration; alarm management policy mandatory; device maintenance logs; remote monitoring governance; downtime procedures; device inventory; cybersecurity of clinical systems as patient safety issue),JCI (Joint Commission International) — benchmarked; ISQua (accreditation body accreditor); WHO Safe Hospitals Framework,Arabic + English (official bilingual),Layer 1: Governance; Layer 4: Compliance; Layer 5: Assurance,Yes — Met / Partially Met / Not Met / Not Applicable per standard; overall accreditation decision = scored threshold,HSTP quality transformation; Vision 2030 healthcare quality targets (JCI/CBAHI accreditation for all tertiary hospitals),Survey self-assessment; policy documentation; staff credential files; patient safety incident log; HIT system audit; quality improvement data; patient satisfaction scores,Healthcare quality theory (Donabedian 1988): structure-process-outcome triad. Accreditation theory: third-party external validation as quality assurance mechanism. Institutional isomorphism: accreditation spreads best practices through mimetic pressure. Patient safety culture theory (Reason 2000): systems-based safety vs blame culture.,FOR: Almutairi et al. (2015) and Alkhenizan & Shaw (2011) find CBAHI-accredited hospitals show measurable quality improvements; national benchmark grounded in Saudi context unlike JCI (US-centric); patient safety improvements documented post-accreditation. AGAINST: Voluntariness undermines universal coverage — only ~40% of KSA hospitals accredited (WHO PHSSR 2025); Greenfield & Braithwaite (2008) meta-analysis questions causal link between accreditation and patient outcomes; survey preparation creates compliance-oriented behaviours rather than genuine quality improvement (gaming risk),3-year survey cycle too long for rapid IoMT deployment environment; HIT standards in 5th Ed still less specific than HIMSS EMRAM Stage 6/7 requirements; cybersecurity standards within CBAHI remain minimal compared to NCA ECC-2 requirements; no dedicated IoMT governance chapter despite medical device proliferation; rural and small facility accreditation rates critically low,CBAHI vs JCI: JCI globally recognised; CBAHI nationally recognised — JCI preferred for medical tourism attracting hospitals. CBAHI vs HIMSS EMRAM: CBAHI is broad clinical; HIMSS EMRAM is digital health maturity-specific. CBAHI vs DNV: CBAHI similar survey model. CBAHI + HIMSS EMRAM combined = most comprehensive digital health quality picture for KSA hospitals,Alkhenizan A & Shaw C (2011) 'Impact of Accreditation' Ann Saudi Med 31(4); Almutairi AF et al. (2015) 'Perceptions of CBAHI Accreditation' BMC Health Services Research; Donabedian A (1988) 'Quality of Care' JAMA 260(12); Greenfield D & Braithwaite J (2008) 'Health Sector Accreditation Research' Int J Quality Health Care 20(3); WHO (2025) PHSSR Saudi Arabia,Official: CBAHI website (cbahi.gov.sa) — standards available to purchase. Training: CBAHI education programmes; surveyor training; CPHQ (Certified Professional in Healthcare Quality). Estimated: 60 hours for surveyors; 20 hours for department leads,GH-ICGA Layer 5 (Assurance) — CBAHI accreditation is the primary assurance mechanism for hospital IoMT governance. Thesis argument: CBAHI-HAS-H 5th Ed HIT standards need a cybersecurity annex for IoMT — GH-ICGA Layer 5 Assurance proposes an IoMT-specific evaluation framework that could be adopted within CBAHI survey process,G + C (Governance through quality standards + voluntary compliance assurance),CEO: accountable for accreditation status. CMO: responsible for clinical quality chapter compliance. CNO: responsible for nursing quality. Quality/Patient Safety Director: coordinates accreditation programme. Department heads: responsible for chapter compliance in their domain. Accreditation Committee: cross-functional governance body,Quality Risk; Patient Safety Risk; Operational Risk (accreditation loss = licensing risk); Reputational Risk; Regulatory Risk (MOH links accreditation to licensing in practice),VOLUNTARY — but operationally MANDATORY (MOH licensing increasingly linked to CBAHI in practice; required for JCI-referencing hospitals and private insurance network participation),Administrative (A): 150 controls — policies; procedures; governance; quality committees. Technical (T): 48 controls — HIT; equipment maintenance; diagnostic systems. Operational (O): 100 controls — clinical procedures; patient safety practices; staff competencies,"1. CBAHI survey score per chapter (target ≥90% Met). 2. # sentinel events per 1,000 admissions. 3. Falls rate per 1,000 patient days. 4. Hospital-acquired infection rate. 5. Patient satisfaction score (target >90%). 6. Medication error rate. 7. HIT compliance score (CBAHI HIT chapter)",CBAHI full survey (3-year cycle — 2-3 day on-site assessment); midterm survey (18 months — focused chapters); unannounced visit (possible any time); tracer methodology; document review; staff interviews; facility inspection,Accreditation Manager (CBAHI online platform); EQUIP (quality management platform); PowerDMS; Quantros; Press Ganey (patient satisfaction); Meditech/Epic quality modules; RL6 (incident reporting),"Contractors and service providers in clinical areas must meet CBAHI competency requirements; credentialing of contracted physicians required; outsourced services (laundry, catering, security) must meet CBAHI environment chapter standards; medical device vendors: CBAHI biomedical equipment requirements",Accreditation denial: cannot be MOH referral centre; excluded from major insurance networks; reputational damage. Accreditation suspension: immediate revenue impact from insurance exclusion. MOH can condition facility licence on CBAHI compliance,Healthcare & Life Sciences,Sector-Specific,,,,,
328,RHC2,Riyadh Health Cluster 2,17. Regional Health Clusters,NCA-ECC-2:2024,Essential Cybersecurity Controls,ضوابط الأمن السيبراني الأساسية,ECC-2:2024,2018,108,MUST COMPLY WITH,Mandatory Complier,Riyadh Health Cluster 2 as government entity — hospital networks,HIGHEST,https://nca.gov.sa/,NCA ECC scope,See official source for version history,Framework issued by the relevant authority. Version details available on the official website referenced in the source URL column. The framework has been updated periodically since first issuance.,See official source,Updated as needed by the issuing authority,CURRENT — consult official source for latest version,Penalties defined by issuing authority; consult official source. NCA ECC-2 penalties apply if relevant,Issuing authority and/or designated inspection department,As specified in framework scope — consult official document,Both (unless specified otherwise in official document),As defined by issuing authority,As defined by issuing authority,To be determined from framework text — see official source,See framework preamble for international standard references,Arabic (primary in KSA); English may be available,Mapped to relevant GH-ICGA layer — requires manual assessment,See official framework for maturity model details,Aligned with relevant Vision 2030 programme — see framework context,As specified in framework compliance requirements,Institutional theory and regulatory governance theory underpin all KSA regulatory frameworks. Specific theoretical basis varies by framework — consult academic literature via Google Scholar using framework name as search term.,"Academic debate varies by framework maturity and international adoption. For KSA-specific debate, consult: Almohammad & Wills (2021) J Cybersecurity; Al-Khater et al. (2020) IEEE Access; Baker McKenzie KSA regulatory updates.",Known criticisms include: implementation gap between standards and practice; resource constraints for smaller healthcare entities; rapid technology evolution outpacing regulation. Consult framework evaluation literature for specifics.,"Framework comparison requires contextual analysis. Key comparators: NIST CSF 2.0 (international benchmark), ISO 27001 (certifiable standard), ECC-2 (KSA mandatory baseline). See main database for cross-framework mapping.","Key foundational references: Almohammad A & Wills G (2021) J Cybersecurity; NIST (2024) CSF 2.0; ISO (2022) 27001; NCA official documents (nca.gov.sa); Baker McKenzie Global Data & Cyber Handbook 2025. Consult SFDA, MOH, and NCA websites for framework-specific official citations.","Official: Consult the official source URL in the database for the primary document. Training: ISACA, ISC2, RAPS, IAPP, and HIMSS offer professional development covering most regulatory domains. Estimated hours vary by framework complexity.",Relevance to GH-ICGA framework varies by layer — see GH-ICGA Layer Mapping column. All KSA mandatory frameworks are compliance inputs to GH-ICGA Layer 4. Frameworks with IoMT-specific provisions directly inform Layers 2 and 3.,G + C (Governance + Compliance — standard regulatory framework positioning),Senior management: accountability for framework compliance. Designated compliance officer: monitor requirements. All staff: awareness of applicable obligations,Regulatory Risk; Operational Risk; Reputational Risk — specific domains determined by framework scope,"See official source — legal obligation, regulatory requirement, or voluntary standard as applicable",Administrative; Technical; Operational — specific distribution determined by framework content,1. Compliance status (compliant/non-compliant). 2. # open findings from last assessment. 3. % controls implemented. 4. Time to remediate findings. 5. Incident rate in framework scope area,Self-assessment; internal audit; external assessment as specified by issuing authority,ServiceNow GRC; IBM OpenPages; RSA Archer; MetricStream — all major GRC platforms support custom framework import. Contact vendor for framework-specific module,Third parties in scope must demonstrate equivalent compliance. Contractual compliance obligations; right-to-audit clauses; annual third-party assessment,Non-compliance: regulatory action by issuing authority; fines and penalties as specified; operational and reputational consequences,ALL 17 SECTORS,YES — ALL SECTORS,,,,,
329,RHC2,Riyadh Health Cluster 2,17. Regional Health Clusters,PDPL-2021,Personal Data Protection Law,نظام حماية البيانات الشخصية,2021+IR 2023,2021,45,MUST COMPLY WITH,Mandatory Complier,Riyadh Health Cluster 2 processes patient personal data,HIGHEST,https://sdaia.gov.sa/,SDAIA,Royal Decree M/19 (16 Sep 2021) → amended Apr 2023,"Original (2021): 19 articles covering consent, data subject rights, controller obligations, sensitive data (health = sensitive), cross-border transfer. Apr 2023 amendment: strengthened enforcement, clarified health data provisions, aligned with PDPL-IR-2023. Grace period for compliance ended Sep 14, 2024. Penalties: up to SAR 5 million fine + 2 years imprisonment for sensitive data breach","Issued: Sep 2021. Amended: Apr 2023. Fully enforceable: Sep 14, 2024",SDAIA reviews annually; amendments expected 2025-2026,CURRENT — fully enforceable since Sep 2024,Criminal offence: up to 2 years imprisonment + SAR 3M fine for disclosing sensitive data (health data = sensitive); administrative fine up to SAR 5M; mandatory breach notification to SDAIA within 72 hours; data subject compensation claims,SDAIA — Data Protection Authority (DPA functions); NDMO for government entities; courts for criminal penalties,Hospital; Clinic; Lab; Pharmacy; Health IT Vendor; Health Insurer; Government Health Entity; Medical Device Manufacturer (all processing patient data),Both + foreign entities processing data of KSA residents,SDAIA audit; controller self-assessment; DPO oversight; data subject complaints trigger investigation,Continuous (breach notification: 72 hours); annual DPO report; SDAIA random audits,12 (IoMT data minimisation; purpose limitation; consent management for wearable data; automated decision-making; data subject rights for IoMT-generated health data; cross-border transfer of IoMT data),GDPR (substantial alignment — adequate protection basis); APEC Privacy Framework; ISO 29100; ISO 27701,Arabic (official); English translation (unofficial),Layer 4: Compliance; Layer 2: Risk Management,No formal maturity model; SDAIA publishes compliance guidance,National Data Strategy; Vision 2030 Digital Economy; Digital Government transformation,Privacy notices; consent records; DPO appointment letter; data mapping/flow diagrams; breach notification records; DPIA for health data processing; controller registration,Privacy as fundamental right (Warren & Brandeis 1890; Westin 1967). Contextual integrity theory (Nissenbaum 2004): health data flows must match medical context norms. Regulatory theory: data protection authority independence and enforcement. Information asymmetry theory: patients cannot protect their own data without regulation.,"FOR: Long-overdue — KSA had no comprehensive data protection law until 2021; health data explicitly protected as sensitive category; aligns KSA with global adequacy standards. AGAINST: Alrawabdeh (2014) and Aldawood & Skinner (2019) documented Saudi privacy culture gap — regulation without public awareness is ineffective; 'GDPR-lite' criticism: exemptions broader than GDPR, enforcement weaker; health data provisions in IR still less specific than UK GDPR Schedule 1 conditions",No independent supervisory authority (SDAIA is both promoter and regulator — conflict of interest noted by Baker McKenzie 2025); patient data portability rights weaker than GDPR Article 20; no explicit right to explanation for AI-automated health decisions; cross-border transfer rules changed twice in 12 months (regulatory instability); small healthcare providers lack capacity for DPO requirements,"PDPL vs GDPR: PDPL penalty lower (SAR 5M vs €20M); PDPL broader government exemptions; GDPR has independent DPA, PDPL does not. PDPL vs HIPAA: PDPL = all personal data (broader); HIPAA = US-specific health data only. PDPL vs UK GDPR: PDPL misses explicit Schedule 1 health data conditions. PDPL ahead of HIPAA on consent granularity; behind GDPR on enforcement independence",Baker McKenzie (2025) Saudi Arabia PDPL Global Data & Cyber Handbook; Alrawabdeh W (2014) 'Internet and Social Media Challenges in Arab World' EJSR; Westin AF (1967) Privacy and Freedom NY Atheneum; Nissenbaum H (2004) 'Privacy as Contextual Integrity' Washington Law Review 79(1); CMS Law (2025) Saudi Arabia Data Protection & Cybersecurity Expert Guide Oct 2025,Official: SDAIA website (sdaia.gov.sa) — Arabic + English. Training: IAPP CIPM/CIPP/E (privacy certifications applicable); OneTrust PDPL compliance course; Baker McKenzie PDPL compliance guide. Language: Arabic primary — strong Arabic reading recommended. Estimated: 50 hours for health sector DPO competence,PRIMARY driver for GH-ICGA Layer 4 (Compliance) — PDPL is the legal foundation for patient data governance in the preliminary GH-ICGA framework. Thesis argument: GH-ICGA Layer 4 operationalises PDPL requirements into IoMT-specific controls (consent management for wearable data; purpose limitation for monitoring streams; breach response for connected device incidents),G + C (Governance + Compliance — strong compliance driver; risk implicit),Board/CEO: ultimate accountability for PDPL compliance. DPO: mandatory for large-scale health data processors — reports to highest management level. Privacy Committee: cross-functional oversight. All staff processing personal data: mandatory privacy awareness training annually,Privacy Risk (PRIMARY); Legal/Regulatory Risk; Reputational Risk; Strategic Risk (data monetisation constraints); Cross-Border Risk (data transfer restrictions),LEGAL — Royal Decree M/19 2021 + Implementing Regulations Sep 2023. Criminal: imprisonment up to 2 years + SAR 3M fine for sensitive data disclosure. Administrative: SAR 5M maximum fine. Civil: data subject compensation claims,Administrative (A): consent management; privacy notices; DPO appointment; DPIA process; breach notification procedure. Technical (T): data minimisation; pseudonymisation; access controls for personal data; encryption. Operational (O): data retention enforcement; data subject rights fulfilment process; cross-border transfer review,1. # data subject rights requests fulfilled on time (target: 100% within 30 days). 2. # data breaches notified to SDAIA within 72h (target: 100%). 3. % staff completed privacy training (target: 100%). 4. # DPIAs completed for high-risk processing activities. 5. Data retention compliance rate (% records within policy). 6. % third-party processors with DPA agreements,Annual DPIA review; data mapping audit (controller record validation); consent record audit; breach response simulation (tabletop); access control review for personal data systems; third-party processor audit; SDAIA compliance self-assessment,OneTrust (market leader for PDPL); TrustArc; Nymity; DataGrail; Varonis (data discovery); Microsoft Purview (data classification). SDAIA National Data Governance Platform for registration. Arabic-capable tools preferred,All third-party data processors must have Data Processing Agreement (DPA). Processors must demonstrate equivalent PDPL compliance. Cross-border transfers require adequacy assessment or appropriate safeguards. Sub-processor disclosure mandatory. Annual processor risk assessment required,Non-compliance: SAR 5M administrative fine; criminal prosecution for sensitive data breach; SDAIA investigation; mandatory breach public disclosure. Business disruption: SDAIA can order processing suspension. Reputational: patient trust damage. Market: EU adequate protection status depends on PDPL enforcement credibility,ALL 17 SECTORS — any entity processing personal data,YES — ALL SECTORS,,,,,
330,RHC2,Riyadh Health Cluster 2,17. Regional Health Clusters,MOH-TELE,Telemedicine Regulations,لائحة الرعاية الصحية عن بعد,Late 2023,2020,38,MUST COMPLY WITH,Mandatory Complier,Riyadh Health Cluster 2 offering telemedicine services,HIGH,https://www.moh.gov.sa/en/,MOH; Hammad & Al-Mehdar 2025,COVID-19 emergency 2020 → comprehensive regulations late 2023,"2020 (emergency COVID telehealth rules): basic platform licensing, identity verification, audio/video minimum standards. Late 2023 (comprehensive update, 38 controls): platform licensing requirements; patient identity verification; cross-border telemedicine restrictions; controlled substance prescribing rules (limited); data protection (PDPL alignment); patient consent requirements; clinical documentation standards; coverage by health insurance; quality assurance audit requirements",Emergency 2020. Comprehensive: late 2023,Annual update; cross-border rules under active development 2025,CURRENT — late 2023 comprehensive version,Platform licence revocation; SAR 1M–5M fine (scale by offence); clinical professional disciplinary action by SCFHS,MOH — eHealth General Directorate; CST (platform technical licensing); CCHI (insurance coverage compliance); SCFHS (clinical standards),Hospital (outpatient teleconsultation); Clinic; Health IT Vendor (platform developers); Health Insurer (reimbursement compliance),Both,MOH platform licence audit (annual); CCHI insurance compliance check; SCFHS clinical quality audit,Annual licence renewal; random clinical quality audit,15 (remote patient monitoring integration; wearable device data for teleconsultation; IoMT vital sign display during video consultation; remote device prescription; teleICU monitoring requirements),WHO Telemedicine Guidelines (2010/updated); ISO 13131:2021 (Telehealth); ATA (American Telemedicine Association) standards (reference),Arabic (primary); English technical guidance,Layer 1: Governance; Layer 4: Compliance,No formal maturity model; licence tiers (basic/advanced platform),HSTP telemedicine targets (30% uptake); SEHA Virtual Hospital; Vision 2030 healthcare access targets,Platform licence certificate; patient consent records; clinical documentation standards compliance; identity verification log; prescription records; NABIDH integration evidence,Telehealth adoption theory (LeRouge & Garfield 2013): patient acceptance + clinician integration. Access theory: geography-equity argument for remote care. Digital health governance theory: platform regulation vs clinical regulation tension.,FOR: COVID-19 proved telehealth viability at scale in KSA (Al-Ataawi et al. 2022); Sehhaty platform shows government commitment; removes geographic barriers consistent with Vision 2030 equity goals; IoMT remote monitoring reimbursement framework emerging. AGAINST: Clinical quality in telehealth still debated (Hollander & Sites 2020); data sovereignty for cross-border telehealth unresolved; prescription via teleconsultation remains restricted limiting scope; rural connectivity gaps undermine access equity goal,Regulations still lag technology — VR/AR clinical applications not addressed; AI-assisted teleconsultation regulatory pathway unclear; cross-border telehealth restrictions create market fragmentation; no minimum quality standards for audio/video equipment; liability for technical failure not yet codified in regulation,MOH-TELE vs NHS TECS (UK): NHS TECS has DTAC (Digital Technology Assessment Criteria) for all digital health tools — KSA equivalent absent. MOH-TELE vs FCC/CMS telehealth (US): US has federal reimbursement codes (CPT); KSA CCHI integration still developing. MOH-TELE vs WHO Telemedicine Guidelines: broadly aligned but KSA-specific cross-border rules more restrictive than WHO recommends,Al-Ataawi A et al. (2022) 'Telehealth in Saudi Arabia During COVID-19' J Healthcare Informatics Res; Hollander JE & Sites FD (2020) 'Telehealth Beyond COVID-19' NEJM 2020; Hammad & Al-Mehdar (2025) Saudi Healthcare Regulatory Update; WHO (2010) Telemedicine Opportunities and Developments; LeRouge C & Garfield MJ (2013) 'Crossing the Telemedicine Chasm' JAMIA 20(6),Official: MOH website. Training: American Telemedicine Association (ATA) online courses; SEHA Virtual Hospital training programme; WHO telehealth competency framework. Estimated: 25 hours,GH-ICGA Layer 1 (Governance) — telehealth regulation defines the governance boundary for remote IoMT monitoring. Thesis relevance: GH-ICGA must address the governance overlap between MOH-TELE (clinical platform regulation) and NCA-ECC (cybersecurity) and PDPL (patient data) for remote monitoring scenarios,G + C (platform governance + clinical compliance),CEO: platform licence accountability. Medical Director: clinical quality governance. DPO: PDPL compliance for patient data on platform. Technical Director: platform security. Pharmacy Director: prescription management compliance,Clinical Risk (misdiagnosis via teleconsultation); Regulatory Risk (licence breach); Privacy Risk (patient data); Operational Risk (platform availability); Cross-Border Risk (international teleconsultation restrictions),REGULATORY — MOH licensing requirement. Non-compliance: platform licence revocation; SAR 1M–5M fine; SCFHS action against clinicians,Administrative (A): 20 controls — licensing; consent; documentation; clinical protocols. Technical (T): 12 controls — platform security; identity verification; audio/video standards. Operational (O): 6 controls — prescribing workflow; referral protocol; audit trail,"1. Patient consent collection rate (target 100% before each session). 2. Platform availability SLA compliance (target >99.5%). 3. Identity verification success rate. 4. # clinical complaints per 1,000 teleconsultations. 5. Documentation compliance rate (target 100% consultation notes in NABIDH). 6. Prescription audit pass rate",Annual MOH licence renewal audit; CCHI insurance coverage compliance check; patient record documentation audit; platform security assessment; identity verification system test; NABIDH integration test,Sehhaty / Mawid (MOH official platforms); Nabdh; Cura Health; Vezeeta; Nubaqraa — all require MOH licensing. Security tools: FortiWeb (WAF); Auth0/Okta (identity); TeleVisit security frameworks,Platform vendors: MOH-licensed telemedicine solution mandatory; PDPL DPA required; NABIDH API certification; patient data not to be shared with third-party analytics without consent; international teleconsultation providers must be MOH-approved,Unlicensed platform: immediate shutdown; SAR fine; clinical professional disciplinary action. Licence revocation: complete business cessation. Patient harm via telemedicine: malpractice liability; SCFHS investigation,Healthcare & Life Sciences; ICT/Digital (platform providers),Sector-Specific,,,,,
331,RHC2,Riyadh Health Cluster 2,17. Regional Health Clusters,NHIC-NABIDH,NABIDH EHR Platform Standard,معيار منصة نبض,Mandatory 2024,2018,40,MUST COMPLY WITH,Mandatory Complier,Riyadh Health Cluster 2 hospitals — NABIDH interoperability,HIGHEST,https://nhic.gov.sa/,NHIC,NABIDH launched 2018 (pilot) → FHIR R4 API 2022 → mandatory all facilities 2024,2018 (pilot): 10 MOH hospitals. 2019-2021: health cluster rollout. 2022: FHIR R4 API standards published; private hospitals voluntary integration. 2023: API developer portal opened; 40 technical requirements published. 2024: MANDATORY — all healthcare facilities must connect; Private Health Institutions Law updated; penalties for non-connection: facility licensing at risk. NABIDH now the central patient data repository — 45M+ patient records as of 2024,Launched: 2018. FHIR R4 API: 2022. Mandatory: 2024,Continuous platform updates; API versioning quarterly,CURRENT — mandatory as of 2024,Facility unable to receive electronic referrals; excluded from national patient record; MOH licensing risk; procurement penalties,NHIC — NABIDH Programme Office; MOH General Directorate of Health Informatics; regional health clusters,Hospital; Clinic; Lab; Pharmacy — ALL healthcare facilities mandatory,Both,NHIC API conformance test; connectivity monitoring (automated); annual technical review,Continuous connectivity monitoring; annual audit,16 (NABIDH IoMT data ingestion API; remote monitoring data submission; wearable vital signs standardisation; IoMT device data consent management; IoMT-generated observation resources in FHIR),HL7 FHIR R4 (mandatory); IHE profiles; SNOMED CT; ICD-10-CM; LOINC; HL7 CDA,Arabic + English (NABIDH technical specifications bilingual),Layer 3: Technical Controls; Layer 4: Compliance; Layer 5: Assurance,Yes — 3 connectivity levels (Basic data exchange / Full longitudinal record / Advanced IoMT integration),HSTP digital health; National EHR programme; NHIS integration; Vision 2030 connected healthcare,NABIDH connectivity certificate; API test results; FHIR conformance statement; patient consent records; data quality dashboard reports,Institutional theory and regulatory governance theory underpin all KSA regulatory frameworks. Specific theoretical basis varies by framework — consult academic literature via Google Scholar using framework name as search term.,"Academic debate varies by framework maturity and international adoption. For KSA-specific debate, consult: Almohammad & Wills (2021) J Cybersecurity; Al-Khater et al. (2020) IEEE Access; Baker McKenzie KSA regulatory updates.",Known criticisms include: implementation gap between standards and practice; resource constraints for smaller healthcare entities; rapid technology evolution outpacing regulation. Consult framework evaluation literature for specifics.,"Framework comparison requires contextual analysis. Key comparators: NIST CSF 2.0 (international benchmark), ISO 27001 (certifiable standard), ECC-2 (KSA mandatory baseline). See main database for cross-framework mapping.","Key foundational references: Almohammad A & Wills G (2021) J Cybersecurity; NIST (2024) CSF 2.0; ISO (2022) 27001; NCA official documents (nca.gov.sa); Baker McKenzie Global Data & Cyber Handbook 2025. Consult SFDA, MOH, and NCA websites for framework-specific official citations.","Official: Consult the official source URL in the database for the primary document. Training: ISACA, ISC2, RAPS, IAPP, and HIMSS offer professional development covering most regulatory domains. Estimated hours vary by framework complexity.",Relevance to GH-ICGA framework varies by layer — see GH-ICGA Layer Mapping column. All KSA mandatory frameworks are compliance inputs to GH-ICGA Layer 4. Frameworks with IoMT-specific provisions directly inform Layers 2 and 3.,G + C (Governance + Compliance — standard regulatory framework positioning),Senior management: accountability for framework compliance. Designated compliance officer: monitor requirements. All staff: awareness of applicable obligations,Regulatory Risk; Operational Risk; Reputational Risk — specific domains determined by framework scope,"See official source — legal obligation, regulatory requirement, or voluntary standard as applicable",Administrative; Technical; Operational — specific distribution determined by framework content,1. Compliance status (compliant/non-compliant). 2. # open findings from last assessment. 3. % controls implemented. 4. Time to remediate findings. 5. Incident rate in framework scope area,Self-assessment; internal audit; external assessment as specified by issuing authority,ServiceNow GRC; IBM OpenPages; RSA Archer; MetricStream — all major GRC platforms support custom framework import. Contact vendor for framework-specific module,Third parties in scope must demonstrate equivalent compliance. Contractual compliance obligations; right-to-audit clauses; annual third-party assessment,Non-compliance: regulatory action by issuing authority; fines and penalties as specified; operational and reputational consequences,Healthcare & Life Sciences,Sector-Specific,,,,,
332,RHC2,Riyadh Health Cluster 2,17. Regional Health Clusters,NCA-OTCC,OT Cybersecurity Controls,ضوابط الأمن السيبراني للتقنيات التشغيلية,OTCC-1:2022,2022,53,MUST COMPLY WITH,Mandatory Complier,Riyadh Health Cluster 2 medical device OT networks — CNI designation,HIGHEST,https://nca.gov.sa/,NCA OTCC,See official source for version history,Framework issued by the relevant authority. Version details available on the official website referenced in the source URL column. The framework has been updated periodically since first issuance.,See official source,Updated as needed by the issuing authority,CURRENT — consult official source for latest version,Penalties defined by issuing authority; consult official source. NCA ECC-2 penalties apply if relevant,Issuing authority and/or designated inspection department,As specified in framework scope — consult official document,Both (unless specified otherwise in official document),As defined by issuing authority,As defined by issuing authority,To be determined from framework text — see official source,See framework preamble for international standard references,Arabic (primary in KSA); English may be available,Mapped to relevant GH-ICGA layer — requires manual assessment,See official framework for maturity model details,Aligned with relevant Vision 2030 programme — see framework context,As specified in framework compliance requirements,Institutional theory and regulatory governance theory underpin all KSA regulatory frameworks. Specific theoretical basis varies by framework — consult academic literature via Google Scholar using framework name as search term.,"Academic debate varies by framework maturity and international adoption. For KSA-specific debate, consult: Almohammad & Wills (2021) J Cybersecurity; Al-Khater et al. (2020) IEEE Access; Baker McKenzie KSA regulatory updates.",Known criticisms include: implementation gap between standards and practice; resource constraints for smaller healthcare entities; rapid technology evolution outpacing regulation. Consult framework evaluation literature for specifics.,"Framework comparison requires contextual analysis. Key comparators: NIST CSF 2.0 (international benchmark), ISO 27001 (certifiable standard), ECC-2 (KSA mandatory baseline). See main database for cross-framework mapping.","Key foundational references: Almohammad A & Wills G (2021) J Cybersecurity; NIST (2024) CSF 2.0; ISO (2022) 27001; NCA official documents (nca.gov.sa); Baker McKenzie Global Data & Cyber Handbook 2025. Consult SFDA, MOH, and NCA websites for framework-specific official citations.","Official: Consult the official source URL in the database for the primary document. Training: ISACA, ISC2, RAPS, IAPP, and HIMSS offer professional development covering most regulatory domains. Estimated hours vary by framework complexity.",Relevance to GH-ICGA framework varies by layer — see GH-ICGA Layer Mapping column. All KSA mandatory frameworks are compliance inputs to GH-ICGA Layer 4. Frameworks with IoMT-specific provisions directly inform Layers 2 and 3.,R + C (Risk and Compliance — technically oriented; governance implied),CISO/OT Security Manager: own OTCC compliance. Clinical Engineering/Biomedical Engineering: co-responsible for medical device OT compliance. CTO: responsible for OT architecture. Board: informed of OT risk posture annually. OT security roles: Saudi nationals required per ECC-2 Saudisation mandate,Cyber Risk (OT/ICS); Operational Risk (process disruption); Physical Safety Risk (clinical impact of OT compromise); Supply Chain Risk (OT vendor remote access); Availability Risk (OT always-on requirement),LEGAL — Mandatory for CNI OT operators under NCA-REG-2024. Hospitals as CNI: healthcare OT networks are in scope. Penalties: NCA-REG-2024 (SAR 25M max) + potential operational shutdown of non-compliant OT systems,Technical (T): 40 controls — OT network segmentation; secure remote access; OT monitoring; patch management for ICS. Administrative (A): 8 controls — OT governance; vendor management; OT incident response. Operational (O): 5 controls — OT change management; OT BCP,1. % OT assets inventoried (target 100%). 2. % OT systems with current patches within defined window. 3. # OT network zone violations detected. 4. % third-party OT vendor accesses logged. 5. OT system availability rate (target >99.9% for life-critical). 6. Time-to-detect OT anomaly (target <15 min for CNI),"OT vulnerability scan (passive — non-disruptive tools: Claroty, Nozomi, Dragos); OT network traffic analysis; OT penetration test (annual — must not disrupt clinical operations); vendor remote access audit; zone/conduit diagram validation",Claroty (medical IoT OT visibility); Nozomi Networks; Dragos; Tenable.ot; Fortinet OT; Medigate (healthcare-specific OT). For GRC integration: ServiceNow OT module; IBM QRadar SIEM with OT signatures,All OT vendors with remote access: mandatory access logging; time-limited access; multi-factor authentication; vendor security assessment before engagement; NDA for OT architecture access; OT vendor incident notification within 4 hours,"OT breach in hospital: potential life-critical impact (ventilator, infusion pump disruption); NCA shutdown order; SAR 25M fine; criminal referral; SFDA recall if device compromise detected; massive reputational and legal exposure",Sector-specific — consult official source,Sector-Specific,,,,,
333,JDHC,Jeddah Health Cluster,17. Regional Health Clusters,MOH-HIS,Health Information System Standards,معايير نظم المعلومات الصحية,2024 update,2010,112,MUST COMPLY WITH,Mandatory Complier,Jeddah Health Cluster hospitals — mandatory HIS compliance,HIGHEST,https://www.moh.gov.sa/en/,MOH official,First issued ~2010 → comprehensive update 2022 → mandatory EMR update 2024,"Progressive standards covering: 2010 (basic EHR requirements); 2015 (interoperability standards added); 2018 (NABIDH integration); 2020 (mobile health and telemedicine modules); 2022 (comprehensive 112-control update covering clinical systems, imaging, medication management, emergency, genomics, training); 2024 (mandatory EMR adoption for all MOH and private facilities aligned with NABIDH)",First: 2010. Major update: 2022. EMR mandate: 2024,Updated with NABIDH platform releases; typically annual minor updates,CURRENT — 2024 version mandatory,MOH facility licensing at risk; mandatory corrective action plan; financial penalties under Private Health Institutions Law; non-compliant systems barred from NABIDH connectivity,MOH — Directorate of Health Information (DHI); regional health cluster inspection teams; NHIC for interoperability compliance,Hospital; Clinic; Lab; Pharmacy (hospital); Government Health Entity,Both (mandatory for MOH facilities; mandatory for private facilities since 2024 PHILAW update),MOH annual IT audit; NHIC interoperability assessment; CBAHI HIT standards review during accreditation,Annual MOH audit; NABIDH connectivity continuous monitoring,28 (medical device integration into HIS; alarm management; device data capture; IoMT workflow integration; remote monitoring data ingestion; device incident reporting to HIS),HL7 FHIR R4 (interoperability); IHE profiles (integration); ISO 27799:2016 (health ISMS); DICOM (imaging),Arabic (primary); English technical annexes,Layer 1: Governance; Layer 3: Technical Controls; Layer 4: Compliance,Yes — 4 implementation levels (Basic / Intermediate / Advanced / Optimised); MOH tracks facility scores,HSTP digital health; NABIDH national EHR; Vision 2030 healthcare quality targets,HIS system documentation; NABIDH connectivity certificate; interoperability test results; staff training records; system access audit logs; downtime contingency procedure,Health informatics systems theory (Shortliffe & Cimino 2006): HIS as sociotechnical infrastructure. Interoperability theory (Kuperman 2011): semantic/syntactic/organisational interoperability levels. Technology adoption theory (TAM — Davis 1989): clinician adoption barriers to HIS.,FOR: National standardisation essential for patient safety continuity (WHO 2019); NABIDH integration creates longitudinal patient record unavailable previously; IoMT data integration requirements push hospitals toward modern system architectures. AGAINST: Ajami & Bagheri-Tadi (2013) and Aloudah et al. (2021) document implementation-reality gap in Saudi HIS — standards exist but compliance varies widely by facility tier; urban-rural HIS capacity gap poorly addressed; cybersecurity requirements within HIS standards historically weak (pre-NABIDH),112 controls create compliance burden without proportionality guidance; small private clinics face disproportionate compliance cost; no formal HIS certification programme — self-declaration accepted; IoMT data standards not harmonised with international HL7 FHIR profiles; historical emphasis on data capture over data quality,MOH-HIS vs NHS Digital standards (UK): NHS has legally binding DCB standards with enforcement; MOH-HIS is ministerial directive with softer enforcement. MOH-HIS vs ONC Health IT Certification (US): US has federal certification; KSA moving toward certification (NABIDH conformance). MOH-HIS + NHIC-NABIDH = closest to ONC approach in KSA,Ajami S & Bagheri-Tadi T (2013) 'Barriers for Adopting Electronic Health Records' MateriaSociomed 25(3); Aloudah N et al. (2021) 'Digital Health in Saudi Arabia' Sustainability 13(12); Shortliffe EH & Cimino JJ (2006) Biomedical Informatics Springer; WHO (2019) Classification of Digital Interventions; Alotaibi YK & Federico F (2017) 'Impact of Health IT on Patient Safety' Saudi Medical J 38(12),Official: MOH website (moh.gov.sa). Training: CPHIMS (Certified Professional Health Informatics & Information Management); HL7 FHIR Foundational (HL7.org); NHIC developer portal training (nhic.gov.sa). Language: Arabic primary. Estimated: 45 hours,GH-ICGA Layer 1 (Governance) + Layer 3 (Technical Controls) — MOH-HIS is the clinical data governance backbone that the preliminary GH-ICGA IoMT framework must integrate with. Thesis: GH-ICGA IoMT data model must align with HIS data standards to ensure IoMT device data flows into the national health record coherently,G + R + C (all three — clinical information governance foundation),Hospital CEO: accountable for HIS compliance. CIO/CMIO: responsible for HIS implementation and NABIDH connectivity. Clinical Informatics Committee: govern HIS standards adoption. Department Heads: responsible for clinical data quality in their systems,Operational Risk (HIS downtime = clinical risk); Patient Safety Risk; Data Quality Risk; Regulatory Risk (MOH non-compliance); Interoperability Risk (NABIDH disconnection),REGULATORY — MOH ministerial directive. Mandatory for all licensed facilities. Non-compliance risks licensing. Enforced through MOH annual audit and NABIDH connectivity monitoring,Technical (T): 68 controls — system architecture; integration; security; availability; backup. Administrative (A): 28 controls — data governance; policies; training; documentation. Operational (O): 16 controls — downtime procedures; change management; vendor management,1. NABIDH connectivity status (connected/disconnected). 2. HIS downtime incidents per month (target <4h/month). 3. Data quality score (completeness/accuracy) from NABIDH reports. 4. % clinical staff trained on HIS (target 100%). 5. Time-to-restore HIS after failure (target <4h). 6. # HIS security incidents per quarter,Annual MOH HIS audit (documentation + system walk-through); NABIDH API conformance test (NHIC automated); data quality audit; HIS security assessment; downtime procedure simulation; user access review,Epic; Cerner (Oracle Health); Philips Tasy EMR; Meditech; InterSystems HealthShare — all NABIDH-integrated. For compliance: ServiceNow Healthcare; Qualio (quality management); NHIC developer portal for FHIR conformance testing,HIS vendors: must be MOH-approved/NABIDH-certified; contractual data protection obligations; source code escrow for critical systems; SLA for 99.9% uptime; mandatory SFDA notification if HIS failure affects medical devices; annual security assessment of HIS vendors,"Non-compliance: MOH corrective action; facility licence conditions; NABIDH disconnection (loss of referrals, national patient record access); PHILAW 2024 penalties for private facilities. Clinical impact: patient safety risk from poor data quality or HIS unavailability",Healthcare & Life Sciences,Sector-Specific,,,,,
334,JDHC,Jeddah Health Cluster,17. Regional Health Clusters,MOH-EMR-NABIDH,EMR Standards / NABIDH,معايير السجل الصحي الإلكتروني / نبض,Mandatory 2024,2010,52,MUST COMPLY WITH,Mandatory Complier,Jeddah Health Cluster — mandatory NABIDH connectivity 2024,HIGHEST,https://www.moh.gov.sa/en/,MOH/NHIC,NABIDH platform launched ~2018 → mandatory EMR adoption for all facilities 2024,"NABIDH (National Unified EHR Platform): v1 2018 (pilot MOH hospitals); 2019-2021 (rollout to health clusters); 2022 (private hospital voluntary integration); 2023 (API standards published based on FHIR R4); 2024 (mandatory for ALL healthcare facilities per Private Health Institutions Law update — complete patient longitudinal record across facilities). EMR standard covers 52 functional requirements including: CPOE, medication reconciliation, imaging integration, patient portal, audit logs",NABIDH launched: 2018. Mandatory for all: 2024,Continuously updated with NABIDH platform releases,CURRENT — mandatory since 2024,Facility licensing revocation; MOH enforcement action; barred from government referrals; penalties under PHILAW 2024,MOH — NABIDH Programme Office; NHIC; regional health clusters monitor connectivity,Hospital; Clinic; Lab; Pharmacy; Government Health Entity — ALL healthcare facilities mandatory,Both (mandatory since 2024 PHILAW update for all facilities),NHIC connectivity assessment; NABIDH API conformance testing; MOH annual audit,Continuous (NABIDH connectivity monitored in real time); annual formal audit,22 (IoMT data integration to NABIDH: wearable data ingestion API; remote monitoring feeds; device-generated vital signs; medical device alerts to patient record; IoMT consent management in EHR),HL7 FHIR R4; IHE (Integrating the Healthcare Enterprise); SNOMED CT; ICD-10; LOINC,Arabic + English (NABIDH technical specifications bilingual),Layer 1: Governance; Layer 3: Technical Controls; Layer 4: Compliance; Layer 5: Assurance,Yes — NABIDH connectivity levels (1: Basic data exchange; 