MOH-EMR-NABIDH,EMR Standards / NABIDH Platform,NABIDH platform launched ~2018 → mandatory EMR adoption for all facilities 2024,"NABIDH (National Unified EHR Platform): v1 2018 (pilot MOH hospitals); 2019-2021 (rollout to health clusters); 2022 (private hospital voluntary integration); 2023 (API standards published based on FHIR R4); 2024 (mandatory for ALL healthcare facilities per Private Health Institutions Law update — complete patient longitudinal record across facilities). EMR standard covers 52 functional requirements including: CPOE, medication reconciliation, imaging integration, patient portal, audit logs",NABIDH launched: 2018. Mandatory for all: 2024,Continuously updated with NABIDH platform releases,CURRENT — mandatory since 2024
MOH-HAJHLT,Hajj & Umrah Health Standards,Annual issuance since 2000; updated every year before Hajj season,"Annual standards updated based on prior year experience and WHO mass-gathering guidance. 2022 added COVID-19 health screening protocols. 2023-2024: IoT crowd monitoring standards (wearable health monitors for high-risk pilgrims), real-time disease surveillance system integration with WHO. Covers: vaccination requirements, emergency response, disease surveillance, mass casualty management, coordination with WHO GOARN",Annual issuance since 2000; 2024 version current,Issued annually before Hajj season (approx. March-April each year),CURRENT — 2024 version (annual)
MOH-HIS,Health Information System Standards,First issued ~2010 → comprehensive update 2022 → mandatory EMR update 2024,"Progressive standards covering: 2010 (basic EHR requirements); 2015 (interoperability standards added); 2018 (NABIDH integration); 2020 (mobile health and telemedicine modules); 2022 (comprehensive 112-control update covering clinical systems, imaging, medication management, emergency, genomics, training); 2024 (mandatory EMR adoption for all MOH and private facilities aligned with NABIDH)",First: 2010. Major update: 2022. EMR mandate: 2024,Updated with NABIDH platform releases; typically annual minor updates,CURRENT — 2024 version mandatory
MOH-HL,Health Law,Royal Decree M/11 1949 (foundational) → multiple Royal Decrees updating through 2024,Foundational health law establishing MOH mandate. Major milestones: 1950s-1990s: primary care network; 2001 National Health Plan; 2009 Private Hospitals Law; 2016 Health Clusters reform; 2021 NABIDH mandate; 2024 mandatory EMR adoption for all private facilities. Constitutes the overarching legal basis for all MOH regulations,Original: 1949. Continuously updated by Royal Decrees,Ongoing — amendments via Royal Decree,CURRENT — foundational law with continuous amendments
MOH-NHIS,National Health Insurance Standards,National Health Insurance System rollout 2024 onwards (in development),"Universal health insurance being rolled out under Vision 2030. Phase 1 (2024): government employees and dependants. Phase 2: private sector employees. Phase 3: citizens. 30 coverage standards include digital claims, e-prior authorisation, pharmacy integration with NABIDH. Coordinates with CCHI for private sector",Development: 2022-2024. Rollout: 2024 onwards,Progressive rollout; full implementation target 2030,IN DEVELOPMENT — phased rollout 2024-2030
MOH-PHILAW,Private Health Institutions Law,Original ~1990 → updated 2009 → major update 2024 mandating digital health,2009 update: private hospital licensing criteria. 2024 major update: mandatory EMR adoption for all private facilities; mandatory NABIDH connectivity; minimum cybersecurity standards for private health facilities (aligned with NCA ECC-2); mandatory health professional licensing verification through SCFHS; civil money penalties for non-compliance. Significant governance shift for private health sector,"Original: ~1990. Updated: 2009, 2024",Next review 2026-2027,CURRENT — 2024 version
MOH-PR,Patient Rights Charter,First Charter ~2012 → updated 2019,"45 rights and obligations. Key provisions: informed consent (including electronic consent for telemedicine), right to access medical records (digital access through NABIDH patient portal), right to privacy of health information, right to refuse treatment, right to second opinion. 2019 update added digital health rights (right to data portability from health apps)",First: ~2012. Updated: 2019,Review 2024-2025 expected to add AI/algorithmic transparency rights,CURRENT — 2019 version
MOH-PS,Patient Safety Standards,First issued ~2015 → updates 2020 → 2024 update,"Based on WHO and JCI patient safety frameworks. Covers: patient identification (minimum 2 identifiers), medication safety (high-alert medications protocol), surgical safety checklist, falls prevention, infection control, critical value reporting, handover communications (SBAR). 2020 update added COVID-19 IPC protocols. 2024 update adds IoMT device safety (alarm fatigue management, device connectivity requirements)","First: ~2015. Updated: 2020, 2024",Annual review with MOH patient safety incidents data,CURRENT — 2024 version
MOH-TELE,Telemedicine Regulations,COVID-19 emergency 2020 → comprehensive regulations late 2023,"2020 (emergency COVID telehealth rules): basic platform licensing, identity verification, audio/video minimum standards. Late 2023 (comprehensive update, 38 controls): platform licensing requirements; patient identity verification; cross-border telemedicine restrictions; controlled substance prescribing rules (limited); data protection (PDPL alignment); patient consent requirements; clinical documentation standards; coverage by health insurance; quality assurance audit requirements",Emergency 2020. Comprehensive: late 2023,Annual update; cross-border rules under active development 2025,CURRENT — late 2023 comprehensive version
