-- ── SHEET: Knowledge Foundation  (rows=12 cols=8) ──
-- \copy <staging.shahin_xlsx_Knowledge_Foundation> FROM stdin WITH (FORMAT csv, HEADER true)
KSA Healthcare IoT Cybersecurity Governance — Knowledge Foundation & Theoretical Basis,,,,,,,
Theory / Concept,Description,Key Theorists / Sources,Frameworks Using This Theory,Relevance to IoMT Governance,GH-ICGA Layer,Doctoral Thesis Application,
Governance Theory (Bevir 2012),"Governance as distributed authority — regulatory bodies shape actor behaviour through rules, norms and markets. Central vs distributed governance debate",Bevir M (2012) Governance: A Very Short Introduction; Rhodes RAW (1997) Understanding Governance; Peters BG (2001) The Future of Governing,NCA-ECC-2:2024; SDAIA-PDPL; SHC-HSTP; SAMA-CSF; all mandatory frameworks,IoMT governance requires multi-authority coordination (NCA+MOH+SFDA+SDAIA) — governance theory explains why GH-ICGA Layer 1 must address authority overlap and coordination mechanisms,Layer 1: Governance,GH-ICGA proposes a 5-layer governance architecture drawing on Bevir's distributed governance model — each KSA regulatory authority governs a different IoMT dimension without a single unified framework,
Institutional Theory (DiMaggio & Powell 1983),"Coercive, mimetic and normative isomorphism explain why organisations adopt similar structures. Coercive = government mandate; mimetic = copying peers; normative = professional standards",DiMaggio PJ & Powell WW (1983) 'The Iron Cage Revisited' American Sociological Review 48(2); Meyer JW & Rowan B (1977) 'Institutionalized Organizations' AJS 83(2),CBAHI accreditation (mimetic); NCA-ECC-2 (coercive); ISO 27001 (normative); HITRUST (mimetic for US-aligned vendors),"IoMT governance adoption in Saudi hospitals is driven by coercive (NCA ECC-2 mandate), mimetic (CBAHI accreditation peer comparison) and normative (SCFHS professional standards) mechanisms — GH-ICGA must account for all three adoption pathways",Layer 1: Governance; Layer 4: Compliance,Thesis RQ1 uses institutional theory to explain current IoMT governance adoption patterns; GH-ICGA framework design must leverage all three isomorphism mechanisms for implementation success,
Risk Management Theory (ISO 31000; Kaplan & Mikes 2012),"Risk as uncertainty affecting objectives — risk identification, assessment, treatment and monitoring cycle. Enterprise risk management (ERM) vs cybersecurity risk management debate",ISO 31000:2018; Kaplan RS & Mikes A (2012) 'Managing Risks: A New Framework' HBR; NIST SP 800-30r1 Guide for Conducting Risk Assessments,NIST-CSF-2.0; ISO-27001:2022; SAMA-CSF; NCA-ECC-2:2024; SFDA-MDR (risk classification),"IoMT risk management requires integration of clinical risk (patient safety), cybersecurity risk (NCA ECC-2) and privacy risk (PDPL) — ISO 31000 provides the unified risk methodology that GH-ICGA Layer 2 adopts",Layer 2: Risk Management,GH-ICGA Layer 2 applies ISO 31000 risk management process adapted for IoMT-specific threat landscape — the three-risk integration (clinical + cyber + privacy) is a novel contribution of the preliminary GH-ICGA framework,
Socio-Technical Systems Theory (Trist 1981; Bostrom & Heinen 1977),"Organisations are joint systems of social (people, processes) and technical (technology, tools) elements — both must be optimised together. Technology cannot be governed independently of human and organisational context",Trist E (1981) 'The Evolution of Socio-Technical Systems' Ontario Quality of Working Life Centre; Bostrom RP & Heinen JS (1977) 'MIS Problems and Failures' MIS Quarterly 1(3),SFDA-AI-MDS-G010; CBAHI-HAS-H; MOH-HIS; MOH-PS; IEC-62443; NCA-OTCC,IoMT governance failure is predominantly socio-technical — technology controls fail because of human/organisational factors (Coventry & Branley 2018; Jalali & Kaiser 2018) — GH-ICGA explicitly incorporates socio-technical design principles,Layer 1: Governance; Layer 3: Technical Controls,Thesis methodology section applies socio-technical theory to justify mixed-methods design — interview phase captures social/organisational factors; survey phase captures technical governance factors,
Critical Infrastructure Protection Theory (Lewis 2006),Critical National Infrastructure (CNI) requires special governance — hospitals as dual-use infrastructure (clinical + digital). Availability > Confidentiality in life-critical systems,Lewis TG (2006) Critical Infrastructure Protection in Homeland Security; DHS (2013) NIPP 2013; ENISA (2022) Critical Entities Resilience,NCA-ECC-2:2024 (CNI scope); NCA-OTCC-1:2022; MOH-HIS; SFDA-MDR; NCA-CSCC-1:2019,"Saudi hospital IoMT networks are CNI — life-critical systems (ICU monitoring, ventilators, infusion pumps) — GH-ICGA Layer 3 applies CIP principles: availability-first control prioritisation, zone/conduit network architecture, supply chain security",Layer 2: Risk; Layer 3: Technical,Thesis positions Saudi hospitals as CNI — justifying rigorous IoMT governance requirements. GH-ICGA's preliminary framework explicitly addresses the clinical availability vs cybersecurity confidentiality tension that CIP theory identifies,
Privacy as Contextual Integrity (Nissenbaum 2004),Information flows are appropriate when they match the norms of the context in which data was shared — patient health data shared for treatment cannot be used for marketing,Nissenbaum H (2004) 'Privacy as Contextual Integrity' Washington Law Review 79(1); Westin AF (1967) Privacy and Freedom; Cavoukian A (2009) Privacy by Design,PDPL-2021; PDPL-IR-2023; MOH-PR; SFDA-AI-MDS-G010; SDAIA-AI-ETH,IoMT data flows must match clinical context norms — wearable data collected for monitoring cannot be shared with insurers without explicit consent. GH-ICGA Layer 4 uses contextual integrity as the theoretical basis for IoMT data governance rules,Layer 4: Compliance; Layer 2: Risk,Thesis Chapter 2 uses Nissenbaum's contextual integrity framework to evaluate adequacy of PDPL provisions for IoMT health data — identifies gap between PDPL general provisions and IoMT-specific data flow complexity,
Regulatory Theory: Responsive Regulation (Ayres & Braithwaite 1992),"Regulatory pyramid — persuasion at base, escalating to sanctions at apex. Most actors comply with persuasion; sanctions reserved for persistent non-compliers. Compliance-based vs deterrence-based regulation debate",Ayres I & Braithwaite J (1992) Responsive Regulation Oxford; Baldwin R et al. (2012) Understanding Regulation Oxford; Black J (2001) 'Decentring Regulation' Current Legal Problems 54(1),NCA-REG-2024 (deterrence); CBAHI accreditation (persuasion); SAMA-CSF self-assessment (compliance); SDAIA-PDPL (escalating penalties),"KSA healthcare IoT regulation uses a mixed model — NCA ECC-2 with strong enforcement (deterrence), CBAHI with accreditation incentives (persuasion), PDPL with graduated penalties (responsive regulation). GH-ICGA Layer 4 incorporates all three regulatory approaches",Layer 4: Compliance,Thesis RQ3 applies responsive regulation theory to evaluate the adequacy of current KSA IoMT regulatory enforcement mechanisms — argues that purely coercive approach (NCA model) is insufficient without capacity-building support,
Technology Acceptance Model / TAM (Davis 1989),Technology adoption determined by perceived usefulness and perceived ease of use — extended by UTAUT (Venkatesh 2003) adding social influence and facilitating conditions,"Davis FD (1989) 'Perceived Usefulness, Ease of Use' MIS Quarterly 13(3); Venkatesh V et al. (2003) 'User Acceptance of IT: UTAUT' MIS Quarterly 27(3)",MOH-HIS (clinician adoption); MOH-TELE (patient adoption); SFDA-AI-MDS-G010 (clinician AI acceptance); SCFHS-CPD (digital skill adoption),Healthcare IoT adoption barriers in Saudi hospitals are predicted by TAM — perceived usefulness (clinical benefit) and perceived ease of use (integration with workflows). GH-ICGA Layer 1 governance must address adoption as well as compliance,Layer 1: Governance,Thesis Phase 1 interview guides use TAM constructs to explore clinician and administrator perceptions of IoMT governance frameworks — adoption barriers inform GH-ICGA design recommendations,
Maturity Model Theory (Crosby 1979; CMM/CMMI),Organisational capability matures through predictable stages — Initial → Managed → Defined → Quantitatively Managed → Optimising. Maturity models enable benchmarking and roadmapping,Crosby PB (1979) Quality is Free; CMMI Institute (2018) CMMI v2.0; HIMSS (2023) EMRAM; NIST (2014) CSF Tiers,SAMA-CSF (6 levels); NCA-ECC-2 (5 levels); CBAHI-HAS-H (Met/Not Met); NIST-CSF-2.0 (4 tiers); HITRUST (5 levels per control),IoMT governance maturity varies enormously across KSA healthcare organisations — GH-ICGA Layer 5 Assurance incorporates a 5-level maturity model to enable organisations to self-assess and roadmap improvement,Layer 5: Assurance,"GH-ICGA's proposed maturity model is a thesis contribution — synthesising SAMA-CSF (6 levels), NCA-ECC-2 (5 levels) and NIST CSF Tiers into a healthcare IoT-specific maturity assessment tool",
Supply Chain Risk Theory (Harland et al. 2003; NIST SP 800-161),"Third-party and supply chain risk as systemic vulnerability — IoMT devices introduce supply chain dependencies (manufacturers, importers, integrators) each creating attack surface",Harland C et al. (2003) 'Risk in Supply Networks' J Purchasing & Supply Management; NIST SP 800-161r1 (2022) Supply Chain Risk Management; ISO 27036 (Supply Chain Security),NCA-ECC-2 (third party domain); SFDA-MDR (manufacturer supply chain); SASO-PS (product safety supply chain); NCA-OTCC (vendor access); SAMA-CSF (third party management),IoMT supply chain (device manufacturer → importer → hospital → clinical user) is a critical governance challenge — GH-ICGA Layer 2 Risk Management explicitly addresses IoMT supply chain risk assessment requirements,Layer 2: Risk; Layer 3: Technical,Thesis identifies IoMT supply chain as a research gap in Saudi healthcare cybersecurity governance — Phase 3 Delphi panel will validate supply chain risk controls proposed in GH-ICGA Layer 2,
-- \.

